  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P1', 'Algebraic Expressions', 'Factorise 2x² + 7x + 3.', '(2x + 1)(x + 3).', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P1', 'Algebraic Expressions', 'Factorise x³ − 4x.', 'x(x − 2)(x + 2).', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P1', 'Algebraic Expressions', 'Simplify √8 × √2.', '√16 = 4.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P1', 'Algebraic Expressions', 'Expand (3 + √2)(3 − √2).', '9 − 2 = 7.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P1', 'Algebraic Expressions', 'Rationalise 6/√3.', '6√3/3 = 2√3.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P1', 'Algebraic Expressions', 'Simplify (x² − 1)/(x + 1).', 'x − 1 (for x ≠ −1).', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Quadratics', 'What is the discriminant, and what do its three cases tell you?', 'b² − 4ac. Positive: two distinct real roots. Zero: one repeated root. Negative: no real roots.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Quadratics', 'What is the difference between ''two distinct real roots'' and ''real roots''?', 'Distinct requires b² − 4ac > 0, ''real roots'' usually allows the repeated case, so b² − 4ac ≥ 0. The wording decides < or ≤ in the answer.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Quadratics', 'What is the condition for a line to be tangent to a curve?', 'Substitute the line into the curve and set the discriminant of the resulting quadratic to zero.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Quadratics', 'How do you complete the square for x² + bx + c?', 'Write (x + b/2)² and subtract (b/2)², then add c. For x² + 6x + 2 this gives (x + 3)² − 7, so the minimum is at (−3, −7).', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Quadratics', 'How do you solve one linear and one quadratic simultaneous equation?', 'Always substitute the linear equation into the quadratic, then substitute the roots back into the linear equation and pair the values correctly.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Quadratics', 'How do you solve a quadratic inequality such as x² − 4x − 5 > 0?', 'Factorise to find the critical values, then read the sign from the parabola''s shape. Upward parabola: positive outside the roots, so x < −1 or x > 5.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Quadratics', 'What are the roots of ax² + bx + c = 0 given by the quadratic formula?', 'x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac)) / 2a.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Quadratics', 'What is the vertex of y = (x − p)² + q?', '(p, q); it is a minimum point.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Quadratics', 'Complete the square for 2x² + 12x + 5.', '2(x² + 6x) + 5 = 2(x + 3)² − 18 + 5 = 2(x + 3)² − 13.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Quadratics', 'What is a quadratic in disguise?', 'An equation such as x⁴ − 5x² + 4 = 0 that becomes quadratic with a substitution (u = x²).', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Quadratics', 'What is the product of the roots of ax² + bx + c = 0?', 'c/a.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Quadratics', 'What is the sum of the roots of ax² + bx + c = 0?', '−b/a.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Quadratics', 'Solve x² − 5x + 6 = 0.', '(x − 2)(x − 3) = 0, so x = 2 or x = 3.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Quadratics', 'Solve x² + 2x − 8 = 0 by factorising.', '(x + 4)(x − 2) = 0, so x = −4 or x = 2.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Quadratics', 'Solve 2x² − 3x − 2 = 0.', '(2x + 1)(x − 2) = 0, so x = −1/2 or x = 2.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Quadratics', 'Solve x² − 6x + 4 = 0 by completing the square.', '(x − 3)² = 5, so x = 3 ± √5.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Quadratics', 'Find the turning point of y = x² − 4x + 7.', 'y = (x − 2)² + 3, so the minimum point is (2, 3).', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Quadratics', 'What does the graph of y = ax² + bx + c look like if a < 0?', 'An n-shaped parabola with a maximum point.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Quadratics', 'What is the y-intercept of y = ax² + bx + c?', '(0, c).', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Quadratics', 'Find the discriminant of 2x² + 3x + 5.', '9 − 40 = −31, so there are no real roots.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Quadratics', 'For what values of k does x² + kx + 9 = 0 have equal roots?', 'k² − 36 = 0, so k = ±6.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Quadratics', 'For what values of k does x² + 4x + k = 0 have two distinct real roots?', '16 − 4k > 0, so k < 4.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Quadratics', 'What is the line of symmetry of y = x² + 6x + 2?', 'x = −3.', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Quadratics', 'Solve x⁴ − 13x² + 36 = 0.', 'Let u = x²: (u − 4)(u − 9) = 0, so x = ±2 or x = ±3.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Quadratics', 'Solve x − 5√x + 6 = 0.', 'Let u = √x: (u − 2)(u − 3) = 0, so x = 4 or x = 9.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Quadratics', 'How can a quadratic model a projectile''s height?', 'h = −at² + bt + c; the maximum height is at the vertex, and roots show when it hits the ground.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Quadratics', 'What is a limitation of a quadratic model?', 'It may give unrealistic values outside a certain range, e.g. negative heights.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Quadratics', 'What is the range of f(x) = x² − 2x + 5?', 'f(x) = (x − 1)² + 4, so f(x) ≥ 4.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Quadratics', 'What does a quadratic function''s discriminant tell you about its graph?', 'Positive: crosses the x-axis twice. Zero: touches it once. Negative: doesn''t meet it.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Quadratics', 'Sketch the key features of y = (x − 1)(x − 5).', 'Roots at 1 and 5, y-intercept 5, minimum at x = 3, y = −4.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P3', 'Equations & Inequalities', 'Why can''t you multiply an inequality through by (x − 3)?', 'Its sign is unknown, so the inequality may or may not reverse. Instead collect over a common denominator, or multiply by the square (x − 3)², which is always positive.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P3', 'Equations & Inequalities', 'When do two solution intervals need ''or'' rather than ''and''?', 'Always, for two separate intervals. ''x < −1 and x > 5'' describes no numbers at all, the union needs ''or''.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P3', 'Equations & Inequalities', 'Write the four proportion relationships and their equations.', 'y ∝ x gives y = kx, y ∝ 1/x gives y = k/x, y ∝ xⁿ gives y = kxⁿ, y ∝ 1/x² gives y = k/x².', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P3', 'Equations & Inequalities', 'How do you represent an inequality on a number line?', 'Use an open circle for strict inequalities (< or >) and a filled circle for ≤ or ≥.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P3', 'Equations & Inequalities', 'What does y ∝ 1/x² mean?', 'y = k/x² for some constant k (inverse square proportion).', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P3', 'Equations & Inequalities', 'Solve the simultaneous equations 2x + y = 7 and x − y = 2.', 'Add: 3x = 9, so x = 3 and y = 1.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P3', 'Equations & Inequalities', 'Solve y = x + 1 and x² + y² = 25.', 'x² + (x + 1)² = 25, so 2x² + 2x − 24 = 0, x = 3 or −4; points (3, 4) and (−4, −3).', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P3', 'Equations & Inequalities', 'Solve 3x − 5 > 7.', '3x > 12, so x > 4.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P3', 'Equations & Inequalities', 'Solve −2x < 6.', 'x > −3 (the inequality reverses when dividing by a negative).', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P3', 'Equations & Inequalities', 'Solve x² < 9.', '−3 < x < 3.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P3', 'Equations & Inequalities', 'Solve x² − 3x − 10 ≥ 0.', '(x − 5)(x + 2) ≥ 0, so x ≤ −2 or x ≥ 5.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P3', 'Equations & Inequalities', 'How do you represent y > 2x + 1 on a graph?', 'Draw the line y = 2x + 1 dashed and shade the region above it.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P3', 'Equations & Inequalities', 'Why is a line dashed for a strict inequality?', 'Points on the line aren''t included in the region.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P3', 'Equations & Inequalities', 'How do you find the region satisfying several inequalities?', 'Draw each boundary line and shade the region that satisfies all the inequalities.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P3', 'Equations & Inequalities', 'What is set notation for x < 2 or x > 5?', '{x : x < 2} ∪ {x : x > 5}.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P3', 'Equations & Inequalities', 'What is set notation for 1 < x < 4?', '{x : x > 1} ∩ {x : x < 4}.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P3', 'Equations & Inequalities', 'How many points of intersection do y = x² and y = 2x − 1 have?', 'x² − 2x + 1 = 0 has discriminant 0, so one point (a tangent) at (1, 1).', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P3', 'Equations & Inequalities', 'When does a line not intersect a curve?', 'When the resulting quadratic has a negative discriminant.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P3', 'Equations & Inequalities', 'Solve (x + 1)(x − 2)(x − 4) > 0.', '−1 < x < 2 or x > 4.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P3', 'Equations & Inequalities', 'How do you solve 1/x < 2?', 'Multiply by x² (positive): x < 2x², so x(2x − 1) > 0, giving x < 0 or x > 1/2.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P3', 'Equations & Inequalities', 'Solve x² + 4x + 3 = 0 and interpret graphically.', 'x = −1 or −3: the curve crosses the x-axis at these points.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P3', 'Equations & Inequalities', 'What is the elimination method for simultaneous equations?', 'Add or subtract equations to eliminate one variable.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P3', 'Equations & Inequalities', 'What is the substitution method?', 'Rearrange one equation and substitute into the other.', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P3', 'Equations & Inequalities', 'Solve 4x + 3y = 18, 2x − y = 4.', 'From the second: y = 2x − 4. Then 4x + 6x − 12 = 18, so x = 3, y = 2.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P3', 'Equations & Inequalities', 'How can inequalities describe a region bounded by curves?', 'E.g. y > x² and y < 4 describes the region above the parabola and below the line.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P3', 'Equations & Inequalities', 'Solve 2x + 3 ≤ 5x − 9.', '12 ≤ 3x, so x ≥ 4.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P3', 'Equations & Inequalities', 'Find the values of k for which y = kx − 1 is tangent to y = x² + 3.', 'x² − kx + 4 = 0 has discriminant k² − 16 = 0, so k = ±4.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P3', 'Equations & Inequalities', 'Solve 5 < 2x + 1 ≤ 9.', '4 < 2x ≤ 8, so 2 < x ≤ 4.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P3', 'Equations & Inequalities', 'What does "the curve lies above the line" mean algebraically?', 'f(x) − g(x) > 0.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P3', 'Equations & Inequalities', 'Solve x² > 4x.', 'x(x − 4) > 0, so x < 0 or x > 4.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P4', 'Graphs & Transformations', 'What must a sketch show when ''key features'' are asked for?', 'Intercepts with both axes, asymptotes drawn dashed and labelled, turning points where relevant, and the behaviour as x → ±∞.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P4', 'Graphs & Transformations', 'What does y = f(x) + a do to the graph?', 'Translates it a units in the positive y-direction. It acts on the output, so it behaves as expected.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P4', 'Graphs & Transformations', 'What does y = f(x + a) do to the graph?', 'Translates it a units to the LEFT. Anything inside the bracket behaves in the opposite sense to what you expect.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P4', 'Graphs & Transformations', 'What does y = af(x) do?', 'A stretch parallel to the y-axis, scale factor a. Every y-coordinate is multiplied by a.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P4', 'Graphs & Transformations', 'What does y = f(ax) do?', 'A stretch parallel to the x-axis, scale factor 1/a. Every x-coordinate is divided by a.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P4', 'Graphs & Transformations', 'How is a translation written in vector notation?', 'y = f(x − 2) + 5 is a translation by the column vector (2, 5) — note that f(x − 2) shifts RIGHT by 2.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P4', 'Graphs & Transformations', 'Why must you factorise the inside before describing a combined transformation?', 'Because the stretch also scales the translation. Write f(3x − 6) as f(3(x − 2)) to get the order right.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P4', 'Graphs & Transformations', 'What do y = −f(x) and y = f(−x) do?', '−f(x) reflects in the x-axis, f(−x) reflects in the y-axis.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P4', 'Graphs & Transformations', 'Where does a vertical asymptote occur?', 'Where the denominator is zero and the numerator is not. Watch for a cancelling factor, which gives a hole instead.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P4', 'Graphs & Transformations', 'How do you find a horizontal asymptote?', 'Consider the behaviour as x → ±∞. For y = x/(x − 1), divide top and bottom by x to get 1/(1 − 1/x) → 1, so y = 1.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P4', 'Graphs & Transformations', 'When does an oblique (slant) asymptote occur?', 'When the numerator''s degree is one more than the denominator''s. For y = (x² + 1)/x = x + 1/x, the asymptote is y = x.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P4', 'Graphs & Transformations', 'Can a curve cross an asymptote?', 'It may cross a horizontal or oblique asymptote, since those describe behaviour only far out. It can never cross a vertical one, where the function is undefined.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P4', 'Graphs & Transformations', 'What does a repeated factor tell you about the graph at that root?', '(x − a) crosses. (x − a)² touches and turns back. (x − a)³ crosses but flattens, giving a point of inflection.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P4', 'Graphs & Transformations', 'How many roots and turning points can a degree-n polynomial have?', 'At most n real roots and at most n − 1 turning points.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P4', 'Graphs & Transformations', 'How do you find the number of solutions of f(x) = k graphically?', 'Draw the horizontal line y = k and count the intersections with y = f(x).', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P4', 'Graphs & Transformations', 'What conditions make a graph symmetric about the y-axis or the origin?', 'f(−x) = f(x) gives symmetry about the y-axis (even), f(−x) = −f(x) gives symmetry about the origin (odd).', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P4', 'Graphs & Transformations', 'Describe the transformation from y = x² to y = (x − 3)² + 2.', 'A translation by the vector (3, 2).', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P4', 'Graphs & Transformations', 'Describe the transformation from y = sin x to y = 3 sin x.', 'A stretch parallel to the y-axis, scale factor 3.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P4', 'Graphs & Transformations', 'Describe the transformation from y = cos x to y = cos 2x.', 'A stretch parallel to the x-axis, scale factor 1/2.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P4', 'Graphs & Transformations', 'Describe the transformation y = f(x) to y = −f(x).', 'A reflection in the x-axis.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P4', 'Graphs & Transformations', 'Describe the transformation y = f(x) to y = f(−x).', 'A reflection in the y-axis.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P4', 'Graphs & Transformations', 'What is the shape of y = x³?', 'An S-shaped curve through the origin, increasing, with a point of inflection at the origin.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P4', 'Graphs & Transformations', 'What is the shape of y = 1/x?', 'A hyperbola in the first and third quadrants, with asymptotes x = 0 and y = 0.', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P4', 'Graphs & Transformations', 'What is the shape of y = 1/x²?', 'Two branches, both above the x-axis, with asymptotes x = 0 and y = 0.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P4', 'Graphs & Transformations', 'Where does y = (x − 1)(x + 2)² touch the x-axis?', 'It crosses at x = 1 and touches (a repeated root) at x = −2.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P4', 'Graphs & Transformations', 'What is the y-intercept of y = (x − 1)(x + 2)(x − 3)?', 'Substitute x = 0: (−1)(2)(−3) = 6.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P4', 'Graphs & Transformations', 'How do you find where two graphs intersect?', 'Set the equations equal and solve.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P4', 'Graphs & Transformations', 'What happens to y = ax³ + … as x → ∞ if a > 0?', 'y → ∞.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P4', 'Graphs & Transformations', 'What are the asymptotes of y = 1/(x − 2) + 3?', 'x = 2 and y = 3.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P4', 'Graphs & Transformations', 'What does a stationary point of inflection look like?', 'The gradient is zero, but the curve doesn''t change from increasing to decreasing (or vice versa), e.g. y = x³ at the origin.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P5', 'Straight Lines & Circles', 'Write down the gradient, midpoint and distance formulae.', 'Gradient (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁), midpoint ((x₁+x₂)/2, (y₁+y₂)/2), distance √((x₂−x₁)² + (y₂−y₁)²).', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P5', 'Straight Lines & Circles', 'What is the relationship between perpendicular gradients?', 'Their product is −1, so one is the negative reciprocal of the other. The rule fails for a vertical line, whose gradient is undefined.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P5', 'Straight Lines & Circles', 'How do you show three points are collinear?', 'Show the gradient between successive pairs is equal, and note they share a common point.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P5', 'Straight Lines & Circles', 'Write down the equation of a circle with centre (a, b) and radius r.', '(x − a)² + (y − b)² = r². Note the signs: centre (2, −3) gives (x − 2)² + (y + 3)².', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P5', 'Straight Lines & Circles', 'How do you find the centre and radius from x² + y² + 2gx + 2fy + c = 0?', 'Complete the square in both x and y. The right-hand side is then r², so remember to take the square root.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P5', 'Straight Lines & Circles', 'What is the commonest error when reading a circle equation?', 'Quoting r² as the radius. From (x − 3)² + (y + 2)² = 25 the radius is 5, not 25.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P5', 'Straight Lines & Circles', 'How do you find a circle''s equation from the endpoints of a diameter?', 'The centre is the midpoint, the radius is half the distance between the endpoints.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P5', 'Straight Lines & Circles', 'How do you tell whether a point is inside, on, or outside a circle?', 'Substitute the coordinates and compare with r². Greater than r² means outside, less means inside.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P5', 'Straight Lines & Circles', 'How do you find the equation of a tangent to a circle at a given point?', 'Find the gradient of the radius to that point, take its negative reciprocal for the tangent, then use the point-gradient form.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P5', 'Straight Lines & Circles', 'How do you find how many times a line meets a circle?', 'Substitute the line into the circle and look at the discriminant of the resulting quadratic: two roots, one (tangent), or none.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P5', 'Straight Lines & Circles', 'Find the equation of the line through (1, 2) with gradient 3.', 'y − 2 = 3(x − 1), so y = 3x − 1.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P5', 'Straight Lines & Circles', 'What is the gradient of the line 2x + 3y = 6?', 'Rearrange to y = −2x/3 + 2, so gradient = −2/3.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P5', 'Straight Lines & Circles', 'Find the equation of the perpendicular bisector of A(0, 0) and B(4, 2).', 'Midpoint (2, 1); gradient AB = 1/2, so perpendicular gradient = −2. y − 1 = −2(x − 2), so y = −2x + 5.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P5', 'Straight Lines & Circles', 'What is the distance between (1, 2) and (4, 6)?', '√((4 − 1)² + (6 − 2)²) = √(9 + 16) = 5.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P5', 'Straight Lines & Circles', 'Find the centre and radius of x² + y² − 4x + 6y − 12 = 0.', '(x − 2)² + (y + 3)² = 25: centre (2, −3), radius 5.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P5', 'Straight Lines & Circles', 'What does the angle in a semicircle theorem state?', 'The angle subtended at the circumference by a diameter is a right angle.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P5', 'Straight Lines & Circles', 'What does the perpendicular from the centre to a chord do?', 'It bisects the chord.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P5', 'Straight Lines & Circles', 'What is the relationship between a tangent and a radius?', 'A tangent is perpendicular to the radius at the point of contact.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P5', 'Straight Lines & Circles', 'How do you find the equation of a circle through three points?', 'Find the perpendicular bisectors of two chords; they intersect at the centre. Then find the radius.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P5', 'Straight Lines & Circles', 'What does it mean if a line meets a circle at exactly one point?', 'The line is a tangent to the circle (the discriminant is zero).', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P5', 'Straight Lines & Circles', 'What is the equation of a line with gradient m through (x₁, y₁)?', 'y − y₁ = m(x − x₁).', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P5', 'Straight Lines & Circles', 'What is the general form of a straight line?', 'ax + by + c = 0, where a, b and c are integers.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P5', 'Straight Lines & Circles', 'Find the equation of the line through (2, 5) and (4, 11).', 'Gradient = 6/2 = 3; y − 5 = 3(x − 2), so y = 3x − 1.', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P5', 'Straight Lines & Circles', 'When are two lines parallel?', 'When they have the same gradient.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P5', 'Straight Lines & Circles', 'Find the gradient of a line perpendicular to y = 2x + 3.', '−1/2.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P5', 'Straight Lines & Circles', 'Find where y = 2x + 1 meets y = −x + 7.', '2x + 1 = −x + 7, so x = 2, y = 5.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P5', 'Straight Lines & Circles', 'What does direct proportion look like on a graph?', 'A straight line through the origin: y = kx.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P5', 'Straight Lines & Circles', 'How can a straight-line graph model a real situation?', 'E.g. cost = fixed charge + rate × units; the gradient is the rate and the intercept is the fixed charge.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P5', 'Straight Lines & Circles', 'What is the area of the triangle formed by y = 2x + 4 and the axes?', 'Intercepts at (0, 4) and (−2, 0), so area = ½ × 2 × 4 = 4.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P5', 'Straight Lines & Circles', 'What is the equation of a circle centred at the origin with radius 3?', 'x² + y² = 9.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P6', 'Trigonometric Ratios', 'State the cosine rule for finding a side.', 'a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P6', 'Trigonometric Ratios', 'State the cosine rule for finding an angle.', 'cos A = (b² + c² − a²)/(2bc).', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P6', 'Trigonometric Ratios', 'State the sine rule.', 'a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P6', 'Trigonometric Ratios', 'State the area of a triangle using sine.', 'Area = ½ab sin C.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P6', 'Trigonometric Ratios', 'Find the area of a triangle with sides 5 and 8 and included angle 30°.', '½ × 5 × 8 × sin 30° = 10.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P6', 'Trigonometric Ratios', 'In triangle ABC, a = 7, b = 5, C = 60°. Find c.', 'c² = 49 + 25 − 70 cos 60° = 39, so c ≈ 6.24.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P6', 'Trigonometric Ratios', 'When do you use the sine rule?', 'When you know a side and its opposite angle, plus one other side or angle.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P6', 'Trigonometric Ratios', 'When do you use the cosine rule?', 'When you know two sides and the included angle, or all three sides.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P6', 'Trigonometric Ratios', 'What is the ambiguous case?', 'When using the sine rule to find an angle, there may be two possible values (acute and obtuse).', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P6', 'Trigonometric Ratios', 'What is the graph of y = sin x like?', 'A wave with period 360°, amplitude 1, passing through the origin.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P6', 'Trigonometric Ratios', 'What is the graph of y = cos x like?', 'A wave with period 360°, amplitude 1, starting at (0, 1).', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P6', 'Trigonometric Ratios', 'What is the graph of y = tan x like?', 'Period 180°, passing through the origin, with asymptotes at x = 90°, 270°, …', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P6', 'Trigonometric Ratios', 'What is the range of sin x and cos x?', '−1 ≤ y ≤ 1.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P6', 'Trigonometric Ratios', 'What is the value of sin 90°?', '1.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P6', 'Trigonometric Ratios', 'What is the value of cos 180°?', '−1.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P6', 'Trigonometric Ratios', 'What is the value of tan 45°?', '1.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P6', 'Trigonometric Ratios', 'What is sin 30°?', '1/2.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P6', 'Trigonometric Ratios', 'What is cos 60°?', '1/2.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P6', 'Trigonometric Ratios', 'What is sin 60°?', '√3/2.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P6', 'Trigonometric Ratios', 'What is tan 30°?', '1/√3 (or √3/3).', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P6', 'Trigonometric Ratios', 'What is the unit circle definition of sin θ and cos θ?', 'For a point on the unit circle at angle θ, x = cos θ and y = sin θ.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P6', 'Trigonometric Ratios', 'What is sin(180° − θ)?', 'sin θ.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P6', 'Trigonometric Ratios', 'What is cos(360° − θ)?', 'cos θ.', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P6', 'Trigonometric Ratios', 'What is cos(180° − θ)?', '−cos θ.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P6', 'Trigonometric Ratios', 'In which quadrants is sin θ positive?', 'The first and second (0° to 180°).', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P6', 'Trigonometric Ratios', 'In which quadrants is cos θ positive?', 'The first and fourth.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P6', 'Trigonometric Ratios', 'In which quadrants is tan θ positive?', 'The first and third.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P6', 'Trigonometric Ratios', 'What is a bearing?', 'An angle measured clockwise from north, given as three figures, e.g. 045°.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P6', 'Trigonometric Ratios', 'Describe the transformation y = sin(x + 30°).', 'A translation by 30° in the negative x-direction.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P6', 'Trigonometric Ratios', 'Describe the transformation y = 2 cos x.', 'A vertical stretch, scale factor 2 (amplitude 2).', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P7', 'Trig Identities & Equations', 'State the identity linking sin θ and cos θ squared.', 'sin²θ + cos²θ = 1.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P7', 'Trig Identities & Equations', 'State the identity for tan θ.', 'tan θ = sin θ/cos θ.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P7', 'Trig Identities & Equations', 'Solve sin θ = 0.5 for 0° ≤ θ < 360°.', 'θ = 30° or 150°.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P7', 'Trig Identities & Equations', 'Solve cos θ = −0.5 for 0° ≤ θ < 360°.', 'θ = 120° or 240°.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P7', 'Trig Identities & Equations', 'Solve tan θ = 1 for 0° ≤ θ < 360°.', 'θ = 45° or 225°.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P7', 'Trig Identities & Equations', 'Solve sin 2θ = √3/2 for 0° ≤ θ < 360°.', '2θ = 60°, 120°, 420°, 480°, so θ = 30°, 60°, 210°, 240°.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P7', 'Trig Identities & Equations', 'Solve cos(θ − 20°) = 0.5 for 0° ≤ θ < 360°.', 'θ − 20° = 60° or 300°, so θ = 80° or 320°.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P7', 'Trig Identities & Equations', 'Solve 2 sin²θ − sin θ − 1 = 0 for 0° ≤ θ < 360°.', '(2 sin θ + 1)(sin θ − 1) = 0: sin θ = 1 gives 90°; sin θ = −1/2 gives 210°, 330°.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P7', 'Trig Identities & Equations', 'Solve 3 sin θ = cos θ for 0° ≤ θ < 360°.', 'tan θ = 1/3, so θ ≈ 18.4° or 198.4°.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P7', 'Trig Identities & Equations', 'Solve 2cos²θ + 3 sin θ = 3.', '2(1 − sin²θ) + 3 sin θ = 3, so 2 sin²θ − 3 sin θ + 1 = 0, sin θ = 1/2 or 1: θ = 30°, 90°, 150°.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P7', 'Trig Identities & Equations', 'Prove that (1 − cos²θ)/sin θ = sin θ.', '1 − cos²θ = sin²θ, and sin²θ/sin θ = sin θ.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P7', 'Trig Identities & Equations', 'Prove that tan θ + 1/tan θ = 1/(sin θ cos θ).', 'sin θ/cos θ + cos θ/sin θ = (sin²θ + cos²θ)/(sin θ cos θ) = 1/(sin θ cos θ).', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P7', 'Trig Identities & Equations', 'Why must you not divide by sin θ when solving sin θ cos θ = sin θ?', 'You''d lose the solutions where sin θ = 0; instead factorise: sin θ(cos θ − 1) = 0.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P7', 'Trig Identities & Equations', 'What is the principal value of arcsin?', 'The value between −90° and 90° returned by a calculator.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P7', 'Trig Identities & Equations', 'How do you find all solutions from a principal value for sin?', 'θ and 180° − θ, then add or subtract multiples of 360°.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P7', 'Trig Identities & Equations', 'How do you find all solutions from a principal value for cos?', 'θ and 360° − θ, then add or subtract multiples of 360°.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P7', 'Trig Identities & Equations', 'How do you find all solutions from a principal value for tan?', 'Add or subtract multiples of 180°.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P7', 'Trig Identities & Equations', 'Solve tan 3θ = −1 for 0° ≤ θ < 180°.', '3θ = 135°, 315°, 495°, so θ = 45°, 105°, 165°.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P7', 'Trig Identities & Equations', 'Express sin²θ in terms of cos θ.', '1 − cos²θ.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P7', 'Trig Identities & Equations', 'If sin θ = 3/5 and θ is acute, find cos θ.', 'cos θ = √(1 − 9/25) = 4/5.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P7', 'Trig Identities & Equations', 'If cos θ = −5/13 and θ is obtuse, find tan θ.', 'sin θ = 12/13, so tan θ = −12/5.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P7', 'Trig Identities & Equations', 'How can you check trig equation solutions?', 'Substitute back, and check against a sketch of the graph.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P7', 'Trig Identities & Equations', 'What is the first step when solving a trig equation with 2θ over a given range?', 'Double the range for 2θ, find all solutions, then halve them.', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P7', 'Trig Identities & Equations', 'Solve sin θ = −0.3 for 0° ≤ θ < 360°.', 'θ ≈ 197.5° or 342.5°.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P7', 'Trig Identities & Equations', 'Solve cos θ = 0.8 for −180° ≤ θ < 180°.', 'θ ≈ ±36.9°.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P7', 'Trig Identities & Equations', 'Solve 4 sin θ cos θ = sin θ for 0° ≤ θ < 360°.', 'sin θ(4 cos θ − 1) = 0: sin θ = 0 gives 0°, 180°; cos θ = 1/4 gives 75.5°, 284.5°.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P7', 'Trig Identities & Equations', 'How many solutions does sin θ = 1.2 have?', 'None, since −1 ≤ sin θ ≤ 1.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P7', 'Trig Identities & Equations', 'How is a quadratic in tan θ solved?', 'Factorise or use the formula to find tan θ, then solve each equation.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P7', 'Trig Identities & Equations', 'Write tan²θ in terms of sin θ and cos θ.', 'sin²θ/cos²θ.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P7', 'Trig Identities & Equations', 'Prove that sin⁴θ − cos⁴θ = sin²θ − cos²θ.', 'Factorise as (sin²θ − cos²θ)(sin²θ + cos²θ) = sin²θ − cos²θ.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P8', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'What is the relationship between aˣ and log_a x?', 'They are inverse functions: x = a^y is equivalent to y = log_a x. Their graphs are reflections in y = x.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P8', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'State the three laws of logarithms.', 'log(xy) = log x + log y, log(x/y) = log x − log y, log(xᵏ) = k log x.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P8', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'Is there a law for log(x + y)?', 'No. The addition law applies to a PRODUCT inside the logarithm, not a sum. log(x + y) ≠ log x + log y.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P8', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'What are log_a a and log_a 1?', 'log_a a = 1 and log_a 1 = 0.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P8', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'How do you solve an equation such as 3ˣ = 20?', 'Take logarithms of both sides, then use the power law to bring x down: x = log 20 ÷ log 3 ≈ 2.727.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P8', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'What must you check after solving a logarithmic equation?', 'That each solution keeps every logarithm''s argument positive. Reject any root that makes an argument zero or negative.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P8', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'What is special about e?', 'It is the base for which the gradient of y = eˣ equals eˣ itself. More generally the gradient of e^(kx) is k e^(kx).', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P8', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'Why does the exponential model suit so many situations?', 'Because the derivative of e^(kt) is proportional to the function itself — exactly the condition ''rate of change proportional to current size''.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P8', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'What is ln x, and what are ln e and e^(ln x)?', 'ln x is log to base e, the inverse of eˣ. ln e = 1, and e^(ln x) = x for x > 0.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P8', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'How do you reduce y = axⁿ to linear form?', 'Take logs: log y = log a + n log x. Plot log y against log x — gradient n, intercept log a.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P8', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'How do you reduce y = abˣ to linear form?', 'Take logs: log y = log a + x log b. Plot log y against PLAIN x — gradient log b, intercept log a.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P8', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'How do you tell a power law from an exponential law using graphs?', 'A power law gives a straight line on log–log axes, an exponential law gives one with log y against plain x. The difference is whether the horizontal axis carries a logarithm.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P8', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'Write down the exponential growth and decay models.', 'N = N₀e^(kt) for growth and N = N₀e^(−kt) for decay, with k > 0 and N₀ the value at t = 0.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P8', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'How do you find the decay constant from a half-life T?', 'Set e^(−kT) = ½, so kT = ln 2 and k = (ln 2)/T.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P8', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'Give two criticisms of an exponential population growth model.', 'It predicts unbounded growth, ignoring finite resources, and it assumes k stays constant, though it may drift with conditions. A logistic model adds a carrying capacity.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P8', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'Solve 2ˣ = 8.', 'x = 3.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P8', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'Solve eˣ = 5.', 'x = ln 5.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P8', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'Simplify log 8 + log 5 − log 4.', 'log(8 × 5/4) = log 10.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P8', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'Write 2 log x − log y as a single log.', 'log(x²/y).', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P8', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'What is the gradient of y = eᵏˣ?', 'keᵏˣ.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P8', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'What is log_a aˣ equal to?', 'x.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P8', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'What is e^(ln 7)?', '7.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P8', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'What is ln(e³)?', '3.', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P8', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'Solve ln x = 2.', 'x = e².', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P8', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'What is the change of base formula?', 'log_a b = log_c b / log_c a.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P8', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'What is the asymptote of y = eˣ?', 'y = 0 (the x-axis).', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P8', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'What is the asymptote of y = ln x?', 'x = 0 (the y-axis).', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P8', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'If y = axⁿ, what does a log–log graph give?', 'log y = log a + n log x: a straight line with gradient n and intercept log a.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P8', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'If y = abˣ, what does plotting log y against x give?', 'log y = log a + x log b: a straight line with gradient log b and intercept log a.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P8', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'Solve 3^(x+1) = 81.', '3^(x+1) = 3⁴, so x = 3.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P9', 'Differentiation (Year 1)', 'Write down the definition of the derivative as a limit.', 'f′(x) = limit as h → 0 of (f(x + h) − f(x))/h — the gradient of a chord becoming the gradient of the tangent.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P9', 'Differentiation (Year 1)', 'Why must you cancel the h before letting h → 0?', 'Substituting h = 0 first gives 0/0, which is undefined. Cancelling is the whole point of the method.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P9', 'Differentiation (Year 1)', 'Does d²y/dx² = 0 prove a point of inflection?', 'No. For y = x⁴ at the origin the second derivative is zero yet the point is a minimum. A genuine inflection needs the second derivative to CHANGE SIGN.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P9', 'Differentiation (Year 1)', 'What are the two marks most often lost in an optimisation question?', 'Not justifying that the stationary point is the required maximum or minimum, and not answering the quantity actually asked for.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P9', 'Differentiation (Year 1)', 'Differentiate y = 3x⁴ − 2x + 7.', 'dy/dx = 12x³ − 2.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P9', 'Differentiation (Year 1)', 'Differentiate y = (2x + 1)⁵.', 'dy/dx = 5(2x + 1)⁴ × 2 = 10(2x + 1)⁴.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P9', 'Differentiation (Year 1)', 'What does it mean if dy/dx > 0?', 'The function is increasing.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P9', 'Differentiation (Year 1)', 'What does a positive second derivative indicate?', 'The curve is concave upwards (convex), and a stationary point there is a minimum.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P9', 'Differentiation (Year 1)', 'What is a point of inflection?', 'A point where the curve changes concavity (d²y/dx² changes sign).', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P9', 'Differentiation (Year 1)', 'What is the gradient of a curve at a point?', 'The gradient of the tangent at that point, found by differentiating.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P9', 'Differentiation (Year 1)', 'Differentiate y = 5x³.', 'dy/dx = 15x².', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P9', 'Differentiation (Year 1)', 'Differentiate y = x⁻².', 'dy/dx = −2x⁻³.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P9', 'Differentiation (Year 1)', 'Differentiate y = 4/x.', 'y = 4x⁻¹, so dy/dx = −4x⁻².', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P9', 'Differentiation (Year 1)', 'Differentiate y = (x + 2)(x − 3).', 'Expand: y = x² − x − 6, so dy/dx = 2x − 1.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P9', 'Differentiation (Year 1)', 'Differentiate y = (x² + 3x)/x.', 'y = x + 3, so dy/dx = 1.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P9', 'Differentiation (Year 1)', 'Find the gradient of y = x² − 5x at x = 3.', 'dy/dx = 2x − 5 = 1.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P9', 'Differentiation (Year 1)', 'Find the equation of the tangent to y = x² at (2, 4).', 'Gradient 4: y − 4 = 4(x − 2), so y = 4x − 4.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P9', 'Differentiation (Year 1)', 'Find the equation of the normal to y = x² at (2, 4).', 'Gradient −1/4: y − 4 = −1/4(x − 2).', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P9', 'Differentiation (Year 1)', 'Find the stationary points of y = x³ − 3x.', 'dy/dx = 3x² − 3 = 0, so x = ±1; points (1, −2) and (−1, 2).', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P9', 'Differentiation (Year 1)', 'Determine the nature of the stationary points of y = x³ − 3x.', 'd²y/dx² = 6x: at x = 1 it is 6 > 0 (minimum); at x = −1 it is −6 < 0 (maximum).', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P9', 'Differentiation (Year 1)', 'What is an increasing function?', 'A function where dy/dx > 0 over an interval.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P9', 'Differentiation (Year 1)', 'Find where y = x³ − 12x is decreasing.', 'dy/dx = 3x² − 12 < 0, so −2 < x < 2.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P9', 'Differentiation (Year 1)', 'What does d²y/dx² represent?', 'The rate of change of the gradient.', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P9', 'Differentiation (Year 1)', 'Differentiate from first principles: f(x) = x².', 'f''(x) = lim (h→0) [(x + h)² − x²]/h = lim (2x + h) = 2x.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P9', 'Differentiation (Year 1)', 'What is the notation f''(x)?', 'The derivative of f(x) with respect to x.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P9', 'Differentiation (Year 1)', 'Find the maximum area of a rectangle with perimeter 20 cm.', 'A = x(10 − x); dA/dx = 10 − 2x = 0, x = 5, so maximum area = 25 cm².', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P9', 'Differentiation (Year 1)', 'What is the rate of change?', 'How fast one quantity changes compared with another, given by the derivative.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P9', 'Differentiation (Year 1)', 'Differentiate V = (4/3)πr³ with respect to r.', 'dV/dr = 4πr².', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P9', 'Differentiation (Year 1)', 'Differentiate y = 6√x.', 'y = 6x^(1/2), so dy/dx = 3x^(−1/2) = 3/√x.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P9', 'Differentiation (Year 1)', 'What is a stationary point?', 'A point where the gradient is zero: a maximum, minimum or point of inflection.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P10', 'Integration (Year 1)', 'What is the integral of xⁿ, and when does it fail?', 'x^(n+1)/(n + 1) + c, failing for n = −1 where the denominator would be zero. Then the integral of 1/x is ln|x| + c.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P10', 'Integration (Year 1)', 'Why does an indefinite integral need + c but a definite one does not?', 'Differentiating a constant gives zero, so it cannot be recovered. In a definite integral the constant appears in both F(b) and F(a) and cancels.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P10', 'Integration (Year 1)', 'State the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus.', 'The definite integral from a to b of f equals F(b) − F(a), where F′ = f. It links area with the antiderivative and shows the two processes are inverse.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P10', 'Integration (Year 1)', 'What happens to a region below the x-axis in a definite integral?', 'It contributes a negative amount. If the curve crosses the axis within the limits, split there and add the magnitudes, or the parts will cancel.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P10', 'Integration (Year 1)', 'How do you find the area between two curves?', 'Integrate (upper − lower) between the intersections, so the integrand is non-negative and the area comes out positive.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P10', 'Integration (Year 1)', 'Integrate 6x² + 4x − 3.', '2x³ + 2x² − 3x + c.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P10', 'Integration (Year 1)', 'Integrate (2x + 1)³.', '(2x + 1)⁴/8 + c.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P10', 'Integration (Year 1)', 'Evaluate ∫ from 0 to 2 of x² dx.', '[x³/3] from 0 to 2 = 8/3.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P10', 'Integration (Year 1)', 'What is the area enclosed between y = x² and y = x?', '∫ from 0 to 1 of (x − x²) dx = 1/2 − 1/3 = 1/6.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P10', 'Integration (Year 1)', 'What is integration?', 'The reverse of differentiation.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P10', 'Integration (Year 1)', 'Integrate x⁵.', 'x⁶/6 + c.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P10', 'Integration (Year 1)', 'Integrate 3x⁻².', '−3x⁻¹ + c.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P10', 'Integration (Year 1)', 'Integrate √x.', '(2/3)x^(3/2) + c.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P10', 'Integration (Year 1)', 'Integrate (x + 1)² by expanding.', '∫(x² + 2x + 1) dx = x³/3 + x² + x + c.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P10', 'Integration (Year 1)', 'Integrate (x² + 1)/x².', '∫(1 + x⁻²) dx = x − 1/x + c.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P10', 'Integration (Year 1)', 'Find y given dy/dx = 4x + 3 and y = 5 when x = 1.', 'y = 2x² + 3x + c; 5 = 2 + 3 + c, so c = 0 and y = 2x² + 3x.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P10', 'Integration (Year 1)', 'Evaluate ∫ from 1 to 3 of 2x dx.', '[x²] from 1 to 3 = 9 − 1 = 8.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P10', 'Integration (Year 1)', 'Evaluate ∫ from 0 to 1 of (3x² + 1) dx.', '[x³ + x] from 0 to 1 = 2.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P10', 'Integration (Year 1)', 'Find the area between y = x² and the x-axis from x = 0 to x = 3.', '∫ from 0 to 3 of x² dx = 9.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P10', 'Integration (Year 1)', 'Find the area between y = x(x − 2) and the x-axis.', '∫ from 0 to 2 of (x² − 2x) dx = 8/3 − 4 = −4/3, so the area is 4/3.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P10', 'Integration (Year 1)', 'Why must you split an area calculation when the curve crosses the x-axis?', 'Areas below the axis give negative integrals, which would cancel positive areas.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P10', 'Integration (Year 1)', 'What is ∫ from a to a of f(x) dx?', '0.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P10', 'Integration (Year 1)', 'What is ∫ from b to a of f(x) dx in terms of ∫ from a to b?', '−∫ from a to b of f(x) dx.', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P10', 'Integration (Year 1)', 'What is the constant of integration?', 'An arbitrary constant c added to an indefinite integral, because constants differentiate to zero.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P10', 'Integration (Year 1)', 'What is a definite integral?', 'An integral with limits, giving a numerical value.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P10', 'Integration (Year 1)', 'What is an indefinite integral?', 'An integral without limits, giving a function plus a constant.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P10', 'Integration (Year 1)', 'Integrate 1/x³.', '−1/(2x²) + c.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P10', 'Integration (Year 1)', 'Integrate 5.', '5x + c.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P10', 'Integration (Year 1)', 'Find the equation of the curve with gradient 6x² that passes through (1, 3).', 'y = 2x³ + c; 3 = 2 + c, so y = 2x³ + 1.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P10', 'Integration (Year 1)', 'How do you find the area between a line and a curve?', 'Find the intersection points, then integrate (top − bottom) between them.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P11', 'Vectors (Year 1)', 'What is a vector?', 'A quantity with both magnitude and direction.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P11', 'Vectors (Year 1)', 'How is a 2D vector written in i, j notation?', 'ai + bj, where i and j are unit vectors in the x and y directions.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P11', 'Vectors (Year 1)', 'Find the magnitude of 5i + 12j.', '√(25 + 144) = 13.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P11', 'Vectors (Year 1)', 'Find the direction of 5i + 12j from the positive x-axis.', 'tan θ = 12/5, so θ ≈ 67.4°.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P11', 'Vectors (Year 1)', 'If a = 3i + 2j and b = i − 4j, find a + b.', '4i − 2j.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P11', 'Vectors (Year 1)', 'If a = 3i + 2j, find 3a.', '9i + 6j.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P11', 'Vectors (Year 1)', 'What is a unit vector?', 'A vector with magnitude 1.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P11', 'Vectors (Year 1)', 'Find the unit vector in the direction of 6i − 8j.', '(6i − 8j)/10 = 0.6i − 0.8j.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P11', 'Vectors (Year 1)', 'What is the position vector of point A(2, 5)?', '2i + 5j.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P11', 'Vectors (Year 1)', 'If A is (1, 2) and B is (4, 6), find AB.', 'b − a = 3i + 4j.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P11', 'Vectors (Year 1)', 'How do you show two vectors are parallel?', 'One is a scalar multiple of the other.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P11', 'Vectors (Year 1)', 'Are 2i + 3j and 6i + 9j parallel?', 'Yes: 6i + 9j = 3(2i + 3j).', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P11', 'Vectors (Year 1)', 'What is the resultant of two vectors?', 'Their sum, which has the same effect as both combined.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P11', 'Vectors (Year 1)', 'How do you write a vector from magnitude and direction?', 'r cos θ i + r sin θ j, where θ is measured from the positive x-axis.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P11', 'Vectors (Year 1)', 'Write a vector of magnitude 10 at 30° above the x-axis.', '10 cos 30° i + 10 sin 30° j = 5√3 i + 5j.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P11', 'Vectors (Year 1)', 'What is the zero vector?', 'The vector 0i + 0j, with magnitude zero.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P11', 'Vectors (Year 1)', 'If OA = a and OB = b, what is the midpoint M of AB?', 'OM = ½(a + b).', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P11', 'Vectors (Year 1)', 'What is −a?', 'A vector with the same magnitude as a but opposite direction.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P11', 'Vectors (Year 1)', 'How can vectors be used to show points are collinear?', 'Show that AB and BC are parallel and share point B.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P11', 'Vectors (Year 1)', 'What is a column vector?', 'A vector written as a column of components, e.g. (3, 4) written vertically.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P11', 'Vectors (Year 1)', 'How do you find the distance between points A and B using vectors?', 'Find |AB| = |b − a|.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P11', 'Vectors (Year 1)', 'A boat travels 3 km east then 4 km north. Find its displacement.', '3i + 4j km, magnitude 5 km.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P11', 'Vectors (Year 1)', 'How can vectors model velocity?', 'The direction gives the direction of motion; the magnitude gives the speed.', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P11', 'Vectors (Year 1)', 'A particle has velocity (3i − 4j) m/s. What is its speed?', '√(9 + 16) = 5 m/s.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P11', 'Vectors (Year 1)', 'What is a displacement vector?', 'The vector from the start point to the end point.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P11', 'Vectors (Year 1)', 'What does it mean if a·i + b·j = 0?', 'Both a = 0 and b = 0, since i and j are independent.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P11', 'Vectors (Year 1)', 'If pa + qb = 3a − 2b with a and b non-parallel, find p and q.', 'p = 3 and q = −2 (equate coefficients).', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P11', 'Vectors (Year 1)', 'How do you find the angle a vector makes with the y-axis?', 'Use tan φ = (x-component)/(y-component), where φ is measured from the y-axis.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P11', 'Vectors (Year 1)', 'What is the magnitude of −3i + 4j?', '5.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P11', 'Vectors (Year 1)', 'In which direction does −3i + 4j point?', 'Up and to the left, at about 126.9° from the positive x-axis.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P12', 'Algebraic Methods', 'What is proof by deduction?', 'Proceeding from given assumptions through a series of logical steps to a conclusion.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P12', 'Algebraic Methods', 'What is proof by exhaustion?', 'Establishing a result by checking every case of a finite set of cases, having first shown the cases cover all possibilities.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P12', 'Algebraic Methods', 'What is proof by contradiction?', 'Assume the statement is false, reason correctly from that assumption, and derive an impossibility. The assumption must therefore be wrong.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P12', 'Algebraic Methods', 'How do you disprove a general statement?', 'Give a single counter-example — one case for which the statement fails. One example never proves a general statement, but one counter-example always disproves it.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P12', 'Algebraic Methods', 'What does P ⇒ Q mean, and how does it differ from P ⇔ Q?', 'P ⇒ Q means P implies Q. P ⇔ Q means each implies the other. Writing ⇔ requires both directions to hold.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P12', 'Algebraic Methods', 'What is the contrapositive of ''if P then Q'', and why is it useful?', '''If not Q then not P''. It is logically equivalent to the original, so proving it proves the original — often easier than a direct argument.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P12', 'Algebraic Methods', 'What is the converse of ''if P then Q''? Is it always true?', '''If Q then P''. It is a different statement and need not be true, confusing the two is a common error.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P12', 'Algebraic Methods', 'Give the counter-example that disproves ''every prime is odd''.', '2 is prime and even.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P12', 'Algebraic Methods', 'Give the counter-example that disproves ''n² − n + 41 is prime for every positive integer n''.', 'n = 41, where the expression equals 41², divisible by 41. It does give primes for n = 1 to 40, which is why testing values is not proof.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P12', 'Algebraic Methods', 'Outline the proof that √2 is irrational.', 'Assume √2 = p/q in lowest terms. Then p² = 2q², so p is even, writing p = 2k gives q² = 2k², so q is even. Both even contradicts lowest terms.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P12', 'Algebraic Methods', 'Why is ''in its lowest terms'' essential to the √2 proof?', 'It is the assumption the final contradiction violates. Without it, showing p and q are both even contradicts nothing and the proof fails.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P12', 'Algebraic Methods', 'Why can the √2 argument not be copied to prove √4 irrational?', 'It would need ''p² divisible by 4 ⇒ p divisible by 4'', which is false (take p = 2). The proof breaks exactly where it must, since √4 = 2 is rational.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P12', 'Algebraic Methods', 'Outline Euclid''s proof that there are infinitely many primes.', 'Assume a finite list p₁…pₙ and form N = p₁p₂⋯pₙ + 1. No prime in the list divides N, so N is prime or has a prime factor outside the list — contradicting completeness.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P12', 'Algebraic Methods', 'In Euclid''s proof, must N = p₁⋯pₙ + 1 be prime?', 'No. It need only have no factor in the list. With 2,3,5,7,11,13 you get 30031 = 59 × 509 — composite, but both factors lie outside the list.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P12', 'Algebraic Methods', 'What is the negation of ''all swans are white''?', '''There exists a swan that is not white.'' It is not ''no swans are white'', which is a much stronger claim.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P12', 'Algebraic Methods', 'What is a conjecture?', 'A statement believed to be true but not yet proved.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P12', 'Algebraic Methods', 'What does "if and only if" require you to prove?', 'Both directions: P ⇒ Q and Q ⇒ P.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P12', 'Algebraic Methods', 'Prove that the sum of two odd numbers is even.', 'Let the odd numbers be 2m + 1 and 2n + 1. Their sum is 2m + 2n + 2 = 2(m + n + 1), which is even.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P12', 'Algebraic Methods', 'What is the commonest error with repeated factors in partial fractions?', 'Omitting the A/(x+a) term and using only B/(x+a)². The system then has no solution.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P12', 'Algebraic Methods', 'Express (3x + 1)/((x − 1)(x + 2)) in partial fractions.', 'A/(x − 1) + B/(x + 2). 3x + 1 = A(x + 2) + B(x − 1). x = 1 gives A = 4/3; x = −2 gives B = 5/3.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P12', 'Algebraic Methods', 'Divide x³ + 2x² − 5x − 6 by (x + 1).', 'x² + x − 6, so the cubic factorises as (x + 1)(x + 3)(x − 2).', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P12', 'Algebraic Methods', 'Simplify (x² − 4)/(x² + x − 6).', '(x − 2)(x + 2)/((x + 3)(x − 2)) = (x + 2)/(x + 3).', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P12', 'Algebraic Methods', 'Simplify 1/(x + 1) + 2/(x − 1).', '(x − 1 + 2x + 2)/((x + 1)(x − 1)) = (3x + 1)/(x² − 1).', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P12', 'Algebraic Methods', 'What is an improper algebraic fraction?', 'A fraction where the degree of the numerator is greater than or equal to that of the denominator.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P12', 'Algebraic Methods', 'How do you deal with an improper fraction before partial fractions?', 'Divide first, to write it as a polynomial plus a proper fraction.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P12', 'Algebraic Methods', 'Express 5/((x + 1)(x − 4)) in partial fractions.', 'A/(x + 1) + B/(x − 4): 5 = A(x − 4) + B(x + 1). x = 4 gives B = 1; x = −1 gives A = −1.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P12', 'Algebraic Methods', 'What form do partial fractions take for 1/((x + 1)(x − 2)²)?', 'A/(x + 1) + B/(x − 2) + C/(x − 2)².', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P12', 'Algebraic Methods', 'Prove by contradiction that there is no largest integer.', 'Assume N is the largest integer. Then N + 1 is an integer larger than N, a contradiction.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P12', 'Algebraic Methods', 'Prove by contradiction that if n² is even, then n is even.', 'Assume n is odd: n = 2k + 1, so n² = 4k² + 4k + 1, which is odd, a contradiction.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P12', 'Algebraic Methods', 'Why is proof by contradiction useful?', 'It can prove statements where a direct proof is difficult, by showing the opposite leads to something impossible.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P13', 'Functions & Graphs', 'State the factor theorem.', 'If f(a) = 0 then (x − a) is a factor of f(x), and conversely.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P13', 'Functions & Graphs', 'State the remainder theorem.', 'The remainder when f(x) is divided by (x − a) is f(a). A remainder of zero means (x − a) is a factor.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P13', 'Functions & Graphs', 'To test whether (x + 3) is a factor, which value do you substitute?', 'x = −3, since (x + 3) = (x − (−3)). Substituting +3 is the standard sign error.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P13', 'Functions & Graphs', 'When hunting for a first factor of a cubic, which values should you test?', 'The divisors of the constant term. For a constant of 6, test ±1, ±2, ±3, ±6.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P13', 'Functions & Graphs', 'What is the domain and what is the range of a function?', 'The domain is the set of permitted inputs, the range is the set of outputs the function actually takes.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P13', 'Functions & Graphs', 'What are the two usual causes of a restricted domain?', 'You cannot divide by zero, and you cannot take the square root of a negative number.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P13', 'Functions & Graphs', 'How do you find the range of a quadratic?', 'Complete the square. For f(x) = x² − 4x + 7 = (x − 2)² + 3, the range is f(x) ≥ 3.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P13', 'Functions & Graphs', 'What does fg(x) mean?', 'f(g(x)) — apply g first, then f. The inner function is the one written next to the x.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P13', 'Functions & Graphs', 'Is composition of functions commutative?', 'No. With f(x) = 2x + 3 and g(x) = x², fg(x) = 2x² + 3 but gf(x) = (2x + 3)².', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P13', 'Functions & Graphs', 'When does an inverse function exist?', 'Only when the function is one-to-one. A many-to-one function such as x² needs its domain restricted first.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P13', 'Functions & Graphs', 'How do you find an inverse function?', 'Write y = f(x), rearrange to make x the subject, then swap the letters. Use the given domain to choose the sign of any square root.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P13', 'Functions & Graphs', 'How are the graphs of f and f⁻¹ related?', 'Each is the reflection of the other in the line y = x. Domain and range swap, and for an INCREASING function any intersection lies on y = x.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P13', 'Functions & Graphs', 'Define |x| as a piecewise function.', '|x| = x for x ≥ 0, and |x| = −x for x < 0. The graph is a V with vertex at the origin.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P13', 'Functions & Graphs', 'How do you obtain y = |f(x)| from y = f(x)?', 'Reflect the parts below the x-axis up into it, leaving the rest unchanged. It acts on the OUTPUT.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P13', 'Functions & Graphs', 'How do you obtain y = f(|x|) from y = f(x)?', 'Keep the x ≥ 0 part and reflect it in the y-axis, discarding the original x < 0 part. It acts on the INPUT.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P13', 'Functions & Graphs', 'When is squaring a safe way to solve a modulus equation?', 'When both sides are non-negative — automatic for |A| = |B|. If one side could be negative, split into cases instead.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P13', 'Functions & Graphs', 'Divide x³ − 2x² − 5x + 6 by (x − 1).', 'x³ − 2x² − 5x + 6 = (x − 1)(x² − x − 6) = (x − 1)(x − 3)(x + 2).', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P13', 'Functions & Graphs', 'What is a many-to-one function?', 'A function where more than one input gives the same output, e.g. f(x) = x².', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P13', 'Functions & Graphs', 'What is a one-to-one function?', 'Each output comes from exactly one input; only one-to-one functions have inverses.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P13', 'Functions & Graphs', 'What is a mapping that is not a function?', 'One where an input maps to more than one output, e.g. y = ±√x.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P13', 'Functions & Graphs', 'Find the inverse of f(x) = (2x + 1)/3.', 'Let y = (2x + 1)/3, so x = (3y − 1)/2. f⁻¹(x) = (3x − 1)/2.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P13', 'Functions & Graphs', 'What is the domain of f⁻¹?', 'The range of f.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P13', 'Functions & Graphs', 'If f(x) = x² and g(x) = x + 3, find fg(x) and gf(x).', 'fg(x) = (x + 3)²; gf(x) = x² + 3.', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P13', 'Functions & Graphs', 'What is the range of f(x) = x² + 4?', 'f(x) ≥ 4.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P13', 'Functions & Graphs', 'What is the range of f(x) = eˣ?', 'f(x) > 0.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P13', 'Functions & Graphs', 'What is the domain of f(x) = ln x?', 'x > 0.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P13', 'Functions & Graphs', 'Solve |2x − 1| = 5.', '2x − 1 = 5 or 2x − 1 = −5, so x = 3 or x = −2.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P13', 'Functions & Graphs', 'Solve |x − 2| < 3.', '−3 < x − 2 < 3, so −1 < x < 5.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P13', 'Functions & Graphs', 'What is an odd function?', 'f(−x) = −f(x); the graph has rotational symmetry of order 2 about the origin, e.g. sin x.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P13', 'Functions & Graphs', 'What is an even function?', 'f(−x) = f(x); the graph is symmetric about the y-axis, e.g. cos x.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P14', 'Sequences & Series', 'Write down the nth term and sum formulae for an arithmetic sequence.', 'uₙ = a + (n − 1)d. Sₙ = (n/2)(2a + (n − 1)d), or (n/2)(a + l) using the last term.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P14', 'Sequences & Series', 'Write down the nth term and sum formulae for a geometric sequence.', 'uₙ = arⁿ⁻¹. Sₙ = a(1 − rⁿ)/(1 − r) for r ≠ 1.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P14', 'Sequences & Series', 'When does a geometric series converge, and what is its sum to infinity?', 'It converges if and only if |r| < 1, and then S∞ = a/(1 − r). Always state the condition.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P14', 'Sequences & Series', 'What happens if you use the sum-to-infinity formula when |r| ≥ 1?', 'It produces a plausible-looking number that is meaningless, because the series diverges.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P14', 'Sequences & Series', 'What is a recurrence relation?', 'A rule defining each term from the previous one, together with a starting value — for example u₍ₙ₊₁₎ = uₙ + 3 with u₁ = 2.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P14', 'Sequences & Series', 'How do you find the limit of a convergent recurrence relation?', 'Replace both u₍ₙ₊₁₎ and uₙ by L and solve for L, rejecting any root inconsistent with the sequence.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P14', 'Sequences & Series', 'Write the binomial expansion of (1 + x)ⁿ for a positive integer n.', '1 + nx + [n(n−1)/2!]x² + [n(n−1)(n−2)/3!]x³ + … It terminates, giving exactly n + 1 terms.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P14', 'Sequences & Series', 'What is the standard error when expanding (1 + 2x)⁵?', 'Forgetting to raise the coefficient too: (2x)³ = 8x³, not 2x³.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P14', 'Sequences & Series', 'How do you find a particular coefficient in (a + b)ⁿ?', 'Use the term ⁿCᵣ a^(n−r) b^r, choosing r to give the required power.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P14', 'Sequences & Series', 'What changes when the binomial index is negative or fractional?', 'The expansion no longer terminates — it becomes an infinite series — and it is valid only for |x| < 1.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P14', 'Sequences & Series', 'What is the validity condition for expanding (1 + ax)ⁿ with non-integer n?', '|ax| < 1, that is |x| < 1/|a|. It is NOT |x| < 1 whenever there is a coefficient on x.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P14', 'Sequences & Series', 'Expand (1 + x)^(1/2) as far as the term in x².', '1 + x/2 − x²/8.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P14', 'Sequences & Series', 'How do you expand √(4 + x) using the binomial series?', 'Factorise first: √(4 + x) = 2(1 + x/4)^(1/2). The series cannot be applied to a bracket not starting with 1.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P14', 'Sequences & Series', 'How do you find the nth term given the sum of the first n terms?', 'uₙ = Sₙ − S₍ₙ₋₁₎. If Sₙ = n² then uₙ = n² − (n − 1)² = 2n − 1.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P14', 'Sequences & Series', 'What are Σ from k=1 to n of k, and of (2k − 1)?', 'n(n + 1)/2 and n² respectively.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P14', 'Sequences & Series', 'What is the commonest confusion in sequences questions?', 'Mixing up uₙ (a single term) with Sₙ (a running total). Read the question twice.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P14', 'Sequences & Series', 'Find the 10th term of the arithmetic sequence 3, 7, 11, …', 'a = 3, d = 4: u₁₀ = 3 + 9 × 4 = 39.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P14', 'Sequences & Series', 'Find the sum of the first 20 terms of 3, 7, 11, …', 'S₂₀ = 20/2 × (2 × 3 + 19 × 4) = 10 × 82 = 820.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P14', 'Sequences & Series', 'Find the sum to infinity of 8 + 4 + 2 + …', 'a = 8, r = 1/2: S∞ = 8/(1 − 1/2) = 16.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P14', 'Sequences & Series', 'What is a periodic sequence?', 'A sequence that repeats after a fixed number of terms, e.g. 1, −1, 1, −1, …', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P14', 'Sequences & Series', 'What is an increasing sequence?', 'Each term is larger than the previous one: uₙ₊₁ > uₙ for all n.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P14', 'Sequences & Series', 'What is a divergent sequence?', 'A sequence that doesn''t tend to a finite limit.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P14', 'Sequences & Series', 'What is sigma notation?', 'A shorthand for sums: Σ from r=1 to n of f(r) means f(1) + f(2) + … + f(n).', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P14', 'Sequences & Series', 'What is ⁿCᵣ?', 'The number of ways of choosing r items from n: n!/(r!(n − r)!).', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P14', 'Sequences & Series', 'Find the coefficient of x³ in (2 + x)⁵.', '⁵C₃ × 2² × x³ = 10 × 4 = 40.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P14', 'Sequences & Series', 'Expand (1 − x)⁻¹ up to x³.', '1 + x + x² + x³, valid for |x| < 1.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P14', 'Sequences & Series', 'Expand (1 + 2x)⁻² up to x².', '1 − 4x + 12x², valid for |x| < 1/2.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P14', 'Sequences & Series', 'What is Pascal''s triangle used for?', 'To find binomial coefficients for small positive integer powers.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P14', 'Sequences & Series', 'What is n! ?', 'n × (n − 1) × … × 2 × 1, with 0! = 1.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P14', 'Sequences & Series', 'How do you find the number of terms in an arithmetic sequence?', 'Use uₙ = a + (n − 1)d, set it equal to the last term and solve for n.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P15', 'Radians', 'What is a radian?', 'The angle subtended at the centre of a circle by an arc equal in length to the radius.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P15', 'Radians', 'How many radians are in 360°?', '2π.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P15', 'Radians', 'Convert 90° to radians.', 'π/2.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P15', 'Radians', 'Convert 45° to radians.', 'π/4.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P15', 'Radians', 'Convert 60° to radians.', 'π/3.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P15', 'Radians', 'Convert 3π/2 radians to degrees.', '270°.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P15', 'Radians', 'Convert 1 radian to degrees.', '180/π ≈ 57.3°.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P15', 'Radians', 'What is the arc length formula in radians?', 's = rθ.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P15', 'Radians', 'What is the sector area formula in radians?', 'A = ½r²θ.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P15', 'Radians', 'Find the arc length for r = 5 and θ = 1.2 radians.', 's = 5 × 1.2 = 6.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P15', 'Radians', 'Find the area of a sector with r = 4 and θ = π/4.', 'A = ½ × 16 × π/4 = 2π.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P15', 'Radians', 'What is the area of a segment?', 'Area of sector − area of triangle = ½r²(θ − sin θ).', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P15', 'Radians', 'Find the area of a segment with r = 6 and θ = π/3.', '½ × 36 × (π/3 − √3/2) ≈ 3.26.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P15', 'Radians', 'What is the perimeter of a sector?', '2r + rθ.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P15', 'Radians', 'What is sin(π/2)?', '1.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P15', 'Radians', 'What is cos π?', '−1.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P15', 'Radians', 'What is tan(π/4)?', '1.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P15', 'Radians', 'What is sin(π/3)?', '√3/2.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P15', 'Radians', 'What is cos(π/6)?', '√3/2.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P15', 'Radians', 'What is the period of sin x in radians?', '2π.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P15', 'Radians', 'What is the period of tan x in radians?', 'π.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P15', 'Radians', 'Solve sin x = √2/2 for 0 ≤ x < 2π.', 'x = π/4 or 3π/4.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P15', 'Radians', 'Solve cos x = 0 for 0 ≤ x < 2π.', 'x = π/2 or 3π/2.', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P15', 'Radians', 'State the small angle approximation for sin θ.', 'sin θ ≈ θ (θ in radians).', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P15', 'Radians', 'State the small angle approximation for cos θ.', 'cos θ ≈ 1 − θ²/2.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P15', 'Radians', 'State the small angle approximation for tan θ.', 'tan θ ≈ θ.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P15', 'Radians', 'Use small angle approximations to simplify (1 − cos 2θ)/(θ sin θ).', '(1 − (1 − 2θ²))/(θ × θ) = 2θ²/θ² = 2.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P15', 'Radians', 'Why must calculus with trig functions use radians?', 'The derivative of sin x is cos x only when x is in radians.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P15', 'Radians', 'Where are the asymptotes of y = tan x in radians?', 'At x = π/2 + nπ.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P15', 'Radians', 'Solve tan x = √3 for 0 ≤ x < 2π.', 'x = π/3 or 4π/3.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P16', 'Trigonometric Functions', 'What is a radian, and how does it relate to degrees?', 'The angle subtended at the centre by an arc equal in length to the radius. π radians = 180°.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P16', 'Trigonometric Functions', 'Write down the arc length and sector area formulae.', 's = rθ and A = ½r²θ, both requiring θ in RADIANS. Using degrees gives an answer wrong by a factor of about 57.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P16', 'Trigonometric Functions', 'State the sine rule, the cosine rule and the triangle area formula.', 'a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C, a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A. Area = ½ab sin C.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P16', 'Trigonometric Functions', 'When do you use the cosine rule rather than the sine rule?', 'With three sides, or two sides and the angle between them. Otherwise use the sine rule.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P16', 'Trigonometric Functions', 'What is the ambiguous case of the sine rule?', 'Given two sides and a non-included angle, sin θ = k has two solutions in 0° to 180°: θ and 180° − θ. Both must be checked.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P16', 'Trigonometric Functions', 'Give the exact values of sin, cos and tan at 30°, 45° and 60°.', 'sin: ½, √2/2, √3/2.  cos: √3/2, √2/2, ½.  tan: 1/√3, 1, √3.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P16', 'Trigonometric Functions', 'How do you find the second solution of sin x = k, cos x = k and tan x = k?', 'For sin: π − x. For cos: 2π − x. For tan: x + π.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P16', 'Trigonometric Functions', 'How do you solve an equation such as sin 2x = k over 0° ≤ x < 360°?', 'Widen the range FIRST: 2x runs over 0° to 720°. Solve there, then halve every answer. Halving first loses half the solutions.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P16', 'Trigonometric Functions', 'Define sec, cosec and cot.', 'sec θ = 1/cos θ, cosec θ = 1/sin θ, cot θ = 1/tan θ. Look at the third letter: se-c goes with c-os.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P16', 'Trigonometric Functions', 'State the two identities derived from sin²θ + cos²θ = 1.', '1 + tan²θ = sec²θ (divide by cos²θ), 1 + cot²θ = cosec²θ (divide by sin²θ).', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P16', 'Trigonometric Functions', 'Write down the compound angle formulae for sin(A ± B) and cos(A ± B).', 'sin(A ± B) = sin A cos B ± cos A sin B, cos(A ± B) = cos A cos B ∓ sin A sin B — note the sign flip.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P16', 'Trigonometric Functions', 'Write down the three forms of cos 2θ.', 'cos²θ − sin²θ, 2cos²θ − 1, 1 − 2sin²θ. Choose the form matching the other function in the equation.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P16', 'Trigonometric Functions', 'What are sin 2θ and tan 2θ?', 'sin 2θ = 2 sin θ cos θ, tan 2θ = 2tan θ/(1 − tan²θ).', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P16', 'Trigonometric Functions', 'How do you write a sin x + b cos x as a single trigonometric function?', 'As R sin(x + α) with R = √(a² + b²) and tan α = b/a. The expression then ranges between −R and R.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P16', 'Trigonometric Functions', 'State the small angle approximations.', 'For small θ in RADIANS: sin θ ≈ θ, tan θ ≈ θ, cos θ ≈ 1 − θ²/2. Also (sin θ)/θ → 1 as θ → 0.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P16', 'Trigonometric Functions', 'What goes wrong if you divide a trigonometric equation by cos x?', 'You lose every solution where cos x = 0. Factorise instead of dividing, and check those cases separately.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P16', 'Trigonometric Functions', 'Convert 150° to radians.', '150 × π/180 = 5π/6.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P16', 'Trigonometric Functions', 'Find the arc length of a sector with radius 6 cm and angle π/3.', 's = rθ = 6 × π/3 = 2π cm.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P16', 'Trigonometric Functions', 'Find the area of a sector with radius 6 cm and angle π/3.', 'A = ½r²θ = ½ × 36 × π/3 = 6π cm².', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P16', 'Trigonometric Functions', 'What is the exact value of sin(π/6)?', '1/2.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P16', 'Trigonometric Functions', 'What is the exact value of cos(π/4)?', '√2/2 (or 1/√2).', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P16', 'Trigonometric Functions', 'What is the exact value of tan(π/3)?', '√3.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P16', 'Trigonometric Functions', 'What is the period of y = sin x and y = tan x?', 'sin x has period 2π (360°); tan x has period π (180°).', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P16', 'Trigonometric Functions', 'What is the identity linking tan, sin and cos?', 'tan θ = sin θ/cos θ.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P16', 'Trigonometric Functions', 'What is 1 + tan²θ equal to?', 'sec²θ.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P16', 'Trigonometric Functions', 'What is 1 + cot²θ equal to?', 'cosec²θ.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P16', 'Trigonometric Functions', 'Solve sin x = 0.5 for 0 ≤ x < 2π.', 'x = π/6 or x = 5π/6.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P16', 'Trigonometric Functions', 'What are the principal value ranges of arcsin, arccos and arctan?', 'arcsin: −π/2 to π/2; arccos: 0 to π; arctan: −π/2 to π/2 (exclusive).', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P16', 'Trigonometric Functions', 'Write sin 3x cos x + cos 3x sin x as a single term.', 'sin(3x + x) = sin 4x.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P16', 'Trigonometric Functions', 'Express 3 sin x + 4 cos x as R sin(x + α).', 'R = 5, tan α = 4/3, so α ≈ 0.927 rad (53.1°): 5 sin(x + 0.927).', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P17', 'Parametric Equations', 'What are parametric equations?', 'x and y are each given as functions of a third variable, the parameter. Each parameter value gives one point on the curve.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P17', 'Parametric Equations', 'Why are parametric equations useful?', 'They describe curves that cannot be written as y = f(x), such as a full circle, which fails the one-output-per-input test.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P17', 'Parametric Equations', 'How do you convert parametric equations to Cartesian form?', 'Eliminate the parameter. Rearrange the SIMPLER equation for the parameter, or use sin²θ + cos²θ = 1 for trigonometric parameters.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P17', 'Parametric Equations', 'What is dy/dx for a parametric curve?', 'dy/dx = (dy/dt) ÷ (dx/dt). Getting the fraction the wrong way up is a standard error.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P17', 'Parametric Equations', 'Write down the parametric equations of a circle.', 'x = a + r cos θ and y = b + r sin θ, for centre (a, b) and radius r. Eliminating θ with cos²θ + sin²θ = 1 returns the Cartesian form.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P17', 'Parametric Equations', 'Convert x = t², y = 2t into Cartesian form.', 't = y/2, so x = y²/4, i.e. y² = 4x.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P17', 'Parametric Equations', 'Find dy/dx for x = t², y = t³.', 'dy/dx = (3t²)/(2t) = 3t/2.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P17', 'Parametric Equations', 'What is the parametric form of the circle (x − a)² + (y − b)² = r²?', 'x = a + r cos θ, y = b + r sin θ.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P17', 'Parametric Equations', 'How do you find where a parametric curve meets the x-axis?', 'Set y = 0, solve for t, then substitute into x.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P17', 'Parametric Equations', 'Convert x = 2t, y = t² to Cartesian form.', 't = x/2, so y = x²/4.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P17', 'Parametric Equations', 'Convert x = 3 cos t, y = 3 sin t to Cartesian form.', 'x² + y² = 9 (a circle of radius 3).', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P17', 'Parametric Equations', 'Convert x = 2 cos t, y = 5 sin t to Cartesian form.', 'x²/4 + y²/25 = 1 (an ellipse).', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P17', 'Parametric Equations', 'Convert x = t + 1, y = 1/t to Cartesian form.', 't = x − 1, so y = 1/(x − 1).', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P17', 'Parametric Equations', 'How do you find the domain of the Cartesian equation from a parametric curve?', 'Use the range of x-values produced by the allowed values of t.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P17', 'Parametric Equations', 'Find dy/dx for x = 2t, y = t².', 'dy/dx = (2t)/2 = t.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P17', 'Parametric Equations', 'Find dy/dx for x = cos t, y = sin t.', 'dy/dx = cos t/(−sin t) = −cot t.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P17', 'Parametric Equations', 'Find the gradient of x = t², y = 2t at t = 3.', 'dy/dx = 2/(2t) = 1/t = 1/3.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P17', 'Parametric Equations', 'How do you find the equation of a tangent to a parametric curve?', 'Find the point and dy/dx at the given t, then use y − y₁ = m(x − x₁).', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P17', 'Parametric Equations', 'Find where x = t², y = t³ − 4t meets the x-axis.', 'y = t(t² − 4) = 0, so t = 0, ±2; x = 0 or 4.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P17', 'Parametric Equations', 'Find where the curve x = t − 1, y = t² meets the line y = x + 7.', 't² = t + 6, so t = 3 or −2; points (2, 9) and (−3, 4).', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P17', 'Parametric Equations', 'How do you find the area under a parametric curve?', '∫ y (dx/dt) dt, with limits in t.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P17', 'Parametric Equations', 'What is a stationary point on a parametric curve?', 'Where dy/dt = 0 (with dx/dt ≠ 0).', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P17', 'Parametric Equations', 'What is a vertical tangent on a parametric curve?', 'Where dx/dt = 0 (with dy/dt ≠ 0).', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P17', 'Parametric Equations', 'How are parametric equations used to model projectiles?', 'x = (u cos θ)t and y = (u sin θ)t − ½gt².', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P17', 'Parametric Equations', 'How do you find where two parametric curves intersect?', 'Solve for the parameter values giving the same x and y.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P17', 'Parametric Equations', 'Which identity helps convert trig parametric equations?', 'sin²t + cos²t = 1 (or 1 + tan²t = sec²t).', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P17', 'Parametric Equations', 'Convert x = sec t, y = tan t to Cartesian form.', 'sec²t − tan²t = 1, so x² − y² = 1.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P17', 'Parametric Equations', 'What is the parameter in parametric equations?', 'A third variable (often t or θ) that both x and y depend on.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P17', 'Parametric Equations', 'Convert x = e^t, y = e^(2t) to Cartesian form.', 'y = x², for x > 0.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P17', 'Parametric Equations', 'Why might a parametric curve be preferred to a Cartesian one?', 'Some curves are easier to describe, or show motion over time.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P18', 'Differentiation (Year 2)', 'What are the derivatives of xⁿ, eˣ and ln x?', 'nxⁿ⁻¹, eˣ, 1/x.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P18', 'Differentiation (Year 2)', 'What are the derivatives of sin x, cos x and tan x?', 'cos x, −sin x, sec²x. These hold only when x is in RADIANS.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P18', 'Differentiation (Year 2)', 'State the chain rule.', 'The derivative of f(g(x)) is f′(g(x)) × g′(x) — the outer derivative times the inner derivative.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P18', 'Differentiation (Year 2)', 'State the product rule.', 'The derivative of uv is u′v + uv′.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P18', 'Differentiation (Year 2)', 'State the quotient rule.', 'The derivative of u/v is (u′v − uv′)/v². Note the minus, and that the order matters — reversing it flips every sign.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P18', 'Differentiation (Year 2)', 'How do you differentiate implicitly?', 'Differentiate both sides with respect to x, applying the chain rule to every y term so each contributes a factor dy/dx, then collect and solve.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P18', 'Differentiation (Year 2)', 'How do you differentiate an inverse function?', 'Use dy/dx = 1 ÷ (dx/dy). For y = ln x, write x = e^y, so dx/dy = e^y = x and dy/dx = 1/x.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P18', 'Differentiation (Year 2)', 'How do you find the gradient of a tangent and of a normal?', 'The tangent gradient is dy/dx at that point, the normal gradient is −1 divided by that value.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P18', 'Differentiation (Year 2)', 'How do you classify a stationary point using the second derivative?', 'Positive means minimum, negative means maximum, zero is inconclusive and requires checking the gradient either side.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P18', 'Differentiation (Year 2)', 'How do connected rates of change work?', 'Link them by the chain rule, for example dV/dt = (dV/dr) × (dr/dt).', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P18', 'Differentiation (Year 2)', 'Differentiate y = √x.', 'y = x^(1/2), so dy/dx = ½x^(−1/2) = 1/(2√x).', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P18', 'Differentiation (Year 2)', 'Differentiate y = e^(3x).', 'dy/dx = 3e^(3x).', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P18', 'Differentiation (Year 2)', 'Differentiate y = ln(5x).', 'dy/dx = 1/x.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P18', 'Differentiation (Year 2)', 'Differentiate y = sin(2x).', 'dy/dx = 2 cos(2x).', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P18', 'Differentiation (Year 2)', 'Differentiate y = x² eˣ.', 'Product rule: dy/dx = 2x eˣ + x² eˣ = x eˣ(2 + x).', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P18', 'Differentiation (Year 2)', 'Differentiate y = (sin x)/x.', 'Quotient rule: dy/dx = (x cos x − sin x)/x².', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P18', 'Differentiation (Year 2)', 'What is the derivative of aˣ?', 'aˣ ln a.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P18', 'Differentiation (Year 2)', 'What is the derivative of sec x?', 'sec x tan x.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P18', 'Differentiation (Year 2)', 'Why do the trigonometric derivatives require radians?', 'They follow from the limit (sin h)/h → 1, which is true only in radians. In degrees an extra constant factor appears.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P18', 'Differentiation (Year 2)', 'Why does a dy/dx appear when differentiating a y term?', 'Because y is itself a function of x, so the chain rule applies: differentiating f(y) with respect to x gives f′(y) × dy/dx.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P18', 'Differentiation (Year 2)', 'Find the gradient of x² + y² = 25 at (3, 4).', '2x + 2y dy/dx = 0, so dy/dx = −x/y = −3/4.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P18', 'Differentiation (Year 2)', 'Differentiate y = e^(x²).', 'dy/dx = 2x e^(x²).', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P18', 'Differentiation (Year 2)', 'Differentiate y = ln(x² + 1).', 'dy/dx = 2x/(x² + 1).', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P18', 'Differentiation (Year 2)', 'Differentiate y = sin³x.', 'dy/dx = 3 sin²x cos x.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P18', 'Differentiation (Year 2)', 'Differentiate y = cos(3x + 1).', 'dy/dx = −3 sin(3x + 1).', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P18', 'Differentiation (Year 2)', 'Differentiate y = x ln x.', 'dy/dx = ln x + 1.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P18', 'Differentiation (Year 2)', 'Differentiate y = tan 2x.', 'dy/dx = 2 sec²2x.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P18', 'Differentiation (Year 2)', 'Find dy/dx if x = y².', 'dx/dy = 2y, so dy/dx = 1/(2y).', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P18', 'Differentiation (Year 2)', 'Differentiate x²y + y³ = 5 implicitly.', '2xy + x² dy/dx + 3y² dy/dx = 0, so dy/dx = −2xy/(x² + 3y²).', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P18', 'Differentiation (Year 2)', 'What is the derivative of cosec x?', '−cosec x cot x.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P19', 'Numerical Methods', 'State the change of sign condition for locating a root.', 'If f is CONTINUOUS on [a, b] and f(a), f(b) have opposite signs, then f(x) = 0 has at least one root in (a, b).', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P19', 'Numerical Methods', 'Which part of the change of sign argument is most often omitted?', 'The statement that f is continuous on the interval. Without it the argument is invalid.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P19', 'Numerical Methods', 'Name two situations in which a change of sign method fails.', 'A repeated root, where the curve touches without crossing, e.g. (x − 2)², and a vertical asymptote, e.g. 1/(x − 2), where the sign changes but there is no root.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P19', 'Numerical Methods', 'How do you show a root is 1.86 correct to 2 decimal places?', 'Show f changes sign between the rounding bounds 1.855 and 1.865, so every value in that interval rounds to 1.86.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P19', 'Numerical Methods', 'What is fixed point iteration?', 'Rearrange f(x) = 0 into x = g(x), choose a starting value, then apply x₍ₙ₊₁₎ = g(xₙ) repeatedly.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P19', 'Numerical Methods', 'When does a fixed point iteration converge?', 'When |g′(x)| < 1 near the root. If |g′(x)| > 1 it diverges, so different rearrangements of the same equation can behave differently.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P19', 'Numerical Methods', 'What is the difference between a staircase and a cobweb diagram?', 'Both are drawn between y = g(x) and y = x. A staircase occurs when g′(x) > 0, a cobweb, spiralling and alternating sides, when g′(x) < 0.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P19', 'Numerical Methods', 'State the Newton-Raphson formula and its geometric meaning.', 'x₍ₙ₊₁₎ = xₙ − f(xₙ)/f′(xₙ). Follow the tangent at the current point down to where it meets the x-axis.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P19', 'Numerical Methods', 'Why does Newton-Raphson converge faster than a simple iteration?', 'Near a simple root the error is roughly SQUARED at each step, so the number of correct digits approximately doubles.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P19', 'Numerical Methods', 'When does Newton-Raphson fail?', 'When f′(xₙ) = 0, since the tangent is horizontal and never meets the axis, and near a stationary point, where a nearly horizontal tangent throws the next estimate far away.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P19', 'Numerical Methods', 'State the trapezium rule.', 'With h = (b − a)/n, the integral is approximately (h/2)[y₀ + yₙ + 2(y₁ + … + y₍ₙ₋₁₎)] — first and last counted once, interior ordinates doubled.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P19', 'Numerical Methods', 'How many ordinates are needed for n strips?', 'n + 1, since adjacent strips share an ordinate.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P19', 'Numerical Methods', 'When does the trapezium rule give an over- or under-estimate?', 'Concave upwards: chords lie above the curve, so it is an OVERestimate. Concave downwards: chords lie below, so it is an UNDERestimate.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P19', 'Numerical Methods', 'How should you justify the direction of a trapezium error?', 'Refer to the concavity: ''the curve is concave upwards on this interval, so each chord lies above it and the estimate is too large''.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P19', 'Numerical Methods', 'What happens to the trapezium rule error if you halve the strip width?', 'It reduces by a factor of roughly 4, since the error is approximately proportional to the square of the width.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P19', 'Numerical Methods', 'How can rectangles give bounds on an integral?', 'For a monotonic function, left-hand and right-hand rectangle sums lie on either side of the true value, bracketing it.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P19', 'Numerical Methods', 'What does the change of sign method rely on?', 'If f is continuous on [a, b] and f(a) and f(b) have opposite signs, there is a root between a and b.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P19', 'Numerical Methods', 'Why can a change of sign occur without a root?', 'At a vertical asymptote (discontinuity), e.g. f(x) = 1/x between −1 and 1.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P19', 'Numerical Methods', 'Why might there be a root with no change of sign?', 'A repeated root where the curve touches the x-axis, or two roots close together.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P19', 'Numerical Methods', 'What is decimal search?', 'Checking values at intervals of 0.1, then 0.01, etc., to locate a root by change of sign.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P19', 'Numerical Methods', 'What is interval bisection?', 'Repeatedly halving the interval containing the root and keeping the half with a sign change.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P19', 'Numerical Methods', 'How do you rearrange f(x) = 0 into x = g(x)?', 'Make x the subject of part of the equation, e.g. x³ − x − 1 = 0 → x = ∛(x + 1).', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P19', 'Numerical Methods', 'What is the convergence condition for xₙ₊₁ = g(xₙ)?', '|g′(x)| < 1 near the root.', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P19', 'Numerical Methods', 'What is a staircase diagram?', 'A diagram showing iteration converging monotonically, when 0 < g′(x) < 1.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P19', 'Numerical Methods', 'What is a cobweb diagram?', 'A diagram showing iteration oscillating around the root, when −1 < g′(x) < 0.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P19', 'Numerical Methods', 'Apply one step of Newton–Raphson to f(x) = x² − 2 from x₀ = 1.', 'x₁ = 1 − (1 − 2)/2 = 1.5.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P19', 'Numerical Methods', 'When does the trapezium rule give an overestimate?', 'When the curve is concave up (convex) over the interval.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P19', 'Numerical Methods', 'When does the trapezium rule give an underestimate?', 'When the curve is concave down over the interval.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P19', 'Numerical Methods', 'How do you improve the accuracy of the trapezium rule?', 'Increase the number of strips (decrease the strip width).', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P19', 'Numerical Methods', 'What is the width h in the trapezium rule?', 'h = (b − a)/n, where n is the number of strips.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P20', 'Integration (Year 2)', 'Why is the modulus written in ln|x|?', 'So the result is valid for negative x too, since the logarithm of a negative number is undefined.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P20', 'Integration (Year 2)', 'When is integration by substitution the right method?', 'When the integrand contains an inner function together with its derivative — for example x e^(x²), where u = x² gives du = 2x dx.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P20', 'Integration (Year 2)', 'State the standard result for f′(x)/f(x).', 'Its integral is ln|f(x)| + c. For example the integral of 2x/(x² + 1) is ln(x² + 1) + c.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P20', 'Integration (Year 2)', 'State the integration by parts formula.', 'The integral of u(dv/dx) equals uv minus the integral of v(du/dx) — the reverse of the product rule.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P20', 'Integration (Year 2)', 'How do you choose u when integrating by parts?', 'Choose u to be the factor that becomes SIMPLER when differentiated, and dv/dx to be one you can integrate.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P20', 'Integration (Year 2)', 'How do you integrate ln x?', 'Write it as 1 × ln x, take u = ln x and dv/dx = 1. This gives x ln x − x + c.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P20', 'Integration (Year 2)', 'How do you integrate eˣ sin x?', 'Apply parts twice, the original integral reappears, so treat it as an unknown and solve the resulting equation algebraically.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P20', 'Integration (Year 2)', 'Why are partial fractions useful in integration?', 'A fraction with a factorised denominator has no standard integral, but splitting it gives terms A/(x + a), each integrating to a logarithm.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P20', 'Integration (Year 2)', 'How do you integrate cos²x?', 'Use the double angle identity cos²x = ½(1 + cos 2x), giving x/2 + (sin 2x)/4 + c.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P20', 'Integration (Year 2)', 'What are the integrals of sin x, cos x and sec²x?', '−cos x + c, sin x + c, tan x + c. The minus on the sine integral is the one most often dropped.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P20', 'Integration (Year 2)', 'Integrate 1/x².', '−1/x + c.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P20', 'Integration (Year 2)', 'Integrate e^(2x).', '½e^(2x) + c.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P20', 'Integration (Year 2)', 'Integrate 1/x.', 'ln|x| + c.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P20', 'Integration (Year 2)', 'Integrate x eˣ by parts.', 'u = x, dv = eˣ: x eˣ − ∫eˣ dx = x eˣ − eˣ + c.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P20', 'Integration (Year 2)', 'Integrate ln x.', 'x ln x − x + c.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P20', 'Integration (Year 2)', 'Integrate sin²x.', 'Use sin²x = ½(1 − cos 2x): ½x − ¼ sin 2x + c.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P20', 'Integration (Year 2)', 'Integrate tan x.', 'ln|sec x| + c (or −ln|cos x| + c).', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P20', 'Integration (Year 2)', 'What is the integral of sec x tan x?', 'sec x + c.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P20', 'Integration (Year 2)', 'What is the difference between a general and a particular solution?', 'A general solution contains an arbitrary constant and represents a family of curves, a particular solution uses an initial condition to fix that constant.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P20', 'Integration (Year 2)', 'How do you separate the variables?', 'If dy/dx = f(x)g(y), gather all the y terms with dy and all the x terms with dx, then integrate both sides.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P20', 'Integration (Year 2)', 'Translate ''the rate of growth is proportional to the size'' into an equation.', 'dP/dt = kP. For decay it is dN/dt = −kN with k positive.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P20', 'Integration (Year 2)', 'Translate ''the rate is proportional to the square of the population''.', 'dP/dt = kP².', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P20', 'Integration (Year 2)', 'Write down Newton''s law of cooling.', 'dθ/dt = −k(θ − θ₀), where θ₀ is the temperature of the surroundings — the rate is proportional to the excess temperature.', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P20', 'Integration (Year 2)', 'What is the solution of dP/dt = kP with P = P₀ at t = 0?', 'P = P₀e^(kt).', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P20', 'Integration (Year 2)', 'Why is it useful to write the constant as ln A?', 'It turns an additive constant into a multiplicative one, so ln y = ln x + c becomes y = Ax instead of y = e^c × x.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P20', 'Integration (Year 2)', 'Solve dy/dx = xy with y = 1 at x = 0.', 'Separate to (1/y)dy = x dx, integrate to ln y = x²/2 + c, apply the condition to get c = 0, so y = e^(x²/2).', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P20', 'Integration (Year 2)', 'What does dy/dt = k(A − y) describe?', 'A quantity rising towards a ceiling A, with the rate falling as the gap closes. The solution has the form y = A − Be^(−kt).', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P20', 'Integration (Year 2)', 'What is terminal velocity, and how is it found from dv/dt = g − kv?', 'The speed at which acceleration becomes zero, so g − kv = 0 and v = g/k. It is approached asymptotically, never reached exactly.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P20', 'Integration (Year 2)', 'Integrate cos(3x).', '⅓ sin(3x) + c.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P20', 'Integration (Year 2)', 'Integrate 2x/(x² + 1).', 'ln(x² + 1) + c (the numerator is the derivative of the denominator).', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P21', 'Vectors (3D)', 'What is the difference between a scalar and a vector?', 'A scalar has magnitude only (mass, time, speed), a vector has magnitude and direction (displacement, velocity, force).', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P21', 'Vectors (3D)', 'How are vectors written in print and by hand?', 'Bold in print, underlined by hand. The magnitude is written |a| or a in italic.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P21', 'Vectors (3D)', 'How do you find the magnitude of a vector in 2D and 3D?', '√(a₁² + a₂²) in two dimensions, √(a₁² + a₂² + a₃²) in three.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P21', 'Vectors (3D)', 'How do you find a unit vector in the direction of a?', 'Divide a by its magnitude: â = a/|a|. For 6i − 8j, the magnitude is 10 so the unit vector is 0.6i − 0.8j.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P21', 'Vectors (3D)', 'When are two non-zero vectors parallel?', 'When one is a scalar multiple of the other. A negative scalar reverses the direction but they remain parallel.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P21', 'Vectors (3D)', 'Write down the vector AB in terms of position vectors.', 'AB = b − a — destination minus start. Reversing it gives the same magnitude in the opposite direction.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P21', 'Vectors (3D)', 'How do you find the distance between two points from their position vectors?', 'It is |b − a|, the magnitude of the vector joining them.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P21', 'Vectors (3D)', 'What is the position vector of the midpoint of AB?', '(a + b)/2. More generally the point dividing AB in the ratio m : n is (na + mb)/(m + n).', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P21', 'Vectors (3D)', 'How do you convert between component and magnitude-direction form?', 'a₁ = r cos θ and a₂ = r sin θ, conversely r = √(a₁² + a₂²) and tan θ = a₂/a₁.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P21', 'Vectors (3D)', 'Why is arctan alone not enough to give a direction?', 'It returns angles only in the first and fourth quadrants. Use the signs of the components to identify the quadrant and adjust — (−3, −4) gives 233.1°, not 53.1°.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P21', 'Vectors (3D)', 'How do you prove three points collinear using vectors?', 'Show one joining vector is a scalar multiple of another, AND state that they share a common point. Parallel alone is not enough.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P21', 'Vectors (3D)', 'What does the sum of force vectors represent?', 'The resultant force. The specification explicitly includes using vectors for problems involving forces.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P21', 'Vectors (3D)', 'What is the condition for a set of forces to be in equilibrium?', 'Their vector sum is the zero vector, so drawn head to tail they form a closed figure.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P21', 'Vectors (3D)', 'Is |a + b| equal to |a| + |b|?', 'Not in general. It lies between ||a| − |b|| and |a| + |b|, reaching the upper value only when the vectors point the same way.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P21', 'Vectors (3D)', 'How does 3D vector work extend the 2D case?', 'A third component along k is added. Magnitudes involve the sum of three squares, and all the 2D techniques carry over.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P21', 'Vectors (3D)', 'What is the zero vector?', 'A vector with zero magnitude and no specific direction.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P21', 'Vectors (3D)', 'Find the magnitude of 3i − 4j.', '√(9 + 16) = 5.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P21', 'Vectors (3D)', 'Find the unit vector in the direction of 3i − 4j.', '(3i − 4j)/5 = 0.6i − 0.8j.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P21', 'Vectors (3D)', 'If a = 2i + j and b = i − 3j, find 2a − b.', '4i + 2j − i + 3j = 3i + 5j.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P21', 'Vectors (3D)', 'What is a position vector?', 'A vector giving the position of a point relative to the origin.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P21', 'Vectors (3D)', 'Find the direction of 3i + 3j.', '45° above the positive x-axis.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P21', 'Vectors (3D)', 'What is the magnitude of 2i − j + 2k?', '√(4 + 1 + 4) = 3.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P21', 'Vectors (3D)', 'How do you find a vector of magnitude 10 in the direction of 3i + 4j?', 'Unit vector (3i + 4j)/5, times 10: 6i + 8j.', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P21', 'Vectors (3D)', 'If AB = b − a, what is BA?', 'a − b.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P21', 'Vectors (3D)', 'What is the resultant of forces (3i + 2j) N and (−i + 4j) N?', '2i + 6j N.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P21', 'Vectors (3D)', 'Express 10 N at 30° above the x-axis in component form.', '10 cos 30° i + 10 sin 30° j = 5√3 i + 5j N.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P21', 'Vectors (3D)', 'How do you show two vectors are parallel?', 'Show one is a scalar multiple of the other.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P21', 'Vectors (3D)', 'What is a column vector?', 'A vector written vertically with components, e.g. (3, 4) written as a column.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P21', 'Vectors (3D)', 'What does it mean for vectors to be equal?', 'They have the same magnitude and direction (equal components).', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'P21', 'Vectors (3D)', 'Find the magnitude of the vector 3i − 6j + 2k.', '√(9 + 36 + 4) = 7.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Statistical Sampling', 'What is the difference between a population and a sample?', 'A population is the entire group being studied, a sample is a subset used to draw conclusions about it.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Statistical Sampling', 'What is the difference between a parameter and a statistic?', 'A parameter describes the POPULATION, a statistic is calculated from a SAMPLE and used to estimate it.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Statistical Sampling', 'Define simple random sampling.', 'Every possible sample of the required size has the same probability of being selected. It requires a complete sampling frame.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Statistical Sampling', 'What is a sampling frame?', 'A list of all members of the population, from which the sample is drawn. An incomplete frame means some members can never be selected.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Statistical Sampling', 'Describe systematic sampling and its main risk.', 'Select every kth member of an ordered list. It fails if the list has a repeating pattern matching the sampling interval.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Statistical Sampling', 'Describe stratified sampling.', 'Divide the population into strata and sample each in proportion to its size, selecting AT RANDOM within each stratum.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Statistical Sampling', 'How does quota sampling differ from stratified sampling?', 'Both fix the number per category, but quota sampling does not select at random within each — the interviewer chooses whoever is convenient.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Statistical Sampling', 'Describe cluster sampling and when it is used.', 'Divide the population into groups and survey whole groups chosen at random. Useful when the population is spread over a wide area and travel costs matter.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Statistical Sampling', 'Why are self-selected samples usually biased?', 'People with strong opinions are far more likely to volunteer, so such samples over-represent strong views.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Statistical Sampling', 'What is opportunity sampling and its weakness?', 'Selecting whoever is conveniently available. Quick and needs no frame, but unlikely to be representative.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Statistical Sampling', 'Define bias in sampling.', 'A method is biased if it systematically favours certain outcomes, so estimates from it are systematically wrong.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Statistical Sampling', 'Name three common sources of bias.', 'An incomplete sampling frame, non-response, where non-responders differ from responders, and the time or place of sampling.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Statistical Sampling', 'Does increasing the sample size remove bias?', 'No. It reduces VARIABILITY — the standard deviation of the sample mean is σ/√n — but a badly designed large sample is still biased.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Statistical Sampling', 'By what factor does quadrupling the sample size reduce the standard deviation of the sample mean?', 'By a factor of 2, since the standard deviation is σ/√n and √4 = 2.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Statistical Sampling', 'Why do two random samples from the same population give different means?', 'Sampling variability. Different samples contain different individuals, so neither result is an error.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Statistical Sampling', 'What is the purpose of the large data set, and what is available in the exam?', 'To make its contexts familiar through classroom use. No printout is provided in the exam, though selected data or summary statistics may be given. From September 2026 a single LDS is used.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Statistical Sampling', 'What is a census?', 'Data collected from every member of the population.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Statistical Sampling', 'What are the advantages of a census?', 'It is complete and accurate, with no sampling variability.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Statistical Sampling', 'What are the disadvantages of a census?', 'It is expensive and time-consuming, and impractical for large populations or destructive tests.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Statistical Sampling', 'What are the advantages of sampling?', 'Cheaper, quicker and more practical than a census.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Statistical Sampling', 'What is a representative sample?', 'A sample that reflects the characteristics of the population.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Statistical Sampling', 'How is a simple random sample selected?', 'Number each member of the sampling frame and use random numbers to select them.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Statistical Sampling', 'What is the advantage of stratified sampling?', 'It ensures each subgroup is represented in proportion to its size.', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Statistical Sampling', 'What is the disadvantage of quota sampling?', 'It is non-random, so interviewer bias may occur.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Statistical Sampling', 'What is the advantage of systematic sampling?', 'It is simple and quick to carry out.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Statistical Sampling', 'What is the disadvantage of cluster sampling?', 'Clusters may not be representative of the whole population.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Statistical Sampling', 'What is the advantage of opportunity sampling?', 'It is quick, easy and cheap.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Statistical Sampling', 'What is non-response bias?', 'Bias caused when people who don''t respond differ from those who do.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Statistical Sampling', 'What is the population in a statistical study?', 'The whole group of interest.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Statistical Sampling', 'What is sampling variability?', 'Different samples from the same population give different results.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Data Presentation & Measures', 'Name the four types of data.', 'Categorical (labels), discrete (separate numerical values), continuous (any value in a range) and ranked (ordered positions).', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Data Presentation & Measures', 'In a histogram, what represents frequency?', 'The AREA of each bar. The vertical axis is frequency density, which is frequency divided by class width.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Data Presentation & Measures', 'How does a frequency chart differ from a histogram?', 'A frequency chart has equal-width bars with FREQUENCY on the vertical axis, a histogram may have unequal widths and uses frequency density.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Data Presentation & Measures', 'What does a box-and-whisker diagram show?', 'The median, the lower and upper quartiles, and the extreme values. The box spans the interquartile range.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Data Presentation & Measures', 'When is the median preferred to the mean?', 'For skewed data or when outliers are present, since the median depends only on position in the order and resists extremes.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Data Presentation & Measures', 'When is a weighted mean appropriate?', 'When combining groups of different sizes, weighting each group mean by the size of that group.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Data Presentation & Measures', 'What is the formula for the sample variance?', 's² = Sxx/(n − 1). Dividing by n − 1, not n, makes it an unbiased estimator of the population variance.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Data Presentation & Measures', 'What are the units of variance and standard deviation?', 'Standard deviation carries the same units as the data, variance carries their square.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Data Presentation & Measures', 'What happens to the mean and standard deviation if a constant is added to every value?', 'The mean increases by that constant, the standard deviation is UNCHANGED, since a translation does not alter the spread.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Data Presentation & Measures', 'What happens if every value is multiplied by k?', 'Both the mean and the standard deviation are multiplied by |k|.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Data Presentation & Measures', 'State the two accepted criteria for an outlier.', 'At least 2 standard deviations from the mean, OR at least 1.5 × IQR beyond the nearer quartile.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Data Presentation & Measures', 'What does data cleaning involve?', 'Dealing with missing data, correcting or removing clear errors, and deciding how to treat outliers — always with the reason stated.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Data Presentation & Measures', 'Does correlation imply causation?', 'No. A third variable may drive both, as with ice cream sales and drownings, which are both driven by hot weather.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Data Presentation & Measures', 'What is discrete data?', 'Data that can only take specific values, often counts, e.g. the number of goals.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Data Presentation & Measures', 'What is continuous data?', 'Data that can take any value in a range, e.g. height or time.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Data Presentation & Measures', 'What is categorical data?', 'Data in categories, e.g. colour or type.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Data Presentation & Measures', 'What is ranked data?', 'Ordinal data that can be put in order.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Data Presentation & Measures', 'What is frequency density?', 'Frequency ÷ class width, used for histogram heights.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Data Presentation & Measures', 'What is the interquartile range?', 'Upper quartile − lower quartile, the range of the middle 50%.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Data Presentation & Measures', 'What is the mode?', 'The most frequent value.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Data Presentation & Measures', 'What is the midrange?', '(Maximum + minimum) ÷ 2.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Data Presentation & Measures', 'How is the mean calculated from a frequency table?', 'Σfx ÷ Σf.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Data Presentation & Measures', 'What is the root mean square deviation?', '√(Sxx/n), where Sxx = Σ(x − x̄)².', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Data Presentation & Measures', 'What is Sxx?', 'Σ(x − x̄)² = Σx² − (Σx)²/n.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Data Presentation & Measures', 'What does positive skew look like?', 'A long tail to the right; mean > median > mode.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Data Presentation & Measures', 'What does negative skew look like?', 'A long tail to the left; mean < median < mode.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Data Presentation & Measures', 'What does a scatter diagram show?', 'The relationship between two variables for bivariate data.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Data Presentation & Measures', 'What is an outlier using the 1.5 × IQR rule?', 'A value more than 1.5 × IQR below Q₁ or above Q₃.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Data Presentation & Measures', 'How do you compare two distributions?', 'Compare a measure of location (mean or median) and a measure of spread (SD or IQR), in context.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Data Presentation & Measures', 'What is linear interpolation used for?', 'Estimating the median or quartiles from grouped data.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Probability', 'How is probability calculated for equally likely outcomes?', 'Favourable outcomes divided by total outcomes. Every probability lies between 0 and 1 inclusive.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Probability', 'What is a complementary event?', 'A′ is the event that A does not occur, and P(A′) = 1 − P(A). It is the fastest route for ''at least one'' questions.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Probability', 'What is the expected frequency of an event in n trials?', 'nP(A). In 200 trials with probability 0.15, the expected frequency is 30.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Probability', 'What do A ∩ B and A ∪ B mean?', 'A ∩ B means both occur. A ∪ B means A or B or both occur.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Probability', 'Define mutually exclusive events and give the addition rule.', 'They cannot both occur, so P(A ∩ B) = 0 and P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B).', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Probability', 'Define independent events and give the multiplication rule.', 'The occurrence of one does not affect the probability of the other, so P(A ∩ B) = P(A)P(B).', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Probability', 'Can mutually exclusive events with non-zero probabilities be independent?', 'No. If B occurred then A definitely did not, which is a strong dependence. The two ideas are generally incompatible.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Probability', 'State the general addition rule.', 'P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A ∩ B). The subtraction avoids double-counting the overlap.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Probability', 'State the conditional probability formula.', 'P(A | B) = P(A ∩ B)/P(B). Rearranged, it gives the multiplication rule that a tree diagram encodes.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Probability', 'What is the test for independence using conditional probability?', 'A and B are independent if and only if P(A | B) = P(A) — knowing B tells you nothing about A.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Probability', 'How does drawing without replacement differ from with replacement?', 'Without replacement the composition changes, so the draws are dependent and conditional probabilities are needed. With replacement they are independent.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Probability', 'How do you use a tree diagram?', 'Multiply along a path to find the probability of that sequence, add the probabilities of all paths giving the required outcome. Branches from any node sum to 1.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Probability', 'Why can a 95% accurate test give mostly false positives?', 'The small false-positive rate applies to the large healthy majority. In 10 000 people with 1% prevalence: 95 true positives against 495 false ones, so only about 16% of positives are genuine.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Probability', 'What does P(A|B) = 0 tell you about mutually exclusive events?', 'That if B has occurred, A cannot have occurred — the conditional probability is zero.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Probability', 'What is a sample space?', 'The set of all possible outcomes.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Probability', 'What is an event?', 'A set of outcomes from the sample space.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Probability', 'What is P(A'') ?', '1 − P(A), the probability that A doesn''t happen.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Probability', 'If P(A) = 0.4 and P(B) = 0.5 and A and B are independent, find P(A ∩ B).', '0.4 × 0.5 = 0.2.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Probability', 'If A and B are mutually exclusive, what is P(A ∪ B)?', 'P(A) + P(B).', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Probability', 'What is P(A ∪ B) in general?', 'P(A) + P(B) − P(A ∩ B).', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Probability', 'What is P(A | B)?', 'P(A ∩ B)/P(B).', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Probability', 'What is a two-way table used for?', 'Showing frequencies for two categorical variables, useful for finding conditional probabilities.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Probability', 'How do you find the probability of at least one success?', '1 − P(no successes).', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Probability', 'How many ways can 5 people be arranged in a line?', '5! = 120.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Probability', 'How many ways can 3 people be chosen from 8?', '⁸C₃ = 56.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Probability', 'What is P(A | A)?', '1.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Probability', 'What does it mean if P(A | B) = P(A)?', 'A and B are independent.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Probability', 'What is the probability of two sixes when rolling two fair dice?', '1/6 × 1/6 = 1/36.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Probability', 'What is a sample space diagram?', 'A table or grid listing all possible outcomes of two events, e.g. two dice.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Probability', 'Two fair dice are rolled. What is P(total = 7)?', '6 outcomes out of 36, so 1/6.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Binomial Distribution', 'What is a discrete random variable?', 'A variable taking separate values each with a given probability. Capital X denotes the variable, lower-case x or r a particular value.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Binomial Distribution', 'What condition must a discrete probability distribution satisfy?', 'All probabilities non-negative and summing to 1.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Binomial Distribution', 'What is a discrete uniform distribution?', 'One in which all outcomes are equally likely, such as the score on a fair die.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Binomial Distribution', 'State the four conditions for a binomial model.', 'A fixed number of trials, exactly two outcomes per trial, the same probability of success each time, and independent trials.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Binomial Distribution', 'What does X ~ B(n, p) mean?', 'X has the binomial distribution with n trials and probability p of success. The symbol ~ is read ''has the distribution''.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Binomial Distribution', 'Write down the binomial probability formula.', 'P(X = r) = ⁿCᵣ pʳ q^(n−r), where q = 1 − p.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Binomial Distribution', 'What is the mean of a binomial distribution?', 'np. For X ~ B(20, 0.3) the mean is 6.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Binomial Distribution', 'How do you find P(X ≥ 1) for a binomial?', 'Use the complement: 1 − P(X = 0) = 1 − qⁿ. Far quicker than summing the terms.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Binomial Distribution', 'When does a binomial model fail, and when is it still acceptable?', 'It fails for sampling without replacement, since p changes. It remains a good approximation when the population is very large relative to the sample.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Binomial Distribution', 'What is the probability function of a random variable?', 'A rule giving P(X = x) for each value x.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Binomial Distribution', 'What is E(X) for a discrete random variable?', 'Σ x P(X = x).', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Binomial Distribution', 'What is the probability of rolling a 4 on a fair die (discrete uniform)?', '1/6.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Binomial Distribution', 'Find P(X = 2) if X ~ B(5, 0.3).', '⁵C₂ × 0.3² × 0.7³ = 10 × 0.09 × 0.343 = 0.3087.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Binomial Distribution', 'What is the variance of a binomial distribution?', 'np(1 − p).', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Binomial Distribution', 'What is the shape of a binomial distribution when p = 0.5?', 'Symmetrical.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Binomial Distribution', 'What is the shape of a binomial distribution when p < 0.5?', 'Positively skewed.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Binomial Distribution', 'How do you find P(X ≤ 3) using a calculator?', 'Use the binomial cumulative distribution function.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Binomial Distribution', 'What is the cumulative binomial table used for?', 'Finding P(X ≤ x) for X ~ B(n, p).', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Binomial Distribution', 'If X ~ B(10, 0.2), how do you find P(X > 3)?', '1 − P(X ≤ 3).', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Binomial Distribution', 'If X ~ B(10, 0.2), how do you find P(2 ≤ X ≤ 5)?', 'P(X ≤ 5) − P(X ≤ 1).', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Binomial Distribution', 'What is P(X < 4) in terms of the cumulative function?', 'P(X ≤ 3).', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Binomial Distribution', 'A fair coin is thrown 8 times. What is P(exactly 3 heads)?', '⁸C₃ × 0.5⁸ = 56/256 ≈ 0.219.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Binomial Distribution', 'A die is rolled 12 times. What distribution models the number of sixes?', 'B(12, 1/6).', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Binomial Distribution', 'What is the expected number of successes in B(20, 0.3)?', '20 × 0.3 = 6.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Binomial Distribution', 'Why must trials be independent in a binomial model?', 'Otherwise the probability of success could change between trials.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Binomial Distribution', 'Why must p be constant in a binomial model?', 'The model assumes each trial has the same probability of success.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Binomial Distribution', 'Give an example of a situation not suitable for a binomial model.', 'Drawing cards without replacement, because the probability changes each draw.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Binomial Distribution', 'What is the probability of all successes in B(n, p)?', 'pⁿ.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Binomial Distribution', 'What is the probability of no successes in B(n, p)?', '(1 − p)ⁿ.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Binomial Distribution', 'What is the mode of a binomial distribution?', 'The most likely number of successes, found from a table or calculator.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S5', 'Hypothesis Testing — Binomial', 'What are the null and alternative hypotheses?', 'H₀ is the default assumption being tested, always an equality. H₁ is what is concluded if H₀ is rejected.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S5', 'Hypothesis Testing — Binomial', 'Must hypotheses refer to the sample or the population?', 'The POPULATION parameter — p or μ. Writing H₀: x̄ = 50 is wrong, it must be H₀: μ = 50.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S5', 'Hypothesis Testing — Binomial', 'What is the significance level?', 'The probability of incorrectly rejecting a TRUE null hypothesis. It sets the size of the critical region.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S5', 'Hypothesis Testing — Binomial', 'Define critical region, acceptance region and critical value.', 'The critical (rejection) region contains test statistic values leading to rejection, the acceptance region those that do not, the critical value is the boundary.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S5', 'Hypothesis Testing — Binomial', 'What is a p-value and how is it used?', 'The probability of a result at least as extreme as the one observed, assuming H₀. Reject H₀ if it is below the significance level.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S5', 'Hypothesis Testing — Binomial', 'When is a test one-tailed and when two-tailed?', 'One-tailed when H₁ specifies a DIRECTION (''biased towards heads''), two-tailed when it does not (''biased'', ''has changed'').', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S5', 'Hypothesis Testing — Binomial', 'For a two-tailed test at the 5% level, what probability does each tail carry?', '0.025 — the level is split equally between the tails.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S5', 'Hypothesis Testing — Binomial', 'Why should you never write ''accept H₀''?', 'Failing to reject is not proof that H₀ is true, it means only that the evidence was not strong enough. Write ''insufficient evidence to reject H₀''.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S5', 'Hypothesis Testing — Binomial', 'Does rejecting H₀ prove the alternative?', 'No. It gives EVIDENCE, never proof — an unlikely result can still occur by chance.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S5', 'Hypothesis Testing — Binomial', 'What form does H₀ take in a binomial test, and what is the test statistic?', 'H₀: p = a particular value. The test statistic is the observed number of successes.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S5', 'Hypothesis Testing — Binomial', 'Why is the rejection probability for a binomial test usually below the significance level?', 'Because the binomial distribution is discrete, so probabilities accumulate in jumps and the region cannot usually match the level exactly.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S5', 'Hypothesis Testing — Binomial', 'What is the distribution of the sample mean from N(μ, σ²)?', 'Normal with mean μ and variance σ²/n, so its standard deviation is σ/√n.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S5', 'Hypothesis Testing — Binomial', 'When is a Normal test on a mean valid?', 'When the population variance is known, OR it is unknown but the sample is large. A small sample with unknown variance satisfies neither.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S5', 'Hypothesis Testing — Binomial', 'Write down the test statistic for a Normal test on a mean.', 'z = (x̄ − μ)/(σ/√n). Dividing by σ instead of σ/√n is the usual slip, and it shrinks z, making real effects harder to detect.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S5', 'Hypothesis Testing — Binomial', 'What must every hypothesis test conclusion include?', 'A statement in the CONTEXT of the original problem, not merely ''reject H₀''.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S5', 'Hypothesis Testing — Binomial', 'What is a test statistic?', 'A value calculated from sample data used to decide whether to reject H₀.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S5', 'Hypothesis Testing — Binomial', 'What is a Type I error?', 'Rejecting H₀ when it is true.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S5', 'Hypothesis Testing — Binomial', 'What is the probability of a Type I error?', 'The actual significance level (the probability of the observed value being in the critical region if H₀ is true).', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S5', 'Hypothesis Testing — Binomial', 'State the hypotheses for testing whether a coin is biased towards heads.', 'H₀: p = 0.5; H₁: p > 0.5, where p is the probability of heads.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S5', 'Hypothesis Testing — Binomial', 'What does a significance level of 5% mean?', 'The probability of rejecting H₀ when it is true is at most 5%.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S5', 'Hypothesis Testing — Binomial', 'What is the conclusion if the p-value is less than the significance level?', 'Reject H₀: there is sufficient evidence to support H₁.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S5', 'Hypothesis Testing — Binomial', 'What is the conclusion if the test statistic isn''t in the critical region?', 'Do not reject H₀: there is insufficient evidence to support H₁.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S5', 'Hypothesis Testing — Binomial', 'How do you find a critical region for a binomial test?', 'Find the values where the cumulative probability in the tail is less than or equal to the significance level.', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S5', 'Hypothesis Testing — Binomial', 'What is the sampling distribution of the mean for a Normal population?', 'X̄ ~ N(μ, σ²/n).', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S5', 'Hypothesis Testing — Binomial', 'What is the test statistic for a Normal mean test?', 'z = (x̄ − μ)/(σ/√n).', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S5', 'Hypothesis Testing — Binomial', 'What is the critical value for a one-tailed 5% Normal test?', '1.645.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S5', 'Hypothesis Testing — Binomial', 'What is the critical value for a two-tailed 5% Normal test?', '±1.96.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S5', 'Hypothesis Testing — Binomial', 'Why should conclusions be stated in context?', 'The conclusion must relate to the original problem, not just state "reject H₀".', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S5', 'Hypothesis Testing — Binomial', 'What is a hypothesis test on a correlation coefficient used for?', 'To test whether there is evidence of correlation in the population, using the sample correlation coefficient.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S5', 'Hypothesis Testing — Binomial', 'What is the actual significance level of a binomial test?', 'The probability of the test statistic falling in the critical region when H₀ is true.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Regression & Correlation', 'What is bivariate data?', 'Data with two variables for each item, e.g. height and weight.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Regression & Correlation', 'What is an explanatory variable?', 'The independent variable, plotted on the x-axis.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Regression & Correlation', 'What is a response variable?', 'The dependent variable, plotted on the y-axis.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Regression & Correlation', 'What is correlation?', 'A relationship between two variables.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Regression & Correlation', 'What does positive correlation mean?', 'As one variable increases, the other tends to increase.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Regression & Correlation', 'What does negative correlation mean?', 'As one variable increases, the other tends to decrease.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Regression & Correlation', 'What does no correlation mean?', 'There is no linear relationship between the variables.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Regression & Correlation', 'What is the product moment correlation coefficient (PMCC)?', 'A measure of the strength of linear correlation, r, between −1 and 1.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Regression & Correlation', 'What does r = 1 mean?', 'Perfect positive linear correlation.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Regression & Correlation', 'What does r = −0.9 mean?', 'Strong negative linear correlation.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Regression & Correlation', 'What does r close to 0 mean?', 'Little or no linear correlation.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Regression & Correlation', 'What is a regression line?', 'The line of best fit, y = a + bx, used to predict y from x.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Regression & Correlation', 'What does the gradient b in a regression line mean?', 'The change in y for each unit increase in x.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Regression & Correlation', 'What does the intercept a mean?', 'The value of y when x = 0 (may not be meaningful in context).', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Regression & Correlation', 'What is interpolation?', 'Predicting within the range of the data, which is usually reliable.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Regression & Correlation', 'What is extrapolation?', 'Predicting outside the range of the data, which is unreliable.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Regression & Correlation', 'Why can''t you use the regression line of y on x to predict x?', 'It minimises errors in y, not x, so it isn''t reliable for predicting x.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Regression & Correlation', 'How do you test for correlation using the PMCC?', 'H₀: ρ = 0, H₁: ρ ≠ 0 (or > 0 / < 0). Compare r with the critical value for the sample size.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Regression & Correlation', 'What is ρ?', 'The population correlation coefficient.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Regression & Correlation', 'What is the critical value in a PMCC test?', 'The value from tables that r must exceed (in absolute value) to reject H₀.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Regression & Correlation', 'How can an exponential model be linearised?', 'If y = ab^x, then log y = log a + x log b, giving a straight line.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Regression & Correlation', 'How can a power model be linearised?', 'If y = ax^n, then log y = log a + n log x, giving a straight line.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Regression & Correlation', 'What does it mean if log y against x is a straight line?', 'y and x are related by an exponential model.', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Regression & Correlation', 'What does it mean if log y against log x is a straight line?', 'y and x are related by a power model.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Regression & Correlation', 'What does causation mean?', 'One variable directly causes changes in another.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Regression & Correlation', 'Why does correlation not imply causation?', 'A third variable may cause both, or the link may be coincidence.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Regression & Correlation', 'What is a scatter diagram?', 'A graph plotting bivariate data as points to show a relationship.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Regression & Correlation', 'What is an outlier in bivariate data?', 'A point that doesn''t fit the general pattern of the data.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Regression & Correlation', 'What is the effect of an outlier on the PMCC?', 'It can greatly change the value of r.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Regression & Correlation', 'When is a regression line reliable?', 'When correlation is strong and predictions are made within the data range.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Conditional Probability', 'What is conditional probability?', 'The probability of an event given that another event has occurred.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Conditional Probability', 'State the formula for P(A | B).', 'P(A | B) = P(A ∩ B)/P(B).', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Conditional Probability', 'If P(A ∩ B) = 0.12 and P(B) = 0.4, find P(A | B).', '0.12/0.4 = 0.3.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Conditional Probability', 'How can a two-way table find conditional probability?', 'Divide the frequency for both events by the total for the given condition.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Conditional Probability', 'How can a tree diagram find conditional probability?', 'The second branches show probabilities conditional on the first outcome.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Conditional Probability', 'What is the multiplication rule for dependent events?', 'P(A ∩ B) = P(A) × P(B | A).', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Conditional Probability', 'When are A and B independent?', 'When P(A | B) = P(A), or equivalently P(A ∩ B) = P(A) × P(B).', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Conditional Probability', 'If P(A) = 0.5, P(B) = 0.4 and P(A ∩ B) = 0.2, are A and B independent?', 'Yes: 0.5 × 0.4 = 0.2.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Conditional Probability', 'What is P(A'' | B)?', '1 − P(A | B).', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Conditional Probability', 'How do you use a Venn diagram for conditional probability?', 'P(A | B) = region A ∩ B ÷ region B.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Conditional Probability', 'What is P(A ∪ B)?', 'P(A) + P(B) − P(A ∩ B).', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Conditional Probability', 'What is P(A'' ∩ B)?', 'P(B) − P(A ∩ B).', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Conditional Probability', 'Two cards are drawn without replacement from 5 red and 3 blue. Find P(both red).', '5/8 × 4/7 = 5/14.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Conditional Probability', 'Given the first card is red, find P(second card is blue).', '3/7.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Conditional Probability', 'What is Bayes'' idea in reverse conditional probability?', 'P(A | B) = P(B | A)P(A)/P(B).', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Conditional Probability', 'How do you find P(B) using a tree diagram?', 'Add the probabilities of all branches ending in B.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Conditional Probability', 'A test is 90% accurate and 1% of people have a condition. Why are many positives false?', 'Most people don''t have the condition, so 10% false positives among the 99% outnumber the true positives.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Conditional Probability', 'What is set notation for "A or B but not both"?', '(A ∩ B'') ∪ (A'' ∩ B).', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Conditional Probability', 'What does A'' mean?', 'The complement of A: not A.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Conditional Probability', 'What is P(A ∩ B) if A and B are mutually exclusive?', '0.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Conditional Probability', 'What does P(A | B) = 1 mean?', 'Whenever B happens, A also happens (B is a subset of A).', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Conditional Probability', 'How do you find P(A | B'') from a Venn diagram?', 'P(A ∩ B'')/P(B'').', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Conditional Probability', 'What is the complement rule?', 'P(A'') = 1 − P(A).', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Conditional Probability', 'How do three events appear on a Venn diagram?', 'Three overlapping circles, with regions for each combination.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Conditional Probability', 'What is P(A ∩ B ∩ C) for independent events?', 'P(A) × P(B) × P(C).', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Conditional Probability', 'If P(A | B) = 0.6 and P(B) = 0.5, find P(A ∩ B).', '0.6 × 0.5 = 0.3.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Conditional Probability', 'How can you show events are not independent?', 'Show that P(A ∩ B) ≠ P(A) × P(B).', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Conditional Probability', 'What is a probability tree for two dependent events?', 'First branches show P(A) and P(A''); second branches show conditional probabilities.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Conditional Probability', 'What is the sum of probabilities on branches from one point?', '1.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Conditional Probability', 'What does "given that" signal in a probability question?', 'Conditional probability: the sample space is restricted to the given event.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Normal Distribution', 'What does X ~ N(μ, σ²) mean, and what is the commonest error?', 'X is Normally distributed with mean μ and VARIANCE σ². Reading the second parameter as the standard deviation is the usual slip — N(100, 25) has σ = 5.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Normal Distribution', 'Name four properties of the Normal curve.', 'Symmetric about the mean, mean = median = mode, total area 1 with area representing probability, points of inflection at μ ± σ.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Normal Distribution', 'How do you standardise a Normal variable?', 'Z = (X − μ)/σ, giving the standard Normal distribution N(0, 1).', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Normal Distribution', 'If X ~ N(μ, σ²) and Y = aX + b, what is the distribution of Y?', 'Also Normal, with mean aμ + b and variance a²σ². The multiplier is SQUARED in the variance, b affects the mean only.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Normal Distribution', 'What is a continuity correction and why is it needed?', 'A half-unit adjustment when a continuous Normal distribution models discrete data. P(X ≥ 12) becomes P(X > 11.5). P(X ≤ 10) becomes P(X < 10.5).', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Normal Distribution', 'What is the mean and variance of Z ~ N(0, 1)?', 'Mean 0 and variance 1.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Normal Distribution', 'If X ~ N(50, 16), find P(X < 54).', 'Z = (54 − 50)/4 = 1: P(Z < 1) ≈ 0.8413.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Normal Distribution', 'What is the inverse Normal used for?', 'To find the value of x corresponding to a given probability.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Normal Distribution', 'When can a binomial B(n, p) be approximated by a Normal?', 'When n is large and p is close to 0.5 (np and n(1 − p) both greater than about 5).', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Normal Distribution', 'What Normal approximation is used for B(n, p)?', 'N(np, np(1 − p)).', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Normal Distribution', 'Roughly what proportion lies within one and two standard deviations of the mean?', 'About 68% within one, and about 95% within two.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Normal Distribution', 'What is the probability of a single value for a continuous distribution?', 'Zero; probabilities are only found for intervals.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Normal Distribution', 'What is the shape of the Normal distribution?', 'Symmetrical and bell-shaped, centred on the mean.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Normal Distribution', 'Where are the points of inflection on a Normal curve?', 'At μ ± σ.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Normal Distribution', 'What proportion of data lies within 3 standard deviations of the mean?', 'About 99.7%.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Normal Distribution', 'What is Z?', 'The standard Normal variable, Z ~ N(0, 1).', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Normal Distribution', 'If Z ~ N(0, 1), what is P(Z < 0)?', '0.5.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Normal Distribution', 'If X ~ N(20, 9), find P(X > 23).', 'Z = (23 − 20)/3 = 1: P(Z > 1) ≈ 0.1587.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Normal Distribution', 'If X ~ N(μ, 4) and P(X < 10) = 0.8413, find μ.', 'z = 1 = (10 − μ)/2, so μ = 8.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Normal Distribution', 'How do you find an unknown σ?', 'Use the inverse Normal to find z, then solve z = (x − μ)/σ.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Normal Distribution', 'If X ~ N(100, 15²), find x such that P(X < x) = 0.95.', 'z = 1.645, so x = 100 + 1.645 × 15 ≈ 124.7.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Normal Distribution', 'Why is the Normal distribution a good model?', 'Many natural measurements (heights, errors) are roughly symmetrical and bell-shaped.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Normal Distribution', 'When is the Normal distribution not a good model?', 'When the data are skewed or can''t take negative values near zero.', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Normal Distribution', 'What is P(X = 5) for a continuous Normal distribution?', '0.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Normal Distribution', 'What continuity correction is used for P(X ≤ 10) when approximating a binomial?', 'P(Y < 10.5).', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Normal Distribution', 'What continuity correction is used for P(X > 10)?', 'P(Y > 10.5).', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Normal Distribution', 'What continuity correction is used for P(X = 10)?', 'P(9.5 < Y < 10.5).', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Normal Distribution', 'How is the Normal used to test a population mean?', 'Use X̄ ~ N(μ, σ²/n) and compare the test statistic with critical values.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Normal Distribution', 'What is the effect of increasing σ on the Normal curve?', 'It becomes wider and flatter.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Normal Distribution', 'What is the effect of increasing μ on the Normal curve?', 'It moves to the right without changing shape.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Modelling in Mechanics', 'What does modelling an object as a particle mean?', 'It has mass but negligible size, so all forces act at a single point and rotation is ignored.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Modelling in Mechanics', 'What does ''light'' mean, and what does it let you deduce?', 'Negligible mass. For a string it means the tension is the same throughout its length.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Modelling in Mechanics', 'What does ''smooth'' mean for a surface or a pulley?', 'No friction. For a pulley it means the tension is unchanged across it, so it is equal on both sides.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Modelling in Mechanics', 'What does ''inextensible'' mean, and why does it matter?', 'The string cannot stretch, so connected particles have accelerations of EQUAL MAGNITUDE — which is what lets you use one value of a.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Modelling in Mechanics', 'What does ''uniform'' mean, and how is it used?', 'Mass is evenly distributed, so the weight acts at the geometric centre — the midpoint for a rod.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Modelling in Mechanics', 'What does ''rigid'' mean?', 'The body does not bend or deform under load, so its shape is fixed.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Modelling in Mechanics', 'Name the three SI base quantities used in mechanics and their units.', 'Length in metres (m), time in seconds (s), mass in kilograms (kg).', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Modelling in Mechanics', 'Give the units of velocity, acceleration, force and moment.', 'm s⁻¹, m s⁻², newton (N), newton metre (N m).', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Modelling in Mechanics', 'Express the newton in base units.', '1 N = 1 kg m s⁻², from F = ma.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Modelling in Mechanics', 'Why is a moment quoted in N m rather than joules?', 'Although the base units coincide, the units are kept distinct to distinguish a turning effect from an energy.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Modelling in Mechanics', 'What is the difference between mass and weight?', 'Mass is a scalar in kilograms and is a property of the object, weight is the force of gravity on it, W = mg, in newtons, and depends on the local value of g.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Modelling in Mechanics', 'What happens to mass and weight on the Moon?', 'The mass is unchanged, the weight decreases, because g is smaller there.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Modelling in Mechanics', 'Convert 36 km h⁻¹ to m s⁻¹.', '36000 m ÷ 3600 s = 10 m s⁻¹. Conversely 5 m s⁻¹ is 18 km h⁻¹.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Modelling in Mechanics', 'When is the particle model inappropriate?', 'When size or rotation matters — a ladder against a wall, a spinning ball, or a cable whose length is the point of the problem.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Modelling in Mechanics', 'What is a mathematical model?', 'A simplified representation of a real-world situation using mathematics.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Modelling in Mechanics', 'What is a modelling assumption?', 'A simplification made to make a model easier to use, e.g. ignoring air resistance.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Modelling in Mechanics', 'Why do we ignore air resistance in many models?', 'It simplifies calculations; the effect may be small for dense, slow objects.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Modelling in Mechanics', 'What does modelling an object as a lamina mean?', 'Treating it as two-dimensional with negligible thickness.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Modelling in Mechanics', 'What does modelling a surface as rough mean?', 'Friction acts between the surface and an object in contact with it.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Modelling in Mechanics', 'What does modelling a rope as a light string mean?', 'Its mass is negligible, so tension is the same throughout.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Modelling in Mechanics', 'What is a peg?', 'A fixed support, usually modelled as smooth, over which a string can pass.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Modelling in Mechanics', 'What is a bead?', 'A particle with a hole threaded on a wire or string.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Modelling in Mechanics', 'What is a rod?', 'A long, thin rigid body, usually modelled as having negligible thickness.', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Modelling in Mechanics', 'What is the unit of mass?', 'Kilogram (kg).', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Modelling in Mechanics', 'What is the unit of force?', 'Newton (N).', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Modelling in Mechanics', 'What is the unit of acceleration?', 'Metres per second squared (m s⁻²).', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Modelling in Mechanics', 'How do you convert 72 km h⁻¹ to m s⁻¹?', '72 × 1000 ÷ 3600 = 20 m s⁻¹.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Modelling in Mechanics', 'What is weight?', 'The force due to gravity on an object: W = mg.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Modelling in Mechanics', 'What does modelling a ball as a particle ignore?', 'Its size and rotation.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Modelling in Mechanics', 'What does modelling a car as a particle assume?', 'All its mass acts at a single point, so its dimensions don''t matter.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Kinematics (SUVAT)', 'What is the difference between distance travelled and displacement?', 'Distance travelled is the total path length, a scalar, displacement is the net change in position, a vector.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Kinematics (SUVAT)', 'What is the difference between speed and velocity?', 'Speed is the magnitude of velocity, so it is a scalar and never negative, velocity carries direction.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Kinematics (SUVAT)', 'Give the formulae for average speed and average velocity.', 'Average speed = distance travelled ÷ time, average velocity = overall displacement ÷ time. A there-and-back journey has zero average velocity.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Kinematics (SUVAT)', 'What do the gradient and area of a velocity-time graph give?', 'Gradient gives acceleration, area gives displacement.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Kinematics (SUVAT)', 'What does the gradient of a displacement-time graph give?', 'Velocity.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Kinematics (SUVAT)', 'What does area below the time axis on a velocity-time graph mean?', 'Displacement in the negative direction. For DISTANCE travelled, add its magnitude rather than subtracting.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Kinematics (SUVAT)', 'Write down the five constant acceleration formulae.', 'v = u + at, s = ut + ½at², s = vt − ½at², s = ½(u + v)t, v² = u² + 2as.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Kinematics (SUVAT)', 'When may the suvat formulae be used?', 'Only when the acceleration is CONSTANT. If acceleration varies with time, calculus is required.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Kinematics (SUVAT)', 'How do you choose which suvat formula to use?', 'Each omits one of the five quantities. Pick the one omitting the quantity you neither know nor want.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Kinematics (SUVAT)', 'A particle starts from rest and accelerates at 2 m s⁻² for 5 s. Find v and s.', 'v = 10 m s⁻¹ and s = 25 m.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Kinematics (SUVAT)', 'Why must you fix a positive direction in a vertical motion problem?', 'Displacement, velocity and acceleration are all signed. If upwards is positive then a = −9.8 THROUGHOUT — including at the top and on the way down.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Kinematics (SUVAT)', 'At the top of a vertical throw, what is zero — the velocity or the acceleration?', 'The velocity. The acceleration is still g downwards, which is why the body does not stay there.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Kinematics (SUVAT)', 'State the calculus relationships for kinematics.', 'v = ds/dt and a = dv/dt, conversely v = ∫a dt and s = ∫v dt, each with a constant fixed by initial conditions.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Kinematics (SUVAT)', 'How do you find distance travelled when the velocity changes sign?', 'Find the times where v = 0, split the interval there, and add the MAGNITUDES of the separate displacements.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Kinematics (SUVAT)', 'Why does integrating velocity straight through give displacement, not distance?', 'Negative velocity contributes negatively, so the parts partially cancel, giving the net change rather than the total path length.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Kinematics (SUVAT)', 'What is relative velocity in one dimension?', 'The velocity of A relative to B is v_A − v_B. Two cars at 30 and 20 m s⁻¹ close the gap at only 10 m s⁻¹.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Kinematics (SUVAT)', 'What is acceleration?', 'The rate of change of velocity.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Kinematics (SUVAT)', 'What does a horizontal line on a velocity–time graph show?', 'Constant velocity (zero acceleration).', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Kinematics (SUVAT)', 'What does a straight line with positive gradient on a velocity–time graph show?', 'Constant acceleration.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Kinematics (SUVAT)', 'What does a horizontal line on a displacement–time graph show?', 'The object is stationary.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Kinematics (SUVAT)', 'A car decelerates from 20 m s⁻¹ to rest in 5 s. What is its acceleration?', 'a = (0 − 20)/5 = −4 m s⁻².', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Kinematics (SUVAT)', 'A ball is dropped from 20 m. How long does it take to hit the ground (g = 9.8)?', 's = ½gt²: 20 = 4.9t², t ≈ 2.02 s.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Kinematics (SUVAT)', 'What is the acceleration of an object in free fall?', 'g ≈ 9.8 m s⁻² downwards (ignoring air resistance).', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Kinematics (SUVAT)', 'Which suvat equation doesn''t include time?', 'v² = u² + 2as.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Kinematics (SUVAT)', 'Which suvat equation doesn''t include final velocity?', 's = ut + ½at².', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Kinematics (SUVAT)', 'Which suvat equation doesn''t include acceleration?', 's = ½(u + v)t.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Kinematics (SUVAT)', 'If v = 3t² − 2t, find the acceleration at t = 2.', 'a = dv/dt = 6t − 2 = 10 m s⁻².', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Kinematics (SUVAT)', 'If a = 6t and v = 0 at t = 0, find v.', 'v = ∫6t dt = 3t² + c; c = 0, so v = 3t².', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Kinematics (SUVAT)', 'What does a curved displacement–time graph show?', 'The velocity is changing (acceleration).', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Kinematics (SUVAT)', 'How is the distance found from a velocity–time graph with negative sections?', 'Add the magnitudes of the areas above and below the axis.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Forces & Newton''s Laws', 'State Newton''s first law.', 'An object continues at rest or in uniform motion in a straight line unless acted on by a resultant force.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Forces & Newton''s Laws', 'State Newton''s second law.', 'A resultant force F on an object of fixed mass m gives acceleration a with F = ma.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Forces & Newton''s Laws', 'State Newton''s third law.', 'When one object exerts a force on another, there is a reaction equal in magnitude and opposite in direction.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Forces & Newton''s Laws', 'What must the F in F = ma be?', 'The RESULTANT of all forces acting. Using a single force and ignoring friction or weight is the standard structural error.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Forces & Newton''s Laws', 'How do you write down an equation of motion?', 'Draw a force diagram, choose a positive direction (usually that of the acceleration), resolve in that direction and set the total equal to ma.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Forces & Newton''s Laws', 'How is equilibrium a special case of the second law?', 'Setting a = 0 gives a resultant force of zero — which also recovers the first law.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Forces & Newton''s Laws', 'Why is the second law a vector equation?', 'Both force and acceleration are vectors, so it holds independently in each direction and may be applied component by component.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Forces & Newton''s Laws', 'For a person in a lift accelerating upwards at a, what is the normal reaction?', 'R − mg = ma, so R = m(g + a) — greater than the weight, which is why they feel heavier.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Forces & Newton''s Laws', 'A lift descends and decelerates. Is the reaction more or less than the weight?', 'More. Decelerating while descending means the acceleration is directed UPWARDS. Read the acceleration, not the direction of travel.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Forces & Newton''s Laws', 'Why are a book''s weight and the table''s reaction NOT a third-law pair?', 'Both act on the BOOK. A third-law pair acts on two different bodies, the true partner of the weight is the book''s pull on the Earth.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Forces & Newton''s Laws', 'What is the simple test for a third-law pair?', 'If the two forces act on the same object, they cannot be a pair.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Forces & Newton''s Laws', 'What two assumptions make a pulley problem solvable?', 'A light inextensible string gives equal tension throughout and equal magnitudes of acceleration, a smooth pulley leaves the tension unchanged across it.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Forces & Newton''s Laws', 'When do you use the whole system, and when individual particles?', 'Whole system for the ACCELERATION, since internal tensions cancel. Individual particles for the TENSION or coupling force, which then appears explicitly.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Forces & Newton''s Laws', 'For masses m₁ > m₂ over a smooth pulley, what is the acceleration?', 'a = (m₁ − m₂)g/(m₁ + m₂). For 5 kg and 3 kg with g = 9.8 this is 2.45 m s⁻², and T = 36.75 N.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Forces & Newton''s Laws', 'How can you check a pulley tension is plausible?', 'It must lie BETWEEN the two weights — less than the heavier, more than the lighter.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Forces & Newton''s Laws', 'What does Newton''s first law tell us about an object at constant velocity?', 'The resultant force on it is zero.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Forces & Newton''s Laws', 'Find the acceleration of a 2 kg mass acted on by a resultant force of 10 N.', 'a = F/m = 5 m s⁻².', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Forces & Newton''s Laws', 'A 1000 kg car accelerates at 2 m s⁻² against 400 N resistance. What is the driving force?', 'F − 400 = 1000 × 2, so F = 2400 N.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Forces & Newton''s Laws', 'What is the tension in a string holding a 3 kg mass at rest?', 'T = mg = 29.4 N.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Forces & Newton''s Laws', 'What happens to the reaction in a lift moving at constant velocity?', 'R = mg (no acceleration).', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Forces & Newton''s Laws', 'What is a connected particle problem?', 'Two or more particles connected by strings or rods, moving together with the same acceleration magnitude.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Forces & Newton''s Laws', 'For a car towing a trailer, what is the tension in the towbar?', 'Apply F = ma to the trailer alone: T − resistance on trailer = m_trailer × a.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Forces & Newton''s Laws', 'What is the equation of motion for a particle on a smooth slope?', 'mg sin θ = ma, so a = g sin θ.', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Forces & Newton''s Laws', 'What is the equation of motion on a rough slope for a particle sliding down?', 'mg sin θ − μmg cos θ = ma.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Forces & Newton''s Laws', 'What is the tension in a string over a smooth pulley connecting masses m₁ and m₂?', 'T = 2m₁m₂g/(m₁ + m₂).', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Forces & Newton''s Laws', 'What is an internal force?', 'A force between parts of the system, such as tension, which cancels when considering the whole system.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Forces & Newton''s Laws', 'What is an external force?', 'A force acting on the system from outside, such as weight or friction.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Forces & Newton''s Laws', 'Why is the acceleration the same for connected particles?', 'The string is inextensible, so both particles move the same distance in the same time.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Forces & Newton''s Laws', 'What does Newton''s third law say about the force between a towbar and a car?', 'The towbar pulls the trailer forward and the trailer pulls the car backward with an equal force.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Forces & Newton''s Laws', 'Two particles of 3 kg and 2 kg are connected over a smooth pulley. Find their acceleration (g = 9.8).', 'a = (3 − 2)g/(3 + 2) = 1.96 m/s².', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Moments', 'Why does the point of application matter for a rigid body but not a particle?', 'A particle has no size, so forces only translate it. A rigid body has extent, so forces can also produce a turning effect.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Moments', 'Define the moment of a force and give its units.', 'Moment = force × perpendicular distance from the point. Units are newton metres, N m.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Moments', 'What is the moment of a force acting through the pivot?', 'Zero, since the perpendicular distance is zero. This is why choosing the pivot cleverly removes unknowns.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Moments', 'State the two conditions for a rigid body to be in equilibrium.', 'The resultant force is zero, AND the sum of the moments about any one point is zero.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Moments', 'Why is the force condition alone insufficient?', 'Two equal and opposite forces applied at different points give no net force but do produce a turning effect.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Moments', 'About which point may moments be taken?', 'Any point, once the body is in equilibrium — so choose the most convenient.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Moments', 'What is the standard pivot-choosing technique?', 'Take moments about a point through which an UNKNOWN force acts, so that force has zero moment and drops out of the equation.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Moments', 'Where does the weight of a body act?', 'Through its centre of mass — the midpoint for a uniform rod, the intersection of the diagonals for a uniform rectangular lamina.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Moments', 'Where is the centre of mass of a NON-uniform rod?', 'Not at the midpoint. It must be given, found by symmetry, or found by taking moments.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Moments', 'A non-uniform rod AB, 4 m long, weight 50 N, rests on supports at A and B with R_A = 20 N. Where is the centre of mass?', 'R_B = 30 N. Moments about A: 50x = 30 × 4 = 120, so x = 2.4 m from A.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Moments', 'How can you sanity-check the position of a centre of mass?', 'It lies closer to the support with the LARGER reaction.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Moments', 'What is the condition for a body to be on the point of tipping?', 'The reaction at the OTHER support falls to zero, so all the weight is carried by the support about which it tips.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Moments', 'How many unknowns can be found for a body in equilibrium under vertical forces?', 'At most two, since only two independent equations are available — one from resolving and one from moments.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Moments', 'How do you fix the sense of each moment in an equation?', 'Choose one sense as positive, usually anticlockwise, and give every moment that sign or its opposite. In equilibrium the total clockwise moment about a point equals the total anticlockwise moment.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Moments', 'What is a moment?', 'The turning effect of a force about a point: force × perpendicular distance.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Moments', 'What is the principle of moments?', 'For a body in equilibrium, the sum of clockwise moments equals the sum of anticlockwise moments about any point.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Moments', 'Find the moment of a 10 N force acting 3 m from a pivot at right angles.', '10 × 3 = 30 N m.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Moments', 'Where is the centre of mass of a uniform rod of length 4 m?', 'At its midpoint, 2 m from each end.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Moments', 'A uniform 6 m beam of weight 100 N rests on supports at each end. What is each reaction?', 'By symmetry, 50 N each.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Moments', 'A uniform beam is about to tip about support B. What is the reaction at the other support A?', 'Zero.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Moments', 'How do you find the reaction forces on a beam?', 'Take moments about one support to find the other reaction, then resolve vertically.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Moments', 'What is the moment of a force at an angle θ to a rod?', 'F × d sin θ, where d is the distance along the rod from the pivot.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Moments', 'Why take moments about a point where an unknown force acts?', 'That force has zero moment about the point, eliminating it from the equation.', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Moments', 'What does a clockwise and anticlockwise sign convention mean?', 'One direction is taken as positive and the other as negative when summing moments.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Moments', 'What is a couple?', 'Two equal and opposite parallel forces that produce a turning effect but no resultant force.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Moments', 'What happens when a person stands at the end of a plank on two supports?', 'The reactions change; if the person is beyond a support, the plank may tip.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Moments', 'What is a uniform lamina''s centre of mass?', 'At its geometric centre (centroid) for a symmetrical shape.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Moments', 'Why must forces be resolved vertically as well as taking moments?', 'Both conditions (zero resultant force and zero resultant moment) are needed for equilibrium.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Moments', 'A uniform rod of length 2 m and weight 40 N is pivoted at one end. What moment does its weight produce?', '40 × 1 = 40 N m about the pivot.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Moments', 'What is a lamina?', 'A flat object with negligible thickness.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Variable Acceleration', 'How are velocity and acceleration obtained from a position vector?', 'By differentiating with respect to t, component by component. Integrating reverses the process.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Variable Acceleration', 'Why can vector calculus be done component by component?', 'The i and j directions are fixed and independent of time, so each component is an independent function of t.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Variable Acceleration', 'What is the speed of a particle with velocity v?', '|v|, the magnitude — a scalar. Quoting a speed as a vector loses the mark.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Variable Acceleration', 'If r = 3t i + t² j, what are v and a?', 'v = 3i + 2t j and a = 2j. At t = 2 the speed is |3i + 4j| = 5.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Variable Acceleration', 'Why is a VECTOR constant of integration needed?', 'Each component has its own arbitrary constant, together they form a constant vector fixed by the initial conditions in both directions.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Variable Acceleration', 'Write down the vector forms of the suvat formulae.', 'v = u + at, s = ut + ½at², s = vt − ½at², s = ½(u + v)t — all valid for constant acceleration.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Variable Acceleration', 'Why is the change in velocity always parallel to the acceleration?', 'From v = u + at, the change is at, a scalar multiple of a.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Variable Acceleration', 'What is the path of a particle with zero acceleration?', 'A straight line travelled at constant velocity.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Variable Acceleration', 'What is the condition for two particles to collide?', 'Their position vectors must be EQUAL AT THE SAME INSTANT — both components must give the same value of t.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Variable Acceleration', 'Why is checking one component not enough for a collision?', 'If the components give different times, the paths cross but the particles are never there together.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Variable Acceleration', 'How do you find when two particles are closest?', 'Write the relative position vector as a function of t, then minimise its magnitude.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Variable Acceleration', 'How do you find the Cartesian equation of a path?', 'Write the components as x and y in terms of t and eliminate t, rearranging the simpler equation first.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Variable Acceleration', 'For r = 3t i + t² j, what is the Cartesian equation?', 'x = 3t gives t = x/3, so y = x²/9 — a parabola.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Variable Acceleration', 'What is the difference between distance from the origin and distance travelled?', 'Distance from the origin is |r|, distance travelled is the length of the path, generally greater.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Variable Acceleration', 'What extra unit vector appears in three dimensions?', 'k, along the z-axis. Magnitudes then involve the sum of three squares.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Variable Acceleration', 'What is a velocity vector?', 'A vector giving the speed and direction of motion.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Variable Acceleration', 'If r = (2t + 1)i + (t² − 3)j, find the velocity at t = 1.', 'v = 2i + 2tj; at t = 1, v = 2i + 2j.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Variable Acceleration', 'How do you find the direction of motion from a velocity vector?', 'Use the angle θ = arctan(vᵧ/vₓ), taking care with the quadrant.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Variable Acceleration', 'When is a particle moving parallel to the i-direction?', 'When the j-component of velocity is zero.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Variable Acceleration', 'How do you find the position vector from a velocity vector?', 'Integrate each component with respect to time and add a vector constant.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Variable Acceleration', 'If a = 2i − j, u = 3i + j, find v at t = 2.', 'v = u + at = 3i + j + 4i − 2j = 7i − j.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Variable Acceleration', 'Find the displacement using s = ut + ½at² with u = i, a = 2j, t = 3.', 's = 3i + ½ × 2j × 9 = 3i + 9j.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Variable Acceleration', 'What does r = r₀ + vt describe?', 'The position of a particle moving with constant velocity v from position r₀.', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Variable Acceleration', 'What is relative velocity in 2D?', 'The velocity of one object as seen from another: v_A − v_B.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Variable Acceleration', 'When is a particle at the origin?', 'When both components of its position vector are zero at the same time.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Variable Acceleration', 'How do you find the time when a particle is due north of the origin?', 'Set the i-component (east) of the position vector to zero, with the j-component positive.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Variable Acceleration', 'How do you find the speed at time t?', 'Find the magnitude of the velocity vector.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Variable Acceleration', 'How do you show that acceleration is constant?', 'Show that the acceleration vector doesn''t depend on t.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Variable Acceleration', 'What is the distance between two particles at time t?', 'The magnitude of the difference between their position vectors.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Variable Acceleration', 'If s = t³ − 6t², find when the particle is instantaneously at rest.', 'v = 3t² − 12t = 0, so t = 0 or t = 4.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Projectiles', 'State the four standard projectile modelling assumptions.', 'No air resistance, the projectile is a particle, gravity always acts in the same direction, gravity is constant in magnitude.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Projectiles', 'What is the central idea that makes projectile problems work?', 'Horizontal and vertical motion are INDEPENDENT. Horizontally there is no acceleration, vertically the acceleration is g downwards. Time links them.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Projectiles', 'Give the initial velocity components for launch at speed u and angle θ.', 'Horizontal u cos θ, vertical u sin θ.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Projectiles', 'Give the displacement components at time t.', 'Horizontal u cos θ × t, vertical u sin θ × t − ½gt².', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Projectiles', 'Write down the time of flight, maximum height and range on level ground.', 'T = 2u sin θ/g. H = u²sin²θ/(2g). R = u² sin 2θ/g.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Projectiles', 'At what angle is the range greatest, and why?', '45°, because R depends on sin 2θ, which is greatest when 2θ = 90°. Maximum range is u²/g.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Projectiles', 'Why do two angles give the same range?', 'Because sin 2θ = sin(180° − 2θ), so θ and 90° − θ give equal ranges. The partner of 25° is 65°.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Projectiles', 'What is the velocity at the highest point?', 'Horizontal, of magnitude u cos θ — NOT zero. Only the vertical component vanishes.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Projectiles', 'Why do a horizontally thrown ball and a dropped ball land together?', 'Both have zero initial vertical velocity, and horizontal motion does not affect the vertical fall.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Projectiles', 'Derive the Cartesian equation of a projectile path.', 'From x = u cos θ t, t = x/(u cos θ). Substituting gives y = x tan θ − gx²/(2u²cos²θ) — a downward parabola.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Projectiles', 'How do you check a projectile clears an obstacle?', 'Substitute the obstacle''s horizontal distance into the path equation and confirm y exceeds its height. Checking the range or maximum height alone is NOT sufficient.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Projectiles', 'How do you handle projection from a height h above the landing point?', 'Take upwards as positive, the vertical displacement on landing is −h. Solve −h = u sin θ t − ½gt² and take the positive root.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Projectiles', 'Why does a projectile pass a given height twice?', 'The vertical displacement equation is a quadratic in t, with two roots — once rising and once falling.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Projectiles', 'What is the speed of a projectile at time t?', '√((u cos θ)² + (u sin θ − gt)²), combining the constant horizontal and changing vertical components.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Projectiles', 'For level ground, how does the landing speed compare with the launch speed?', 'They are equal, and the landing angle below the horizontal equals the projection angle above it.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Projectiles', 'What is a projectile?', 'An object projected into the air that moves under gravity alone (in the simple model).', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Projectiles', 'What is the horizontal acceleration of a projectile?', 'Zero (ignoring air resistance).', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Projectiles', 'What is the vertical acceleration of a projectile?', 'g downwards.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Projectiles', 'A ball is thrown horizontally at 10 m s⁻¹ from 5 m high. How long until it lands (g = 9.8)?', '5 = 4.9t², so t ≈ 1.01 s.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Projectiles', 'How far does that ball travel horizontally?', '10 × 1.01 ≈ 10.1 m.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Projectiles', 'What is the time to reach maximum height?', 't = u sin θ/g.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Projectiles', 'What is the maximum height of a projectile?', 'H = u² sin²θ/(2g).', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Projectiles', 'What is the range on horizontal ground?', 'R = u² sin 2θ/g.', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Projectiles', 'What is the maximum range for a given launch speed?', 'u²/g, at θ = 45°.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Projectiles', 'What is the equation of a projectile''s path?', 'y = x tan θ − gx²/(2u² cos²θ).', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Projectiles', 'What is the direction of motion at time t?', 'The angle arctan(vᵧ/vₓ) with the horizontal.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Projectiles', 'When is the speed of a projectile least?', 'At the highest point, where it equals u cos θ.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Projectiles', 'What happens to the time of flight if the launch speed doubles?', 'It doubles (T = 2u sin θ/g).', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Projectiles', 'What happens to the range if the launch speed doubles?', 'It quadruples (R ∝ u²).', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Projectiles', 'What is the time of flight for a projectile launched horizontally from height h?', 't = √(2h/g).', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M7', 'Further Forces', 'Name four common forces and their directions.', 'Weight acts vertically downwards, normal reaction perpendicular to the surface, tension along a string pulling inwards, friction along the surface opposing motion.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M7', 'Further Forces', 'What is the difference between tension and thrust?', 'Tension pulls the ends together, thrust pushes them apart. A string can only pull, a rod can do either.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M7', 'Further Forces', 'Is the normal reaction always equal to the weight?', 'No. It depends on the other forces: pressing down increases it, pulling upwards at an angle reduces it, and on a slope it is mg cos θ.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M7', 'Further Forces', 'Give the components of weight on a plane inclined at θ.', 'mg sin θ along the plane and mg cos θ perpendicular to it. Check with θ = 0: sin 0 = 0, so the along-plane component vanishes as it should.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M7', 'Further Forces', 'Why resolve parallel and perpendicular to an inclined plane?', 'It puts the normal reaction on one axis and the friction on the other, so each equation contains fewer unknowns.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M7', 'Further Forces', 'State the condition for a particle to be in equilibrium.', 'The resultant of the forces is zero. Equivalently, resolving in ANY direction gives zero.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M7', 'Further Forces', 'Does equilibrium mean at rest?', 'No. Constant velocity also gives zero acceleration and therefore zero resultant force.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M7', 'Further Forces', 'What shape do forces in equilibrium form when drawn head to tail?', 'A closed polygon — a closed triangle for three forces.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M7', 'Further Forces', 'State the friction inequality.', 'F ≤ μR. Friction takes whatever value prevents motion, up to a maximum of μR.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M7', 'Further Forces', 'When can you use F = μR?', 'Only when the body is moving or ON THE POINT of moving (limiting friction). Otherwise friction simply equals the applied force.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M7', 'Further Forces', 'What are the units of the coefficient of friction?', 'None — it is a ratio of two forces, so the units cancel.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M7', 'Further Forces', 'State the condition for a block to remain at rest on a rough slope.', 'tan θ ≤ μ. Note it is independent of the mass, which cancels.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M7', 'Further Forces', 'Which way does friction act on a block in limiting equilibrium on a slope?', 'UP the plane, opposing the tendency to slide down.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M7', 'Further Forces', 'Why is pulling a case easier than pushing it down at an angle?', 'Pulling upwards at an angle reduces the normal reaction and hence the maximum friction, pushing downwards increases both.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M7', 'Further Forces', 'What is the acceleration of a block released on a SMOOTH plane inclined at θ?', 'a = g sin θ, since there is no friction. At 30° with g = 9.8 this is 4.9 m s⁻².', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M7', 'Further Forces', 'What is a resultant force?', 'The single force equivalent to all the forces acting on an object.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M7', 'Further Forces', 'How do you find the resultant of two perpendicular forces?', 'Use Pythagoras for the magnitude and trigonometry for the direction.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M7', 'Further Forces', 'What is limiting friction?', 'The maximum friction force, F = μR, reached when an object is on the point of moving.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M7', 'Further Forces', 'What happens to friction when an object is moving?', 'It takes its maximum value F = μR and acts opposite to the direction of motion.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M7', 'Further Forces', 'What is the normal reaction on a horizontal surface with no other vertical forces?', 'R = mg.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M7', 'Further Forces', 'What is the normal reaction on an inclined plane?', 'R = mg cos θ (with no other forces perpendicular to the plane).', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M7', 'Further Forces', 'A 5 kg block rests on a rough horizontal surface with μ = 0.4. What is the maximum friction?', 'F = 0.4 × 5 × 9.8 = 19.6 N.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M7', 'Further Forces', 'What is the condition for a block to slide down a slope under gravity alone?', 'tan θ > μ.', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M7', 'Further Forces', 'What is the angle of friction?', 'The angle λ where tan λ = μ.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M7', 'Further Forces', 'What is thrust?', 'A pushing force in a rod or strut.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M7', 'Further Forces', 'What is tension?', 'A pulling force in a string, rope or rod.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M7', 'Further Forces', 'What does "on the point of moving" mean?', 'Friction is limiting and the object is still in equilibrium.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M7', 'Further Forces', 'How do you resolve forces in equilibrium?', 'Resolve in two perpendicular directions and set the sum of components in each direction to zero.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M7', 'Further Forces', 'What is Lami''s theorem?', 'For three forces in equilibrium, each force is proportional to the sine of the angle between the other two. (Resolving is the usual method.)', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'M7', 'Further Forces', 'A 4 kg box is pulled along rough ground (μ = 0.3) by a horizontal 20 N force. Find its acceleration (g = 9.8).', 'F = 0.3 × 4 × 9.8 = 11.76 N; a = (20 − 11.76)/4 ≈ 2.06 m/s².', 30)
),
fresh AS (
  SELECT v.* FROM v
  WHERE NOT EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM flashcards f
    WHERE f.subject = v.subject AND f.exam_board = v.exam_board AND f.level = v.level
      AND f.topic_id = v.topic_id AND f.front = v.front)
),
cur AS (
  SELECT subject, exam_board, level, topic_id, count(*) AS n, max(card_order) AS mx
  FROM flashcards
  WHERE subject = 'Mathematics' AND exam_board = 'Edexcel' AND level = 'alevel'
  GROUP BY 1, 2, 3, 4
),
ranked AS (
  SELECT fr.*, coalesce(c.n, 0) AS n, coalesce(c.mx, 0) AS mx,
         row_number() OVER (PARTITION BY fr.topic_id ORDER BY fr.ord) AS rn
  FROM fresh fr
  LEFT JOIN cur c USING (subject, exam_board, level, topic_id)
)
INSERT INTO flashcards (subject, exam_board, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, card_order, level)
SELECT subject, exam_board, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, mx + rn, level
FROM ranked
WHERE n + rn <= 30;

-- Flashcard top-up: Mathematics|OCR|alevel — brings each topic up to 30 cards.
-- Generated by scripts/build-flashcard-topup.js from flashcards/topup/src/maths-ocr-alevel.txt
-- Safe to re-run: skips fronts already present and never takes a topic past 30.
WITH v(subject, exam_board, level, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, ord) AS (VALUES
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.1', 'Proof', 'What is proof by deduction?', 'Proceeding from given assumptions through a series of logical steps to a conclusion.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.1', 'Proof', 'What is proof by exhaustion?', 'Establishing a result by checking every case of a finite set of cases, having first shown the cases cover all possibilities.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.1', 'Proof', 'What is proof by contradiction?', 'Assume the statement is false, reason correctly from that assumption, and derive an impossibility. The assumption must therefore be wrong.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.1', 'Proof', 'How do you disprove a general statement?', 'Give a single counter-example — one case for which the statement fails. One example never proves a general statement, but one counter-example always disproves it.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.1', 'Proof', 'What does P ⇒ Q mean, and how does it differ from P ⇔ Q?', 'P ⇒ Q means P implies Q. P ⇔ Q means each implies the other. Writing ⇔ requires both directions to hold.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.1', 'Proof', 'What is the contrapositive of ''if P then Q'', and why is it useful?', '''If not Q then not P''. It is logically equivalent to the original, so proving it proves the original — often easier than a direct argument.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.1', 'Proof', 'What is the converse of ''if P then Q''? Is it always true?', '''If Q then P''. It is a different statement and need not be true, confusing the two is a common error.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.1', 'Proof', 'Give the counter-example that disproves ''every prime is odd''.', '2 is prime and even.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.1', 'Proof', 'Give the counter-example that disproves ''n² − n + 41 is prime for every positive integer n''.', 'n = 41, where the expression equals 41², divisible by 41. It does give primes for n = 1 to 40, which is why testing values is not proof.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.1', 'Proof', 'Outline the proof that √2 is irrational.', 'Assume √2 = p/q in lowest terms. Then p² = 2q², so p is even, writing p = 2k gives q² = 2k², so q is even. Both even contradicts lowest terms.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.1', 'Proof', 'Why is ''in its lowest terms'' essential to the √2 proof?', 'It is the assumption the final contradiction violates. Without it, showing p and q are both even contradicts nothing and the proof fails.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.1', 'Proof', 'Why can the √2 argument not be copied to prove √4 irrational?', 'It would need ''p² divisible by 4 ⇒ p divisible by 4'', which is false (take p = 2). The proof breaks exactly where it must, since √4 = 2 is rational.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.1', 'Proof', 'Outline Euclid''s proof that there are infinitely many primes.', 'Assume a finite list p₁…pₙ and form N = p₁p₂⋯pₙ + 1. No prime in the list divides N, so N is prime or has a prime factor outside the list — contradicting completeness.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.1', 'Proof', 'In Euclid''s proof, must N = p₁⋯pₙ + 1 be prime?', 'No. It need only have no factor in the list. With 2,3,5,7,11,13 you get 30031 = 59 × 509 — composite, but both factors lie outside the list.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.1', 'Proof', 'What is the negation of ''all swans are white''?', '''There exists a swan that is not white.'' It is not ''no swans are white'', which is a much stronger claim.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.1', 'Proof', 'What is a conjecture?', 'A statement believed to be true but not yet proved.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.1', 'Proof', 'What does "if and only if" require you to prove?', 'Both directions: P ⇒ Q and Q ⇒ P.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.1', 'Proof', 'Prove that the sum of two odd numbers is even.', 'Let the odd numbers be 2m + 1 and 2n + 1. Their sum is 2m + 2n + 2 = 2(m + n + 1), which is even.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.1', 'Proof', 'How do you represent an even and an odd integer algebraically?', 'Even: 2n. Odd: 2n + 1 (or 2n − 1), where n is an integer.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.1', 'Proof', 'How do you represent consecutive integers?', 'n, n + 1, n + 2, … where n is an integer.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.1', 'Proof', 'Prove that the square of any odd number is odd.', '(2n + 1)² = 4n² + 4n + 1 = 2(2n² + 2n) + 1, which is odd.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.1', 'Proof', 'Prove that n³ − n is always divisible by 6.', 'n³ − n = (n − 1)n(n + 1), the product of three consecutive integers. One is a multiple of 3 and at least one is even, so the product is divisible by 6.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.1', 'Proof', 'What is the first step in a proof by contradiction?', 'Assume the negation of the statement you want to prove.', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.1', 'Proof', 'How do you prove there is no largest even number?', 'Assume N is the largest even number. Then N + 2 is even and larger, a contradiction.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.1', 'Proof', 'When is proof by exhaustion practical?', 'When there is a small finite number of cases to check, e.g. all integers from 1 to 10, or cases such as n odd and n even.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.1', 'Proof', 'What does ∴ mean in a proof?', '"Therefore".', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.1', 'Proof', 'What does the symbol ∈ mean?', '"Is an element of", e.g. n ∈ ℤ means n is an integer.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.1', 'Proof', 'What do ℕ, ℤ, ℚ and ℝ represent?', 'Natural numbers, integers, rational numbers and real numbers respectively.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.1', 'Proof', 'Why is a single example not a proof of a general statement?', 'It only shows the statement holds in that case; a proof must cover every case.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.1', 'Proof', 'Prove that the product of two consecutive integers is even.', 'Consider n(n + 1). One of n and n + 1 is even, so the product has a factor of 2 and is even.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.2', 'Algebra & Functions', 'How do you simplify a surd such as √50?', 'Extract the largest square factor: √50 = √(25 × 2) = 5√2.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.2', 'Algebra & Functions', 'How do you rationalise a denominator of the form a + √b?', 'Multiply numerator and denominator by the conjugate a − √b, using the difference of two squares to clear the surd.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.2', 'Algebra & Functions', 'Is √(a + b) equal to √a + √b?', 'No. The product rule holds but the sum rule does not: √(9 + 16) = 5 while √9 + √16 = 7.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.2', 'Algebra & Functions', 'State the laws of indices for multiplication, division and powers.', 'xᵃ × xᵇ = x^(a+b), xᵃ ÷ xᵇ = x^(a−b). (xᵃ)ᵇ = x^(ab).', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.2', 'Algebra & Functions', 'What are x⁰, x^(−a) and x^(m/n)?', 'x⁰ = 1 for x ≠ 0, x^(−a) = 1/xᵃ, x^(m/n) = (ⁿ√x)^m — take the root first to keep the numbers small.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.2', 'Algebra & Functions', 'What is the discriminant, and what do its three cases tell you?', 'b² − 4ac. Positive: two distinct real roots. Zero: one repeated root. Negative: no real roots.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.2', 'Algebra & Functions', 'What is the difference between ''two distinct real roots'' and ''real roots''?', 'Distinct requires b² − 4ac > 0, ''real roots'' usually allows the repeated case, so b² − 4ac ≥ 0. The wording decides < or ≤ in the answer.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.2', 'Algebra & Functions', 'What is the condition for a line to be tangent to a curve?', 'Substitute the line into the curve and set the discriminant of the resulting quadratic to zero.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.2', 'Algebra & Functions', 'How do you complete the square for x² + bx + c?', 'Write (x + b/2)² and subtract (b/2)², then add c. For x² + 6x + 2 this gives (x + 3)² − 7, so the minimum is at (−3, −7).', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.2', 'Algebra & Functions', 'How do you solve one linear and one quadratic simultaneous equation?', 'Always substitute the linear equation into the quadratic, then substitute the roots back into the linear equation and pair the values correctly.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.2', 'Algebra & Functions', 'How do you solve a quadratic inequality such as x² − 4x − 5 > 0?', 'Factorise to find the critical values, then read the sign from the parabola''s shape. Upward parabola: positive outside the roots, so x < −1 or x > 5.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.2', 'Algebra & Functions', 'Why can''t you multiply an inequality through by (x − 3)?', 'Its sign is unknown, so the inequality may or may not reverse. Instead collect over a common denominator, or multiply by the square (x − 3)², which is always positive.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.2', 'Algebra & Functions', 'When do two solution intervals need ''or'' rather than ''and''?', 'Always, for two separate intervals. ''x < −1 and x > 5'' describes no numbers at all, the union needs ''or''.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.2', 'Algebra & Functions', 'Write the four proportion relationships and their equations.', 'y ∝ x gives y = kx, y ∝ 1/x gives y = k/x, y ∝ xⁿ gives y = kxⁿ, y ∝ 1/x² gives y = k/x².', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.2', 'Algebra & Functions', 'What is the commonest error with repeated factors in partial fractions?', 'Omitting the A/(x+a) term and using only B/(x+a)². The system then has no solution.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.2', 'Algebra & Functions', 'Simplify √12 + √27.', '2√3 + 3√3 = 5√3.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.2', 'Algebra & Functions', 'State the factor theorem.', 'If f(a) = 0 then (x − a) is a factor of f(x), and conversely.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.2', 'Algebra & Functions', 'State the remainder theorem.', 'The remainder when f(x) is divided by (x − a) is f(a). A remainder of zero means (x − a) is a factor.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.2', 'Algebra & Functions', 'To test whether (x + 3) is a factor, which value do you substitute?', 'x = −3, since (x + 3) = (x − (−3)). Substituting +3 is the standard sign error.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.2', 'Algebra & Functions', 'When hunting for a first factor of a cubic, which values should you test?', 'The divisors of the constant term. For a constant of 6, test ±1, ±2, ±3, ±6.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.2', 'Algebra & Functions', 'What is the domain and what is the range of a function?', 'The domain is the set of permitted inputs, the range is the set of outputs the function actually takes.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.2', 'Algebra & Functions', 'What are the two usual causes of a restricted domain?', 'You cannot divide by zero, and you cannot take the square root of a negative number.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.2', 'Algebra & Functions', 'How do you find the range of a quadratic?', 'Complete the square. For f(x) = x² − 4x + 7 = (x − 2)² + 3, the range is f(x) ≥ 3.', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.2', 'Algebra & Functions', 'What does fg(x) mean?', 'f(g(x)) — apply g first, then f. The inner function is the one written next to the x.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.2', 'Algebra & Functions', 'Is composition of functions commutative?', 'No. With f(x) = 2x + 3 and g(x) = x², fg(x) = 2x² + 3 but gf(x) = (2x + 3)².', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.2', 'Algebra & Functions', 'When does an inverse function exist?', 'Only when the function is one-to-one. A many-to-one function such as x² needs its domain restricted first.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.2', 'Algebra & Functions', 'How do you find an inverse function?', 'Write y = f(x), rearrange to make x the subject, then swap the letters. Use the given domain to choose the sign of any square root.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.2', 'Algebra & Functions', 'How are the graphs of f and f⁻¹ related?', 'Each is the reflection of the other in the line y = x. Domain and range swap, and for an INCREASING function any intersection lies on y = x.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.2', 'Algebra & Functions', 'Define |x| as a piecewise function.', '|x| = x for x ≥ 0, and |x| = −x for x < 0. The graph is a V with vertex at the origin.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.2', 'Algebra & Functions', 'How do you obtain y = |f(x)| from y = f(x)?', 'Reflect the parts below the x-axis up into it, leaving the rest unchanged. It acts on the OUTPUT.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.3', 'Coordinate Geometry', 'Write down the gradient, midpoint and distance formulae.', 'Gradient (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁), midpoint ((x₁+x₂)/2, (y₁+y₂)/2), distance √((x₂−x₁)² + (y₂−y₁)²).', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.3', 'Coordinate Geometry', 'What is the relationship between perpendicular gradients?', 'Their product is −1, so one is the negative reciprocal of the other. The rule fails for a vertical line, whose gradient is undefined.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.3', 'Coordinate Geometry', 'How do you show three points are collinear?', 'Show the gradient between successive pairs is equal, and note they share a common point.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.3', 'Coordinate Geometry', 'Write down the equation of a circle with centre (a, b) and radius r.', '(x − a)² + (y − b)² = r². Note the signs: centre (2, −3) gives (x − 2)² + (y + 3)².', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.3', 'Coordinate Geometry', 'How do you find the centre and radius from x² + y² + 2gx + 2fy + c = 0?', 'Complete the square in both x and y. The right-hand side is then r², so remember to take the square root.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.3', 'Coordinate Geometry', 'What is the commonest error when reading a circle equation?', 'Quoting r² as the radius. From (x − 3)² + (y + 2)² = 25 the radius is 5, not 25.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.3', 'Coordinate Geometry', 'How do you find a circle''s equation from the endpoints of a diameter?', 'The centre is the midpoint, the radius is half the distance between the endpoints.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.3', 'Coordinate Geometry', 'How do you tell whether a point is inside, on, or outside a circle?', 'Substitute the coordinates and compare with r². Greater than r² means outside, less means inside.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.3', 'Coordinate Geometry', 'How do you find the equation of a tangent to a circle at a given point?', 'Find the gradient of the radius to that point, take its negative reciprocal for the tangent, then use the point-gradient form.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.3', 'Coordinate Geometry', 'How do you find how many times a line meets a circle?', 'Substitute the line into the circle and look at the discriminant of the resulting quadratic: two roots, one (tangent), or none.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.3', 'Coordinate Geometry', 'What are parametric equations?', 'x and y are each given as functions of a third variable, the parameter. Each parameter value gives one point on the curve.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.3', 'Coordinate Geometry', 'Why are parametric equations useful?', 'They describe curves that cannot be written as y = f(x), such as a full circle, which fails the one-output-per-input test.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.3', 'Coordinate Geometry', 'How do you convert parametric equations to Cartesian form?', 'Eliminate the parameter. Rearrange the SIMPLER equation for the parameter, or use sin²θ + cos²θ = 1 for trigonometric parameters.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.3', 'Coordinate Geometry', 'What is dy/dx for a parametric curve?', 'dy/dx = (dy/dt) ÷ (dx/dt). Getting the fraction the wrong way up is a standard error.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.3', 'Coordinate Geometry', 'Write down the parametric equations of a circle.', 'x = a + r cos θ and y = b + r sin θ, for centre (a, b) and radius r. Eliminating θ with cos²θ + sin²θ = 1 returns the Cartesian form.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.3', 'Coordinate Geometry', 'Find the equation of the line through (1, 2) with gradient 3.', 'y − 2 = 3(x − 1), so y = 3x − 1.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.3', 'Coordinate Geometry', 'What is the gradient of the line 2x + 3y = 6?', 'Rearrange to y = −2x/3 + 2, so gradient = −2/3.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.3', 'Coordinate Geometry', 'Find the equation of the perpendicular bisector of A(0, 0) and B(4, 2).', 'Midpoint (2, 1); gradient AB = 1/2, so perpendicular gradient = −2. y − 1 = −2(x − 2), so y = −2x + 5.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.3', 'Coordinate Geometry', 'What is the distance between (1, 2) and (4, 6)?', '√((4 − 1)² + (6 − 2)²) = √(9 + 16) = 5.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.3', 'Coordinate Geometry', 'Find the centre and radius of x² + y² − 4x + 6y − 12 = 0.', '(x − 2)² + (y + 3)² = 25: centre (2, −3), radius 5.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.3', 'Coordinate Geometry', 'What does the angle in a semicircle theorem state?', 'The angle subtended at the circumference by a diameter is a right angle.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.3', 'Coordinate Geometry', 'What does the perpendicular from the centre to a chord do?', 'It bisects the chord.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.3', 'Coordinate Geometry', 'What is the relationship between a tangent and a radius?', 'A tangent is perpendicular to the radius at the point of contact.', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.3', 'Coordinate Geometry', 'How do you find the equation of a circle through three points?', 'Find the perpendicular bisectors of two chords; they intersect at the centre. Then find the radius.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.3', 'Coordinate Geometry', 'Convert x = t², y = 2t into Cartesian form.', 't = y/2, so x = y²/4, i.e. y² = 4x.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.3', 'Coordinate Geometry', 'Find dy/dx for x = t², y = t³.', 'dy/dx = (3t²)/(2t) = 3t/2.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.3', 'Coordinate Geometry', 'What is the parametric form of the circle (x − a)² + (y − b)² = r²?', 'x = a + r cos θ, y = b + r sin θ.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.3', 'Coordinate Geometry', 'How do you find where a parametric curve meets the x-axis?', 'Set y = 0, solve for t, then substitute into x.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.3', 'Coordinate Geometry', 'What does it mean if a line meets a circle at exactly one point?', 'The line is a tangent to the circle (the discriminant is zero).', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.3', 'Coordinate Geometry', 'Find the equation of the tangent to x² + y² = 25 at (3, 4).', 'The radius has gradient 4/3, so the tangent has gradient −3/4: y − 4 = −3/4(x − 3), i.e. 3x + 4y = 25.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.4', 'Sequences & Series', 'Write down the nth term and sum formulae for an arithmetic sequence.', 'uₙ = a + (n − 1)d. Sₙ = (n/2)(2a + (n − 1)d), or (n/2)(a + l) using the last term.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.4', 'Sequences & Series', 'Write down the nth term and sum formulae for a geometric sequence.', 'uₙ = arⁿ⁻¹. Sₙ = a(1 − rⁿ)/(1 − r) for r ≠ 1.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.4', 'Sequences & Series', 'When does a geometric series converge, and what is its sum to infinity?', 'It converges if and only if |r| < 1, and then S∞ = a/(1 − r). Always state the condition.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.4', 'Sequences & Series', 'What happens if you use the sum-to-infinity formula when |r| ≥ 1?', 'It produces a plausible-looking number that is meaningless, because the series diverges.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.4', 'Sequences & Series', 'What is a recurrence relation?', 'A rule defining each term from the previous one, together with a starting value — for example u₍ₙ₊₁₎ = uₙ + 3 with u₁ = 2.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.4', 'Sequences & Series', 'How do you find the limit of a convergent recurrence relation?', 'Replace both u₍ₙ₊₁₎ and uₙ by L and solve for L, rejecting any root inconsistent with the sequence.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.4', 'Sequences & Series', 'Write the binomial expansion of (1 + x)ⁿ for a positive integer n.', '1 + nx + [n(n−1)/2!]x² + [n(n−1)(n−2)/3!]x³ + … It terminates, giving exactly n + 1 terms.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.4', 'Sequences & Series', 'What is the standard error when expanding (1 + 2x)⁵?', 'Forgetting to raise the coefficient too: (2x)³ = 8x³, not 2x³.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.4', 'Sequences & Series', 'How do you find a particular coefficient in (a + b)ⁿ?', 'Use the term ⁿCᵣ a^(n−r) b^r, choosing r to give the required power.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.4', 'Sequences & Series', 'What changes when the binomial index is negative or fractional?', 'The expansion no longer terminates — it becomes an infinite series — and it is valid only for |x| < 1.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.4', 'Sequences & Series', 'What is the validity condition for expanding (1 + ax)ⁿ with non-integer n?', '|ax| < 1, that is |x| < 1/|a|. It is NOT |x| < 1 whenever there is a coefficient on x.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.4', 'Sequences & Series', 'Expand (1 + x)^(1/2) as far as the term in x².', '1 + x/2 − x²/8.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.4', 'Sequences & Series', 'How do you expand √(4 + x) using the binomial series?', 'Factorise first: √(4 + x) = 2(1 + x/4)^(1/2). The series cannot be applied to a bracket not starting with 1.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.4', 'Sequences & Series', 'How do you find the nth term given the sum of the first n terms?', 'uₙ = Sₙ − S₍ₙ₋₁₎. If Sₙ = n² then uₙ = n² − (n − 1)² = 2n − 1.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.4', 'Sequences & Series', 'What are Σ from k=1 to n of k, and of (2k − 1)?', 'n(n + 1)/2 and n² respectively.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.4', 'Sequences & Series', 'What is the commonest confusion in sequences questions?', 'Mixing up uₙ (a single term) with Sₙ (a running total). Read the question twice.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.4', 'Sequences & Series', 'Find the 10th term of the arithmetic sequence 3, 7, 11, …', 'a = 3, d = 4: u₁₀ = 3 + 9 × 4 = 39.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.4', 'Sequences & Series', 'Find the sum of the first 20 terms of 3, 7, 11, …', 'S₂₀ = 20/2 × (2 × 3 + 19 × 4) = 10 × 82 = 820.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.4', 'Sequences & Series', 'Find the sum to infinity of 8 + 4 + 2 + …', 'a = 8, r = 1/2: S∞ = 8/(1 − 1/2) = 16.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.4', 'Sequences & Series', 'What is a periodic sequence?', 'A sequence that repeats after a fixed number of terms, e.g. 1, −1, 1, −1, …', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.4', 'Sequences & Series', 'What is an increasing sequence?', 'Each term is larger than the previous one: uₙ₊₁ > uₙ for all n.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.4', 'Sequences & Series', 'What is a divergent sequence?', 'A sequence that doesn''t tend to a finite limit.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.4', 'Sequences & Series', 'What is sigma notation?', 'A shorthand for sums: Σ from r=1 to n of f(r) means f(1) + f(2) + … + f(n).', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.4', 'Sequences & Series', 'What is ⁿCᵣ?', 'The number of ways of choosing r items from n: n!/(r!(n − r)!).', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.4', 'Sequences & Series', 'Find the coefficient of x³ in (2 + x)⁵.', '⁵C₃ × 2² × x³ = 10 × 4 = 40.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.4', 'Sequences & Series', 'Expand (1 − x)⁻¹ up to x³.', '1 + x + x² + x³, valid for |x| < 1.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.4', 'Sequences & Series', 'Expand (1 + 2x)⁻² up to x².', '1 − 4x + 12x², valid for |x| < 1/2.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.4', 'Sequences & Series', 'What is Pascal''s triangle used for?', 'To find binomial coefficients for small positive integer powers.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.4', 'Sequences & Series', 'What is n! ?', 'n × (n − 1) × … × 2 × 1, with 0! = 1.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.4', 'Sequences & Series', 'How do you find the number of terms in an arithmetic sequence?', 'Use uₙ = a + (n − 1)d, set it equal to the last term and solve for n.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.5', 'Trigonometry', 'What is a radian, and how does it relate to degrees?', 'The angle subtended at the centre by an arc equal in length to the radius. π radians = 180°.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.5', 'Trigonometry', 'Write down the arc length and sector area formulae.', 's = rθ and A = ½r²θ, both requiring θ in RADIANS. Using degrees gives an answer wrong by a factor of about 57.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.5', 'Trigonometry', 'State the sine rule, the cosine rule and the triangle area formula.', 'a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C, a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A. Area = ½ab sin C.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.5', 'Trigonometry', 'When do you use the cosine rule rather than the sine rule?', 'With three sides, or two sides and the angle between them. Otherwise use the sine rule.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.5', 'Trigonometry', 'What is the ambiguous case of the sine rule?', 'Given two sides and a non-included angle, sin θ = k has two solutions in 0° to 180°: θ and 180° − θ. Both must be checked.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.5', 'Trigonometry', 'Give the exact values of sin, cos and tan at 30°, 45° and 60°.', 'sin: ½, √2/2, √3/2.  cos: √3/2, √2/2, ½.  tan: 1/√3, 1, √3.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.5', 'Trigonometry', 'How do you find the second solution of sin x = k, cos x = k and tan x = k?', 'For sin: π − x. For cos: 2π − x. For tan: x + π.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.5', 'Trigonometry', 'How do you solve an equation such as sin 2x = k over 0° ≤ x < 360°?', 'Widen the range FIRST: 2x runs over 0° to 720°. Solve there, then halve every answer. Halving first loses half the solutions.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.5', 'Trigonometry', 'Define sec, cosec and cot.', 'sec θ = 1/cos θ, cosec θ = 1/sin θ, cot θ = 1/tan θ. Look at the third letter: se-c goes with c-os.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.5', 'Trigonometry', 'State the two identities derived from sin²θ + cos²θ = 1.', '1 + tan²θ = sec²θ (divide by cos²θ), 1 + cot²θ = cosec²θ (divide by sin²θ).', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.5', 'Trigonometry', 'Write down the compound angle formulae for sin(A ± B) and cos(A ± B).', 'sin(A ± B) = sin A cos B ± cos A sin B, cos(A ± B) = cos A cos B ∓ sin A sin B — note the sign flip.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.5', 'Trigonometry', 'Write down the three forms of cos 2θ.', 'cos²θ − sin²θ, 2cos²θ − 1, 1 − 2sin²θ. Choose the form matching the other function in the equation.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.5', 'Trigonometry', 'What are sin 2θ and tan 2θ?', 'sin 2θ = 2 sin θ cos θ, tan 2θ = 2tan θ/(1 − tan²θ).', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.5', 'Trigonometry', 'How do you write a sin x + b cos x as a single trigonometric function?', 'As R sin(x + α) with R = √(a² + b²) and tan α = b/a. The expression then ranges between −R and R.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.5', 'Trigonometry', 'State the small angle approximations.', 'For small θ in RADIANS: sin θ ≈ θ, tan θ ≈ θ, cos θ ≈ 1 − θ²/2. Also (sin θ)/θ → 1 as θ → 0.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.5', 'Trigonometry', 'What goes wrong if you divide a trigonometric equation by cos x?', 'You lose every solution where cos x = 0. Factorise instead of dividing, and check those cases separately.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.5', 'Trigonometry', 'Convert 150° to radians.', '150 × π/180 = 5π/6.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.5', 'Trigonometry', 'Find the arc length of a sector with radius 6 cm and angle π/3.', 's = rθ = 6 × π/3 = 2π cm.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.5', 'Trigonometry', 'Find the area of a sector with radius 6 cm and angle π/3.', 'A = ½r²θ = ½ × 36 × π/3 = 6π cm².', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.5', 'Trigonometry', 'What is the exact value of sin(π/6)?', '1/2.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.5', 'Trigonometry', 'What is the exact value of cos(π/4)?', '√2/2 (or 1/√2).', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.5', 'Trigonometry', 'What is the exact value of tan(π/3)?', '√3.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.5', 'Trigonometry', 'What is the period of y = sin x and y = tan x?', 'sin x has period 2π (360°); tan x has period π (180°).', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.5', 'Trigonometry', 'What is the identity linking tan, sin and cos?', 'tan θ = sin θ/cos θ.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.5', 'Trigonometry', 'What is 1 + tan²θ equal to?', 'sec²θ.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.5', 'Trigonometry', 'What is 1 + cot²θ equal to?', 'cosec²θ.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.5', 'Trigonometry', 'Solve sin x = 0.5 for 0 ≤ x < 2π.', 'x = π/6 or x = 5π/6.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.5', 'Trigonometry', 'What are the principal value ranges of arcsin, arccos and arctan?', 'arcsin: −π/2 to π/2; arccos: 0 to π; arctan: −π/2 to π/2 (exclusive).', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.5', 'Trigonometry', 'Write sin 3x cos x + cos 3x sin x as a single term.', 'sin(3x + x) = sin 4x.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.5', 'Trigonometry', 'Express 3 sin x + 4 cos x as R sin(x + α).', 'R = 5, tan α = 4/3, so α ≈ 0.927 rad (53.1°): 5 sin(x + 0.927).', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.6', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'What is the relationship between aˣ and log_a x?', 'They are inverse functions: x = a^y is equivalent to y = log_a x. Their graphs are reflections in y = x.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.6', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'State the three laws of logarithms.', 'log(xy) = log x + log y, log(x/y) = log x − log y, log(xᵏ) = k log x.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.6', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'Is there a law for log(x + y)?', 'No. The addition law applies to a PRODUCT inside the logarithm, not a sum. log(x + y) ≠ log x + log y.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.6', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'What are log_a a and log_a 1?', 'log_a a = 1 and log_a 1 = 0.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.6', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'How do you solve an equation such as 3ˣ = 20?', 'Take logarithms of both sides, then use the power law to bring x down: x = log 20 ÷ log 3 ≈ 2.727.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.6', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'What must you check after solving a logarithmic equation?', 'That each solution keeps every logarithm''s argument positive. Reject any root that makes an argument zero or negative.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.6', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'What is special about e?', 'It is the base for which the gradient of y = eˣ equals eˣ itself. More generally the gradient of e^(kx) is k e^(kx).', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.6', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'Why does the exponential model suit so many situations?', 'Because the derivative of e^(kt) is proportional to the function itself — exactly the condition ''rate of change proportional to current size''.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.6', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'What is ln x, and what are ln e and e^(ln x)?', 'ln x is log to base e, the inverse of eˣ. ln e = 1, and e^(ln x) = x for x > 0.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.6', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'How do you reduce y = axⁿ to linear form?', 'Take logs: log y = log a + n log x. Plot log y against log x — gradient n, intercept log a.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.6', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'How do you reduce y = abˣ to linear form?', 'Take logs: log y = log a + x log b. Plot log y against PLAIN x — gradient log b, intercept log a.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.6', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'How do you tell a power law from an exponential law using graphs?', 'A power law gives a straight line on log–log axes, an exponential law gives one with log y against plain x. The difference is whether the horizontal axis carries a logarithm.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.6', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'Write down the exponential growth and decay models.', 'N = N₀e^(kt) for growth and N = N₀e^(−kt) for decay, with k > 0 and N₀ the value at t = 0.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.6', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'How do you find the decay constant from a half-life T?', 'Set e^(−kT) = ½, so kT = ln 2 and k = (ln 2)/T.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.6', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'Give two criticisms of an exponential population growth model.', 'It predicts unbounded growth, ignoring finite resources, and it assumes k stays constant, though it may drift with conditions. A logistic model adds a carrying capacity.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.6', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'Solve 2ˣ = 8.', 'x = 3.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.6', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'Solve eˣ = 5.', 'x = ln 5.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.6', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'Simplify log 8 + log 5 − log 4.', 'log(8 × 5/4) = log 10.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.6', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'Write 2 log x − log y as a single log.', 'log(x²/y).', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.6', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'What is the gradient of y = eᵏˣ?', 'keᵏˣ.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.6', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'What is log_a aˣ equal to?', 'x.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.6', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'What is e^(ln 7)?', '7.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.6', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'What is ln(e³)?', '3.', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.6', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'Solve ln x = 2.', 'x = e².', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.6', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'What is the change of base formula?', 'log_a b = log_c b / log_c a.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.6', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'What is the asymptote of y = eˣ?', 'y = 0 (the x-axis).', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.6', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'What is the asymptote of y = ln x?', 'x = 0 (the y-axis).', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.6', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'If y = axⁿ, what does a log–log graph give?', 'log y = log a + n log x: a straight line with gradient n and intercept log a.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.6', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'If y = abˣ, what does plotting log y against x give?', 'log y = log a + x log b: a straight line with gradient log b and intercept log a.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.6', 'Exponentials & Logarithms', 'Solve 5^(2x) − 6 × 5^x + 5 = 0.', 'Let u = 5^x: u² − 6u + 5 = 0, so u = 1 or 5, giving x = 0 or x = 1.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.7', 'Differentiation', 'Write down the definition of the derivative as a limit.', 'f′(x) = limit as h → 0 of (f(x + h) − f(x))/h — the gradient of a chord becoming the gradient of the tangent.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.7', 'Differentiation', 'Why must you cancel the h before letting h → 0?', 'Substituting h = 0 first gives 0/0, which is undefined. Cancelling is the whole point of the method.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.7', 'Differentiation', 'What are the derivatives of xⁿ, eˣ and ln x?', 'nxⁿ⁻¹, eˣ, 1/x.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.7', 'Differentiation', 'What are the derivatives of sin x, cos x and tan x?', 'cos x, −sin x, sec²x. These hold only when x is in RADIANS.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.7', 'Differentiation', 'Why do the trigonometric derivatives require radians?', 'They follow from the limit (sin h)/h → 1, which is true only in radians. In degrees an extra constant factor appears.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.7', 'Differentiation', 'State the chain rule.', 'The derivative of f(g(x)) is f′(g(x)) × g′(x) — the outer derivative times the inner derivative.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.7', 'Differentiation', 'State the product rule.', 'The derivative of uv is u′v + uv′.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.7', 'Differentiation', 'State the quotient rule.', 'The derivative of u/v is (u′v − uv′)/v². Note the minus, and that the order matters — reversing it flips every sign.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.7', 'Differentiation', 'How do you differentiate implicitly?', 'Differentiate both sides with respect to x, applying the chain rule to every y term so each contributes a factor dy/dx, then collect and solve.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.7', 'Differentiation', 'Why does a dy/dx appear when differentiating a y term?', 'Because y is itself a function of x, so the chain rule applies: differentiating f(y) with respect to x gives f′(y) × dy/dx.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.7', 'Differentiation', 'How do you differentiate an inverse function?', 'Use dy/dx = 1 ÷ (dx/dy). For y = ln x, write x = e^y, so dx/dy = e^y = x and dy/dx = 1/x.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.7', 'Differentiation', 'How do you find the gradient of a tangent and of a normal?', 'The tangent gradient is dy/dx at that point, the normal gradient is −1 divided by that value.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.7', 'Differentiation', 'How do you classify a stationary point using the second derivative?', 'Positive means minimum, negative means maximum, zero is inconclusive and requires checking the gradient either side.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.7', 'Differentiation', 'Does d²y/dx² = 0 prove a point of inflection?', 'No. For y = x⁴ at the origin the second derivative is zero yet the point is a minimum. A genuine inflection needs the second derivative to CHANGE SIGN.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.7', 'Differentiation', 'How do connected rates of change work?', 'Link them by the chain rule, for example dV/dt = (dV/dr) × (dr/dt).', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.7', 'Differentiation', 'What are the two marks most often lost in an optimisation question?', 'Not justifying that the stationary point is the required maximum or minimum, and not answering the quantity actually asked for.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.7', 'Differentiation', 'Differentiate y = 3x⁴ − 2x + 7.', 'dy/dx = 12x³ − 2.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.7', 'Differentiation', 'Differentiate y = √x.', 'y = x^(1/2), so dy/dx = ½x^(−1/2) = 1/(2√x).', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.7', 'Differentiation', 'Differentiate y = e^(3x).', 'dy/dx = 3e^(3x).', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.7', 'Differentiation', 'Differentiate y = ln(5x).', 'dy/dx = 1/x.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.7', 'Differentiation', 'Differentiate y = sin(2x).', 'dy/dx = 2 cos(2x).', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.7', 'Differentiation', 'Differentiate y = (2x + 1)⁵.', 'dy/dx = 5(2x + 1)⁴ × 2 = 10(2x + 1)⁴.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.7', 'Differentiation', 'Differentiate y = x² eˣ.', 'Product rule: dy/dx = 2x eˣ + x² eˣ = x eˣ(2 + x).', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.7', 'Differentiation', 'Differentiate y = (sin x)/x.', 'Quotient rule: dy/dx = (x cos x − sin x)/x².', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.7', 'Differentiation', 'What is the derivative of aˣ?', 'aˣ ln a.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.7', 'Differentiation', 'What does it mean if dy/dx > 0?', 'The function is increasing.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.7', 'Differentiation', 'What does a positive second derivative indicate?', 'The curve is concave upwards (convex), and a stationary point there is a minimum.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.7', 'Differentiation', 'What is a point of inflection?', 'A point where the curve changes concavity (d²y/dx² changes sign).', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.7', 'Differentiation', 'Find the gradient of x² + y² = 25 at (3, 4).', '2x + 2y dy/dx = 0, so dy/dx = −x/y = −3/4.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.7', 'Differentiation', 'What is the derivative of sec x?', 'sec x tan x.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.8', 'Integration', 'What is the integral of xⁿ, and when does it fail?', 'x^(n+1)/(n + 1) + c, failing for n = −1 where the denominator would be zero. Then the integral of 1/x is ln|x| + c.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.8', 'Integration', 'Why is the modulus written in ln|x|?', 'So the result is valid for negative x too, since the logarithm of a negative number is undefined.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.8', 'Integration', 'Why does an indefinite integral need + c but a definite one does not?', 'Differentiating a constant gives zero, so it cannot be recovered. In a definite integral the constant appears in both F(b) and F(a) and cancels.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.8', 'Integration', 'State the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus.', 'The definite integral from a to b of f equals F(b) − F(a), where F′ = f. It links area with the antiderivative and shows the two processes are inverse.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.8', 'Integration', 'What happens to a region below the x-axis in a definite integral?', 'It contributes a negative amount. If the curve crosses the axis within the limits, split there and add the magnitudes, or the parts will cancel.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.8', 'Integration', 'How do you find the area between two curves?', 'Integrate (upper − lower) between the intersections, so the integrand is non-negative and the area comes out positive.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.8', 'Integration', 'When is integration by substitution the right method?', 'When the integrand contains an inner function together with its derivative — for example x e^(x²), where u = x² gives du = 2x dx.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.8', 'Integration', 'What must you also change when substituting in a DEFINITE integral?', 'The limits, converting them to values of u — or substitute back to x before applying the original limits.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.8', 'Integration', 'State the standard result for f′(x)/f(x).', 'Its integral is ln|f(x)| + c. For example the integral of 2x/(x² + 1) is ln(x² + 1) + c.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.8', 'Integration', 'State the integration by parts formula.', 'The integral of u(dv/dx) equals uv minus the integral of v(du/dx) — the reverse of the product rule.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.8', 'Integration', 'How do you choose u when integrating by parts?', 'Choose u to be the factor that becomes SIMPLER when differentiated, and dv/dx to be one you can integrate.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.8', 'Integration', 'How do you integrate ln x?', 'Write it as 1 × ln x, take u = ln x and dv/dx = 1. This gives x ln x − x + c.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.8', 'Integration', 'How do you integrate eˣ sin x?', 'Apply parts twice, the original integral reappears, so treat it as an unknown and solve the resulting equation algebraically.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.8', 'Integration', 'Why are partial fractions useful in integration?', 'A fraction with a factorised denominator has no standard integral, but splitting it gives terms A/(x + a), each integrating to a logarithm.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.8', 'Integration', 'How do you integrate cos²x?', 'Use the double angle identity cos²x = ½(1 + cos 2x), giving x/2 + (sin 2x)/4 + c.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.8', 'Integration', 'What are the integrals of sin x, cos x and sec²x?', '−cos x + c, sin x + c, tan x + c. The minus on the sine integral is the one most often dropped.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.8', 'Integration', 'Integrate 6x² + 4x − 3.', '2x³ + 2x² − 3x + c.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.8', 'Integration', 'Integrate 1/x².', '−1/x + c.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.8', 'Integration', 'Integrate e^(2x).', '½e^(2x) + c.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.8', 'Integration', 'Integrate 1/x.', 'ln|x| + c.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.8', 'Integration', 'Integrate cos(3x).', '⅓ sin(3x) + c.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.8', 'Integration', 'Integrate (2x + 1)³.', '(2x + 1)⁴/8 + c.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.8', 'Integration', 'What is the difference between a general and a particular solution?', 'A general solution contains an arbitrary constant and represents a family of curves, a particular solution uses an initial condition to fix that constant.', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.8', 'Integration', 'How do you separate the variables?', 'If dy/dx = f(x)g(y), gather all the y terms with dy and all the x terms with dx, then integrate both sides.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.8', 'Integration', 'Translate ''the rate of growth is proportional to the size'' into an equation.', 'dP/dt = kP. For decay it is dN/dt = −kN with k positive.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.8', 'Integration', 'Translate ''the rate is proportional to the square of the population''.', 'dP/dt = kP².', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.8', 'Integration', 'Write down Newton''s law of cooling.', 'dθ/dt = −k(θ − θ₀), where θ₀ is the temperature of the surroundings — the rate is proportional to the excess temperature.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.8', 'Integration', 'What is the solution of dP/dt = kP with P = P₀ at t = 0?', 'P = P₀e^(kt).', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.8', 'Integration', 'Why is it useful to write the constant as ln A?', 'It turns an additive constant into a multiplicative one, so ln y = ln x + c becomes y = Ax instead of y = e^c × x.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.8', 'Integration', 'Solve dy/dx = xy with y = 1 at x = 0.', 'Separate to (1/y)dy = x dx, integrate to ln y = x²/2 + c, apply the condition to get c = 0, so y = e^(x²/2).', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.9', 'Numerical Methods', 'State the change of sign condition for locating a root.', 'If f is CONTINUOUS on [a, b] and f(a), f(b) have opposite signs, then f(x) = 0 has at least one root in (a, b).', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.9', 'Numerical Methods', 'Which part of the change of sign argument is most often omitted?', 'The statement that f is continuous on the interval. Without it the argument is invalid.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.9', 'Numerical Methods', 'Name two situations in which a change of sign method fails.', 'A repeated root, where the curve touches without crossing, e.g. (x − 2)², and a vertical asymptote, e.g. 1/(x − 2), where the sign changes but there is no root.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.9', 'Numerical Methods', 'How do you show a root is 1.86 correct to 2 decimal places?', 'Show f changes sign between the rounding bounds 1.855 and 1.865, so every value in that interval rounds to 1.86.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.9', 'Numerical Methods', 'What is fixed point iteration?', 'Rearrange f(x) = 0 into x = g(x), choose a starting value, then apply x₍ₙ₊₁₎ = g(xₙ) repeatedly.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.9', 'Numerical Methods', 'When does a fixed point iteration converge?', 'When |g′(x)| < 1 near the root. If |g′(x)| > 1 it diverges, so different rearrangements of the same equation can behave differently.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.9', 'Numerical Methods', 'What is the difference between a staircase and a cobweb diagram?', 'Both are drawn between y = g(x) and y = x. A staircase occurs when g′(x) > 0, a cobweb, spiralling and alternating sides, when g′(x) < 0.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.9', 'Numerical Methods', 'State the Newton-Raphson formula and its geometric meaning.', 'x₍ₙ₊₁₎ = xₙ − f(xₙ)/f′(xₙ). Follow the tangent at the current point down to where it meets the x-axis.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.9', 'Numerical Methods', 'Why does Newton-Raphson converge faster than a simple iteration?', 'Near a simple root the error is roughly SQUARED at each step, so the number of correct digits approximately doubles.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.9', 'Numerical Methods', 'When does Newton-Raphson fail?', 'When f′(xₙ) = 0, since the tangent is horizontal and never meets the axis, and near a stationary point, where a nearly horizontal tangent throws the next estimate far away.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.9', 'Numerical Methods', 'State the trapezium rule.', 'With h = (b − a)/n, the integral is approximately (h/2)[y₀ + yₙ + 2(y₁ + … + y₍ₙ₋₁₎)] — first and last counted once, interior ordinates doubled.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.9', 'Numerical Methods', 'How many ordinates are needed for n strips?', 'n + 1, since adjacent strips share an ordinate.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.9', 'Numerical Methods', 'When does the trapezium rule give an over- or under-estimate?', 'Concave upwards: chords lie above the curve, so it is an OVERestimate. Concave downwards: chords lie below, so it is an UNDERestimate.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.9', 'Numerical Methods', 'How should you justify the direction of a trapezium error?', 'Refer to the concavity: ''the curve is concave upwards on this interval, so each chord lies above it and the estimate is too large''.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.9', 'Numerical Methods', 'What happens to the trapezium rule error if you halve the strip width?', 'It reduces by a factor of roughly 4, since the error is approximately proportional to the square of the width.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.9', 'Numerical Methods', 'How can rectangles give bounds on an integral?', 'For a monotonic function, left-hand and right-hand rectangle sums lie on either side of the true value, bracketing it.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.9', 'Numerical Methods', 'What does the change of sign method rely on?', 'If f is continuous on [a, b] and f(a) and f(b) have opposite signs, there is a root between a and b.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.9', 'Numerical Methods', 'Why can a change of sign occur without a root?', 'At a vertical asymptote (discontinuity), e.g. f(x) = 1/x between −1 and 1.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.9', 'Numerical Methods', 'Why might there be a root with no change of sign?', 'A repeated root where the curve touches the x-axis, or two roots close together.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.9', 'Numerical Methods', 'What is decimal search?', 'Checking values at intervals of 0.1, then 0.01, etc., to locate a root by change of sign.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.9', 'Numerical Methods', 'What is interval bisection?', 'Repeatedly halving the interval containing the root and keeping the half with a sign change.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.9', 'Numerical Methods', 'How do you rearrange f(x) = 0 into x = g(x)?', 'Make x the subject of part of the equation, e.g. x³ − x − 1 = 0 → x = ∛(x + 1).', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.9', 'Numerical Methods', 'What is the convergence condition for xₙ₊₁ = g(xₙ)?', '|g′(x)| < 1 near the root.', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.9', 'Numerical Methods', 'What is a staircase diagram?', 'A diagram showing iteration converging monotonically, when 0 < g′(x) < 1.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.9', 'Numerical Methods', 'What is a cobweb diagram?', 'A diagram showing iteration oscillating around the root, when −1 < g′(x) < 0.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.9', 'Numerical Methods', 'Apply one step of Newton–Raphson to f(x) = x² − 2 from x₀ = 1.', 'x₁ = 1 − (1 − 2)/2 = 1.5.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.9', 'Numerical Methods', 'When does the trapezium rule give an overestimate?', 'When the curve is concave up (convex) over the interval.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.9', 'Numerical Methods', 'When does the trapezium rule give an underestimate?', 'When the curve is concave down over the interval.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.9', 'Numerical Methods', 'How do you improve the accuracy of the trapezium rule?', 'Increase the number of strips (decrease the strip width).', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.9', 'Numerical Methods', 'What is the width h in the trapezium rule?', 'h = (b − a)/n, where n is the number of strips.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.10', 'Vectors', 'What is the difference between a scalar and a vector?', 'A scalar has magnitude only (mass, time, speed), a vector has magnitude and direction (displacement, velocity, force).', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.10', 'Vectors', 'How are vectors written in print and by hand?', 'Bold in print, underlined by hand. The magnitude is written |a| or a in italic.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.10', 'Vectors', 'How do you find the magnitude of a vector in 2D and 3D?', '√(a₁² + a₂²) in two dimensions, √(a₁² + a₂² + a₃²) in three.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.10', 'Vectors', 'How do you find a unit vector in the direction of a?', 'Divide a by its magnitude: â = a/|a|. For 6i − 8j, the magnitude is 10 so the unit vector is 0.6i − 0.8j.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.10', 'Vectors', 'When are two non-zero vectors parallel?', 'When one is a scalar multiple of the other. A negative scalar reverses the direction but they remain parallel.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.10', 'Vectors', 'Write down the vector AB in terms of position vectors.', 'AB = b − a — destination minus start. Reversing it gives the same magnitude in the opposite direction.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.10', 'Vectors', 'How do you find the distance between two points from their position vectors?', 'It is |b − a|, the magnitude of the vector joining them.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.10', 'Vectors', 'What is the position vector of the midpoint of AB?', '(a + b)/2. More generally the point dividing AB in the ratio m : n is (na + mb)/(m + n).', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.10', 'Vectors', 'How do you convert between component and magnitude-direction form?', 'a₁ = r cos θ and a₂ = r sin θ, conversely r = √(a₁² + a₂²) and tan θ = a₂/a₁.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.10', 'Vectors', 'Why is arctan alone not enough to give a direction?', 'It returns angles only in the first and fourth quadrants. Use the signs of the components to identify the quadrant and adjust — (−3, −4) gives 233.1°, not 53.1°.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.10', 'Vectors', 'How do you prove three points collinear using vectors?', 'Show one joining vector is a scalar multiple of another, AND state that they share a common point. Parallel alone is not enough.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.10', 'Vectors', 'What does the sum of force vectors represent?', 'The resultant force. The specification explicitly includes using vectors for problems involving forces.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.10', 'Vectors', 'What is the condition for a set of forces to be in equilibrium?', 'Their vector sum is the zero vector, so drawn head to tail they form a closed figure.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.10', 'Vectors', 'Is |a + b| equal to |a| + |b|?', 'Not in general. It lies between ||a| − |b|| and |a| + |b|, reaching the upper value only when the vectors point the same way.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.10', 'Vectors', 'How does 3D vector work extend the 2D case?', 'A third component along k is added. Magnitudes involve the sum of three squares, and all the 2D techniques carry over.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.10', 'Vectors', 'What is the zero vector?', 'A vector with zero magnitude and no specific direction.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.10', 'Vectors', 'Find the magnitude of 3i − 4j.', '√(9 + 16) = 5.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.10', 'Vectors', 'Find the unit vector in the direction of 3i − 4j.', '(3i − 4j)/5 = 0.6i − 0.8j.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.10', 'Vectors', 'If a = 2i + j and b = i − 3j, find 2a − b.', '4i + 2j − i + 3j = 3i + 5j.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.10', 'Vectors', 'What is a position vector?', 'A vector giving the position of a point relative to the origin.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.10', 'Vectors', 'Find the direction of 3i + 3j.', '45° above the positive x-axis.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.10', 'Vectors', 'What is the magnitude of 2i − j + 2k?', '√(4 + 1 + 4) = 3.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.10', 'Vectors', 'How do you find a vector of magnitude 10 in the direction of 3i + 4j?', 'Unit vector (3i + 4j)/5, times 10: 6i + 8j.', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.10', 'Vectors', 'If AB = b − a, what is BA?', 'a − b.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.10', 'Vectors', 'What is the resultant of forces (3i + 2j) N and (−i + 4j) N?', '2i + 6j N.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.10', 'Vectors', 'Express 10 N at 30° above the x-axis in component form.', '10 cos 30° i + 10 sin 30° j = 5√3 i + 5j N.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.10', 'Vectors', 'How do you show two vectors are parallel?', 'Show one is a scalar multiple of the other.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.10', 'Vectors', 'What is a column vector?', 'A vector written vertically with components, e.g. (3, 4) written as a column.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.10', 'Vectors', 'What does it mean for vectors to be equal?', 'They have the same magnitude and direction (equal components).', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '1.10', 'Vectors', 'Find the angle between the vector 3i + 4j and the positive x-axis.', 'tan θ = 4/3, so θ ≈ 53.1°.', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.1', 'Statistics', 'What is the difference between a population and a sample?', 'A population is the entire group being studied, a sample is a subset used to draw conclusions about it.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.1', 'Statistics', 'What is the difference between a parameter and a statistic?', 'A parameter describes the POPULATION, a statistic is calculated from a SAMPLE and used to estimate it.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.1', 'Statistics', 'Define simple random sampling.', 'Every possible sample of the required size has the same probability of being selected. It requires a complete sampling frame.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.1', 'Statistics', 'What is a sampling frame?', 'A list of all members of the population, from which the sample is drawn. An incomplete frame means some members can never be selected.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.1', 'Statistics', 'Describe systematic sampling and its main risk.', 'Select every kth member of an ordered list. It fails if the list has a repeating pattern matching the sampling interval.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.1', 'Statistics', 'Describe stratified sampling.', 'Divide the population into strata and sample each in proportion to its size, selecting AT RANDOM within each stratum.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.1', 'Statistics', 'Name the four types of data.', 'Categorical (labels), discrete (separate numerical values), continuous (any value in a range) and ranked (ordered positions).', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.1', 'Statistics', 'In a histogram, what represents frequency?', 'The AREA of each bar. The vertical axis is frequency density, which is frequency divided by class width.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.1', 'Statistics', 'How does a frequency chart differ from a histogram?', 'A frequency chart has equal-width bars with FREQUENCY on the vertical axis, a histogram may have unequal widths and uses frequency density.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.1', 'Statistics', 'What does a box-and-whisker diagram show?', 'The median, the lower and upper quartiles, and the extreme values. The box spans the interquartile range.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.1', 'Statistics', 'When is the median preferred to the mean?', 'For skewed data or when outliers are present, since the median depends only on position in the order and resists extremes.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.1', 'Statistics', 'When is a weighted mean appropriate?', 'When combining groups of different sizes, weighting each group mean by the size of that group.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.1', 'Statistics', 'How is probability calculated for equally likely outcomes?', 'Favourable outcomes divided by total outcomes. Every probability lies between 0 and 1 inclusive.', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.1', 'Statistics', 'What is a complementary event?', 'A′ is the event that A does not occur, and P(A′) = 1 − P(A). It is the fastest route for ''at least one'' questions.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.1', 'Statistics', 'What is the expected frequency of an event in n trials?', 'nP(A). In 200 trials with probability 0.15, the expected frequency is 30.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.1', 'Statistics', 'What do A ∩ B and A ∪ B mean?', 'A ∩ B means both occur. A ∪ B means A or B or both occur.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.1', 'Statistics', 'Define mutually exclusive events and give the addition rule.', 'They cannot both occur, so P(A ∩ B) = 0 and P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B).', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.1', 'Statistics', 'Define independent events and give the multiplication rule.', 'The occurrence of one does not affect the probability of the other, so P(A ∩ B) = P(A)P(B).', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.1', 'Statistics', 'What is a discrete random variable?', 'A variable taking separate values each with a given probability. Capital X denotes the variable, lower-case x or r a particular value.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.1', 'Statistics', 'What condition must a discrete probability distribution satisfy?', 'All probabilities non-negative and summing to 1.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.1', 'Statistics', 'What is a discrete uniform distribution?', 'One in which all outcomes are equally likely, such as the score on a fair die.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.1', 'Statistics', 'State the four conditions for a binomial model.', 'A fixed number of trials, exactly two outcomes per trial, the same probability of success each time, and independent trials.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.1', 'Statistics', 'What does X ~ B(n, p) mean?', 'X has the binomial distribution with n trials and probability p of success. The symbol ~ is read ''has the distribution''.', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.1', 'Statistics', 'Write down the binomial probability formula.', 'P(X = r) = ⁿCᵣ pʳ q^(n−r), where q = 1 − p.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.1', 'Statistics', 'What are the null and alternative hypotheses?', 'H₀ is the default assumption being tested, always an equality. H₁ is what is concluded if H₀ is rejected.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.1', 'Statistics', 'Must hypotheses refer to the sample or the population?', 'The POPULATION parameter — p or μ. Writing H₀: x̄ = 50 is wrong, it must be H₀: μ = 50.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.1', 'Statistics', 'What is the significance level?', 'The probability of incorrectly rejecting a TRUE null hypothesis. It sets the size of the critical region.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.1', 'Statistics', 'Define critical region, acceptance region and critical value.', 'The critical (rejection) region contains test statistic values leading to rejection, the acceptance region those that do not, the critical value is the boundary.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.1', 'Statistics', 'What is a p-value and how is it used?', 'The probability of a result at least as extreme as the one observed, assuming H₀. Reject H₀ if it is below the significance level.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.1', 'Statistics', 'When is a test one-tailed and when two-tailed?', 'One-tailed when H₁ specifies a DIRECTION (''biased towards heads''), two-tailed when it does not (''biased'', ''has changed'').', 30),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.2', 'Mechanics', 'What is the difference between distance travelled and displacement?', 'Distance travelled is the total path length, a scalar, displacement is the net change in position, a vector.', 1),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.2', 'Mechanics', 'What is the difference between speed and velocity?', 'Speed is the magnitude of velocity, so it is a scalar and never negative, velocity carries direction.', 2),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.2', 'Mechanics', 'Give the formulae for average speed and average velocity.', 'Average speed = distance travelled ÷ time, average velocity = overall displacement ÷ time. A there-and-back journey has zero average velocity.', 3),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.2', 'Mechanics', 'What do the gradient and area of a velocity-time graph give?', 'Gradient gives acceleration, area gives displacement.', 4),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.2', 'Mechanics', 'What does the gradient of a displacement-time graph give?', 'Velocity.', 5),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.2', 'Mechanics', 'How are velocity and acceleration obtained from a position vector?', 'By differentiating with respect to t, component by component. Integrating reverses the process.', 6),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.2', 'Mechanics', 'Why can vector calculus be done component by component?', 'The i and j directions are fixed and independent of time, so each component is an independent function of t.', 7),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.2', 'Mechanics', 'What is the speed of a particle with velocity v?', '|v|, the magnitude — a scalar. Quoting a speed as a vector loses the mark.', 8),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.2', 'Mechanics', 'If r = 3t i + t² j, what are v and a?', 'v = 3i + 2t j and a = 2j. At t = 2 the speed is |3i + 4j| = 5.', 9),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.2', 'Mechanics', 'State the four standard projectile modelling assumptions.', 'No air resistance, the projectile is a particle, gravity always acts in the same direction, gravity is constant in magnitude.', 10),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.2', 'Mechanics', 'What is the central idea that makes projectile problems work?', 'Horizontal and vertical motion are INDEPENDENT. Horizontally there is no acceleration, vertically the acceleration is g downwards. Time links them.', 11),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.2', 'Mechanics', 'Give the initial velocity components for launch at speed u and angle θ.', 'Horizontal u cos θ, vertical u sin θ.', 12),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.2', 'Mechanics', 'Give the displacement components at time t.', 'Horizontal u cos θ × t, vertical u sin θ × t − ½gt².', 13),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.2', 'Mechanics', 'Write down the time of flight, maximum height and range on level ground.', 'T = 2u sin θ/g. H = u²sin²θ/(2g). R = u² sin 2θ/g.', 14),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.2', 'Mechanics', 'Name four common forces and their directions.', 'Weight acts vertically downwards, normal reaction perpendicular to the surface, tension along a string pulling inwards, friction along the surface opposing motion.', 15),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.2', 'Mechanics', 'What is the difference between tension and thrust?', 'Tension pulls the ends together, thrust pushes them apart. A string can only pull, a rod can do either.', 16),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.2', 'Mechanics', 'Is the normal reaction always equal to the weight?', 'No. It depends on the other forces: pressing down increases it, pulling upwards at an angle reduces it, and on a slope it is mg cos θ.', 17),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.2', 'Mechanics', 'Give the components of weight on a plane inclined at θ.', 'mg sin θ along the plane and mg cos θ perpendicular to it. Check with θ = 0: sin 0 = 0, so the along-plane component vanishes as it should.', 18),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.2', 'Mechanics', 'Why resolve parallel and perpendicular to an inclined plane?', 'It puts the normal reaction on one axis and the friction on the other, so each equation contains fewer unknowns.', 19),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.2', 'Mechanics', 'State Newton''s first law.', 'An object continues at rest or in uniform motion in a straight line unless acted on by a resultant force.', 20),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.2', 'Mechanics', 'State Newton''s second law.', 'A resultant force F on an object of fixed mass m gives acceleration a with F = ma.', 21),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.2', 'Mechanics', 'State Newton''s third law.', 'When one object exerts a force on another, there is a reaction equal in magnitude and opposite in direction.', 22),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.2', 'Mechanics', 'What must the F in F = ma be?', 'The RESULTANT of all forces acting. Using a single force and ignoring friction or weight is the standard structural error.', 23),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.2', 'Mechanics', 'How do you write down an equation of motion?', 'Draw a force diagram, choose a positive direction (usually that of the acceleration), resolve in that direction and set the total equal to ma.', 24),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.2', 'Mechanics', 'How is equilibrium a special case of the second law?', 'Setting a = 0 gives a resultant force of zero — which also recovers the first law.', 25),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.2', 'Mechanics', 'Why does the point of application matter for a rigid body but not a particle?', 'A particle has no size, so forces only translate it. A rigid body has extent, so forces can also produce a turning effect.', 26),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.2', 'Mechanics', 'Define the moment of a force and give its units.', 'Moment = force × perpendicular distance from the point. Units are newton metres, N m.', 27),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.2', 'Mechanics', 'What is the moment of a force acting through the pivot?', 'Zero, since the perpendicular distance is zero. This is why choosing the pivot cleverly removes unknowns.', 28),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.2', 'Mechanics', 'State the two conditions for a rigid body to be in equilibrium.', 'The resultant force is zero, AND the sum of the moments about any one point is zero.', 29),
  ('Mathematics', 'OCR', 'alevel', '2.2', 'Mechanics', 'Why is the force condition alone insufficient?', 'Two equal and opposite forces applied at different points give no net force but do produce a turning effect.', 30)
),
fresh AS (
  SELECT v.* FROM v
  WHERE NOT EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM flashcards f
    WHERE f.subject = v.subject AND f.exam_board = v.exam_board AND f.level = v.level
      AND f.topic_id = v.topic_id AND f.front = v.front)
),
cur AS (
  SELECT subject, exam_board, level, topic_id, count(*) AS n, max(card_order) AS mx
  FROM flashcards
  WHERE subject = 'Mathematics' AND exam_board = 'OCR' AND level = 'alevel'
  GROUP BY 1, 2, 3, 4
),
ranked AS (
  SELECT fr.*, coalesce(c.n, 0) AS n, coalesce(c.mx, 0) AS mx,
         row_number() OVER (PARTITION BY fr.topic_id ORDER BY fr.ord) AS rn
  FROM fresh fr
  LEFT JOIN cur c USING (subject, exam_board, level, topic_id)
)
INSERT INTO flashcards (subject, exam_board, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, card_order, level)
SELECT subject, exam_board, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, mx + rn, level
FROM ranked
WHERE n + rn <= 30;

-- Flashcard top-up: Mathematics|OCR B|alevel — brings each topic up to 30 cards.
-- Generated by scripts/build-flashcard-topup.js from flashcards/topup/src/maths-ocr-b-alevel.txt
